    ('0294d74b-0e97-4509-913a-475e3c21e1a7'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:0294d74b-0e97-4509-913a-475e3c21e1a7:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''5'' restored on Punic Building, Żurrieq', 'fb32146f0e5157a56ffc8431a6d6b0f2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic structures'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 24 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32987", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0011:chunk-0001: description ''The Punic building in Zurrieq is the most substantial surviving example of Phoenician-Punic architecture in Malta, featuring a 5.5 m-high tower of ashlar limestone blocks laid without mortar, crowned by an Egyptian-style cavetto cornice. Possibly dating to the late 6th century BC, its purpose remains debated -- theories include a monumental tomb, or the temple of Melqart described by Ptolemy. A 1964 excavation revealed stepped foundations and pottery spanning Punic to modern periods. Scheduled as a Class A archaeological site in 1998, it sits within the private garden of the Domus Curialis.'' -> \"The remains of an unidentified Punic building exist incorporated into several properties in Żurrieq, Malta [1]. They include a well-preserved structure commonly known as the Punic Tower or the Żurrieq Tower which is found inside the private garden of the Domus Curialis, the house of the town''s archpriest [1]. The site consists of a well-preserved 5.6 m-high tower with a square plan topped by a cavetto cornice showing inspiration from ancient Egyptian architecture, along with some adjacent walls which are believed to have originally formed part of a larger building [1]. Both the tower and the walls are constructed out of ashlar limestone masonry without mortar, with each block having dimensions of up to 66 cm × 180 cm [1]. The building''s age and purpose are not known, but it might date back to around the late 6th century BC [1]. The building is not believed to have been a defensive tower [1]. The building''s existence was first recorded by Bishop Miguel Jerónimo de Molina in 1680 [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
