    ('0371ed0a-82f5-4578-b7bf-10dc40faf580'::uuid, 'legacy', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:0371ed0a-82f5-4578-b7bf-10dc40faf580:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''1'' restored on Baalbek', '5b4597847d9d80efec406a19c73318b8', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 4 from period -7000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43719", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0081:chunk-0001: description \"Baalbek is an ancient city in Lebanon''s Beqaa Valley, inhabited since at least 9000 BCE [1]. The site features one of the largest Roman temple complexes in the world, including the monumental Temple of Jupiter and the well-preserved Temple of Bacchus [2].\" -> ''Baalbek was called Heliopolis during the Roman Empire, a latinisation of the Greek Hēlioúpolis used during the Hellenistic period, meaning \"Sun City\" in reference to the solar cult there [1]. After Alexander the Great\\''s conquest of Persia in the 330s BC, Baalbek formed part of the Diadochi kingdoms of Egypt & Syria [1]. \"Baalbek, with its colossal structures, is one of the finest examples of Imperial Roman architecture at its apogee\", UNESCO reported in making Baalbek a World Heritage Site in 1984 [1]. Recent cleaning operations at the Temple of Jupiter discovered the deep trench at its edge, whose study pushed back the date of Tell Baalbek\\''s settlement to the PPNB Neolithic [1]. The Tell Baalbek temple complex, fortified as the town\\''s citadel during the Middle Ages, was constructed from local stone, mostly white granite and a rough white marble [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
