    ('03b9c5be-ec62-4fe7-8231-c098a4d2b921'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:03b9c5be-ec62-4fe7-8231-c098a4d2b921:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Temple of Concordia, Agrigento', '711c63afb0645b24d5f514962867ff22', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27930", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0032:chunk-0001: site_type ''Temple complex'' -> ''Temple''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32991", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0011:chunk-0001: description ''An exceptionally well-preserved 5th-century BC Doric temple in the Valley of the Temples at Agrigento, Sicily, built around 440 BC during the Greek Classical period. The peripteral temple spans 39 metres in length and 16.9 metres in width, with 34 columns standing approximately 7 metres high in a 6-by-13 arrangement. Its remarkable preservation owes largely to its conversion into a Christian basilica dedicated to Saints Peter and Paul in the 6th century AD. Part of the UNESCO World Heritage-listed Valle dei Templi since 1997, it is the largest and best-preserved Doric temple in Sicily.'' -> ''The Temple of Concordia is an ancient Greek temple of Magna Graecia in the Valle dei Templi (Valley of the Temples) in Agrigento on the south coast of Sicily, Italy [1]. It is the largest and best-preserved Doric temple in Sicily and one of the best-preserved Greek temples in general, especially of the Doric order [1]. It is located a kilometer east to the Temple of Heracles [1]. The temple was built c.\\u2009440–430 BC [1]. The columns are 6 m high and carved with twenty flutes and harmonious entasis (tapering at the tops of the columns and swelling around the middles) [1]. The temple was converted into a Christian basilica in the 6th century dedicated to the apostles Peter and Paul by San Gregorio delle Rape, bishop of Agrigento and thus survived the destruction of pagan places of worship [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
