unich\\''s connection with the polity Naranjo solidified [1]. The core of the city Xunantunich occupies about one square mile, consisting of a series of six plazas surrounded by more than 26 temples and palaces [1]. One of Xunantunich\\''s better known structures is the pyramid known as \"El Castillo\" (not to be confused with the El Castillo at Chichen Itza) [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('03d63367-fb7a-4014-b1db-a704e99a10f3'::uuid, 'total_power', '27', '28', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:03d63367-fb7a-4014-b1db-a704e99a10f3:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: total_power ''28'' restored on Xunantunich', 'c88d26085c091202d07743bf3339dff6', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 27 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 3 + 7 + 10 + 6 + 1"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32993", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0011:chunk-0001: description \"Xunantunich (''Stone Woman'' in Yucatec Maya) is a Classic Maya ceremonial center in western Belize that rose to prominence between 700-1000 AD. Its centerpiece, El Castillo, stands 40 meters tall -- the second tallest structure in Belize -- with large stucco friezes depicting astronomical symbols, the sun god, moon, and Venus. The site comprises six plazas surrounded by over 26 temples and palaces. In 2016, archaeologists discovered the largest Maya royal tomb found at the site, containing a young man buried with jade beads, obsidian blades, and hieroglyphic panels linked to the powerful Snake Dynasty and Lord Kan II''s 642 AD conquest of Naranjo.\" -> ''Xunantunich is an ancient Maya archaeological site in western Belize, about 70 miles west of Belize City, in the Cayo District [1]. Xunantunich is located atop a ridge above the Mopan River [1]. Xunantunich\\''s name means \"Maiden of the Rock\" in the Maya languages Mopan and Yucatec [1]. Like many names given to Maya archaeological sites, \"Xunantunich\" is a modern name; the ancient name is unknown [1]. Architectural constructions boomed in Hats’ Chaak phase (AD 670–750) when Xunantunich\\''s connection with the polity Naranjo solidified [1]. The core of the city Xunantunich occupies about one square mile, consisting of a series of six plazas surrounded by more than 26 temples and palaces [1]. One of Xunantunich\\''s better known structures is the pyramid known as \"El Castillo\" (not to be confused with the El Castillo at Chichen Itza) [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
