    ('0490ef6d-5381-449d-aa8c-385ab7dbd8e7'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:0490ef6d-5381-449d-aa8c-385ab7dbd8e7:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Xagħra Stone Circle', 'ca35f79033089a19f1618462afdb095f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Necropolis/tombs complex'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 47 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28476", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0141:chunk-0001: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-4100''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32997", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0011:chunk-0001: description ''Xagħra Stone Circle (4100–2400 BC) is a Neolithic funerary complex in Xagħra, Gozo, Malta, comprising caves used for burial surrounded by a walled enclosure [1]. The main phase dates to c. 3000–2400 BC, with the earliest tombs from 4100–3800 BC [1]. The caves collapsed before 2000 BC and the site later served domestic and agricultural functions [1].'' -> ''The Xagħra Stone Circle, also known as the Xagħra Hypogeum or the Brochtorff Circle, is a Neolithic funerary complex located in Xagħra, Gozo, Malta [1]. It consists of a series of caves which were used to bury the dead [1]. It mainly dates back to around 3000 to 2400 BC, although the earliest tombs at the site date back to 4100 to 3800 BC [1]. The caves collapsed sometime before 2000 BC, and the site was later used for domestic and agricultural purposes [1]. The Xagħra Stone Circle originally consisted of a walled enclosure surrounding caves which were used as a necropolis [1]. Excavations at the site have shown that the bodies of the deceased were dismembered, and the different body parts were buried at separate places [1]. The site was the burial ground of the same community which built the nearby Ġgantija temple [1]. The Xagħra Stone Circle was first recorded by Jean Houel in 1788 [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35639", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-4100'' -> ''-4500''"}]'::jsonb),
