    ('0849cdd5-5b9a-40c2-9aa9-f58c1ac32b22'::uuid, 'antiquity', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:0849cdd5-5b9a-40c2-9aa9-f58c1ac32b22:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: antiquity ''7'' restored on Kavousi Kastro', '650550579f902c8a7a17bde578d4beb4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 6 from period_start -1200"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28258", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0101:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-1200''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30338", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33027", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0012:chunk-0001: description \"Early Bronze Age to Middle Bronze Age settlement on Crete''s eastern coast, Kavousi Kastro occupies a fortified hilltop 1.4 km southeast of modern Kavousi village at 713 m elevation, overlooking the Gulf of Mirabello in the Thripti Mountains [1]. The site controlled trade routes and agricultural lands in the Mirabello region from approximately 3000 BC until around 1500 BC [1].\" -> ''Kavousi Kastro is an archaeological site in eastern Crete, Greece, about 1.4 km southeast of the modern village of Kavousi [1]. The Kastro is best known as a defensible “refuge” settlement of the “Greek Dark Ages”, inhabited from the early 12th to the mid-7th centuries BCE [1]. Most of the visible architectural remains on the site belong to the Late Geometric–Early Orientalizing phases of occupation (8th to 7th centuries BCE) [1]. Investigations by the Kavousi Project have demonstrated that a settlement on the Kastro was occupied continuously from its foundation early in the Late Minoan IIIC period (12th century BCE), through the Protogeometric (10th century BCE), Geometric (9th-8th centuries BCE) and Orientalizing (7th century BCE) periods, after which the site was abandoned [1]. The site was briefly explored in 1900 by the American archaeologist Harriet Boyd [Hawes], who discovered 13 rooms of what she thought was the house of “an early chieftain”, but now interpreted as multiple, separate house complexes spread across several terraces [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
