    ('0c4851de-cccd-41fc-8cb1-223a83b2e67f'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Monuments', 'Megalithic', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:0c4851de-cccd-41fc-8cb1-223a83b2e67f:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Megalithic'' restored on Arena of Nîmes', 'ad6bcb408ceecbb026aabac83308902d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Monuments from site_type ''Amphitheatre'' is in group ''Monuments''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28976", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0220:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Amphitheatre''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43911", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0082:chunk-0001: description ''The Arena of Nîmes is a Roman amphitheatre in Nîmes, southern France, built around 100 AD, shortly after the Colosseum in Rome[1]. It is one of the best-preserved Roman amphitheatres in the world, measuring 133 meters long and 101 meters wide, with an arena of 68 by 38 meters[1]. The facade reaches 21 meters in height with two storeys of 60 arcades[1]. In Roman times, the building could hold 24,000 spectators, spread over 34 tiers of terraces divided into four self-contained zones or maeniana[1]. It is among the 20 largest Roman amphitheatres of the 400 that existed in the empire[1].'' -> ''The Arena of Nîmes is a Roman amphitheatre in Nîmes, Southern France [1]. Built around 100 AD, only about twenty years after the Colosseum of Rome, it is one of the best-preserved Roman amphitheatres in the world [1]. It is 133 metres long and 101 metres wide, with an arena measuring 68 by 38 metres [1]. The outer facade is 21 metres high with two storeys of 60 arcades [1]. In Roman times, the building could hold 24,000 spectators, who were spread over 34 tiers of terraces divided into four self-contained zones or maeniana [1]. During Roman times, the Arena of Nîmes functioned as an arena where gladiators battled each other and wild animals [1]. The advent of early medieval Christianity marked the end of these events, prompting the transformation of the amphitheater into a fortress and subsequently a walled town [1]. The 19th century saw the restoration of the arena, accompanied by the removal of houses that had been constructed inside it [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
