 metres high with two storeys of 60 arcades [1]. In Roman times, the building could hold 24,000 spectators, who were spread over 34 tiers of terraces divided into four self-contained zones or maeniana [1]. During Roman times, the Arena of Nîmes functioned as an arena where gladiators battled each other and wild animals [1]. The advent of early medieval Christianity marked the end of these events, prompting the transformation of the amphitheater into a fortress and subsequently a walled town [1]. The 19th century saw the restoration of the arena, accompanied by the removal of houses that had been constructed inside it [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0da526f9-499d-4d15-a0ea-a45093fc4970'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:0da526f9-499d-4d15-a0ea-a45093fc4970:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on London Wall', 'd4983c036720aa5e9df00125bc01e253', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Wall'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43915", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0082:chunk-0001: description \"The London Wall was a Roman defensive wall first constructed around Londinium around AD 200, following the destructive Boudican revolt of 60–61 AD. [1] The wall enclosed approximately 330 acres of the city, stretching about 2 miles in circumference and reaching heights of 20 feet, with six main gates controlling entry points. [1] An earlier fort at Cripplegate, established around 120–150 AD, was later incorporated into the defensive network. [1] Today, the wall''s route is marked by the modern street name London Wall in the City of London. [1]\" -> ''The London Wall is a defensive wall first built by the Romans around the strategically important port town of Londinium in c. AD 200, as well as the name of a modern street in the City of London, England [1]. The original gates, clockwise from Ludgate in the west to Aldgate in the east, were: Ludgate, Newgate, Cripplegate, Bishopsgate and Aldgate [1]. Aldersgate, between Newgate and Cripplegate, was added around 350 [1]. The length and size of the wall made it one of the biggest construction projects in Roman Britain [1]. It had gateways, towers and defensive ditches, and was built from Kentish ragstone, which was brought by barge from quarries near Maidstone [1]. The city walls of London were repaired as the city slowly grew until about 950 when urban activity increased dramatically [1]. During the early medieval period – following the Norman Conquest of England – the walls underwent substantial work that included crenellations, additional gates and further towers and bastions [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0da526f9-499d-4d15-a0ea-a45093fc4970'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '40', '43', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:0da526f9-499d-4d15-a0ea-a45093fc4970:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: rarity_score ''43'' restored on London Wall', 'd4983c036720aa5e9df00125bc01e253', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 40 from mystery 6, cultural_influence 10"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43915", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0082:chunk-0001: description \"The London Wall was a Roman defensive wall first constructed around Londinium around AD 200, following the destructive Boudican revolt of 60–61 AD. [1] The wall enclosed approximately 330 acres of the city, stretching about 2 miles in circumference and reaching heights of 20 feet, with six main gates controlling entry points. [1] An earlier fort at Cripplegate, established around 120–150 AD, was later incorporated into the defensive network. [1] Today, the wall''s route is marked by the modern street name London Wall in the City of London. [1]\" -> ''The London Wall is a defensive wall first built by the Romans around the strategically important port town of Londinium in c. AD 200, as well as the name of a modern street in the City of London, England [1]. The original gates, clockwise from Ludgate in the west to Aldgate in the east, were: Ludgate, Newgate, Cripplegate, Bishopsgate and Aldgate [1]. Aldersgate, between Newgate and Cripplegate, was added around 350 [1]. The length and size of the wall made it one of the biggest construction projects in Roman Britain [1]. It had gateways, towers and defensive ditches, and was built from Kentish ragstone, which was brought by barge from quarries near Maidstone [1]. The city walls of London were repaired as the city slowly grew until about 950 when urban activity increased dramatically [1]. During the early medieval period – following the Norman Conquest of England – the walls underwent substantial work that included crenellations, additional gates and further towers and bastions [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
