    ('15511706-1e3e-4b7f-974d-7464988b0462'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:15511706-1e3e-4b7f-974d-7464988b0462:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Temple of Ares', '4a8a444b8c5325d9c13280632a9eedfd', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Temple'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 6 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28902", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0212:chunk-0001: site_type ''Temple complex'' -> ''Temple''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33139", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0015:chunk-0001: description \"(4th c. BC) The Temple of Ares was a Doric hexastyle peripteral temple dedicated to the god of war, located in the northern part of the Ancient Agora of Athens [1]. Archaeological excavations uncovered fragments throughout the Agora, enabling a full, if tentative, reconstruction of the temple''s appearance and sculptural programme [1]. The temple featured a large altar to the east and was surrounded by statues [1]. A terrace to the north looked down on the Panathenaic Way [1]. Notably, the northwest corner of the temple overlays one of the best-preserved Mycenaean tombs in the Agora, which was in use from ca. 1450-1000 BC [1].\" -> ''The Temple of Ares was a Doric hexastyle peripteral temple dedicated to Ares, located in the northern part of the Ancient Agora of Athens [1]. The temple had a large altar to the east and was surrounded by statues [1]. The temple was originally located at Pallene, where it was dedicated to Athena Pallenis and - probably - Apollo [1]. The temple was transferred to the Agora and rededicated to Ares during the reign of Augustus [1]. Around 230 fragments of the superstructure have been found reused in later structures throughout the Agora [1]. They are now collected at the western end of the temple [1]. They are made of a distinctive crystalline Pentelic marble with gray-green chlorite veins [1]. They can be dated stylistically to the second half of the 5th century BC, probably the 430s BC [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
