 of Tambomachay and Puka Pukara, near Qenko [1]. This site named Amaru Marka Wasi was possibly the former residence of Amaru Yupanqui, also known as Amaro Tupac, the eldest son of Pachakutiq Inka Yupanki [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('1d4dd1f5-e841-4c8c-9761-2d4ad3eec155'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:1d4dd1f5-e841-4c8c-9761-2d4ad3eec155:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on Kinniside Stone Circle', 'd54d2ecc8e630f748f7233e0fc0ff00b', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Stone circle'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 12 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33219", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0017:chunk-0001: description ''Kinniside Stone Circle (also called Blakeley Raise) is a reconstructed Bronze Age stone circle in the civil parish of Ennerdale and Kinniside, Cumbria, situated alongside the fell road from Ennerdale Bridge to Calder Bridge. It consists of 11 local granite stones forming a circle 18 m in diameter, with the tallest stone reaching 1.15-1.5 m. A possible cairn 5 m across occupies the centre. The original circle was dismantled in the 18th century by a farmer who reused the stones as gateposts; Dr. Quine of Frizington re-erected it in 1925 with the stones set in concrete. The accuracy of the reconstruction to the original layout remains uncertain.'' -> ''Kinniside Stone Circle is a reconstructed stone circle in the civil parish of Ennerdale and Kinniside in Cumbria [1]. Kinniside Stone Circle is situated next to the fell road from Ennerdale Bridge to Calder Bridge in Cumbria [1]. It consists of 11 small stones of local granite with a possible cairn at its centre [1]. The circle was apparently dismantled in the 18th century by a farmer who used the stones for gate-posts [1]. In 1925 a Doctor Quine of Frizington \"restored\" the stone circle, setting the stones in concrete [1]. Whether or not he erected the stones in their original stone holes is unclear, and it is unlikely that all of the re-erected stones come from the original circle [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
