    ('1db1cb71-0431-46ec-ab93-39338844dbab'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:1db1cb71-0431-46ec-ab93-39338844dbab:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''6'' restored on Dun Carloway', 'c3f6d95fc663c2f8a6331161075eafc4', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Minaret/tower'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 9 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29348", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0276:chunk-0001: site_type ''Minaret/tower'' -> ''Fortification''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33225", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0017:chunk-0001: description \"Dun Carloway is one of Scotland''s best-preserved broch towers, standing on a rocky outcrop on the west coast of the Isle of Lewis. Probably built around 200 BC, it served as a residence and status symbol for a local chief. The double-skinned walls still reach 9 metres on the east side, with an external diameter of 14.3 metres and internal courtyard of 7.4 metres. Architectural features include two interior gallery tiers, a ground-level guard cell, an internal stairway, and a stone scarcement ledge that supported upper floors. Remarkably, the broch saw intermittent use for nearly two millennia -- excavations found peat-ovens from 400-700 AD, and a family reportedly lived within its walls as late as the 1870s. In state management since 1887, it is now cared for by Historic Environment Scotland.\" -> ''Dun Carloway is a broch situated in the district of Carloway, on the west coast of the Isle of Lewis [1]. Dun Carloway was probably built in the 1st century AD [1]. Through the centuries Dun Carloway remained in use until the floor level was too high due to build-up of the occupation layers [1]. The broch was occasionally used in later times as a stronghold [1]. Dun Carloway is built on a rock on a steep south slope at the height of 50 metres [1]. It is the best preserved broch in the Outer Hebrides [1]. The wall of the broch rises on the south side to 9.2 metres [1]. Only Mousa Broch and Dun Telve have walls that are higher [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35684", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: site_type ''Fortification'' -> ''Minaret/tower''"}]'::jsonb),
