he centuries Dun Carloway remained in use until the floor level was too high due to build-up of the occupation layers [1]. The broch was occasionally used in later times as a stronghold [1]. Dun Carloway is built on a rock on a steep south slope at the height of 50 metres [1]. It is the best preserved broch in the Outer Hebrides [1]. The wall of the broch rises on the south side to 9.2 metres [1]. Only Mousa Broch and Dun Telve have walls that are higher [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35684", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: site_type ''Fortification'' -> ''Minaret/tower''"}]'::jsonb),
