    ('1dd3934e-3083-4c17-b1b6-219e4ae18f15'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Monuments', 'Megalithic', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:1dd3934e-3083-4c17-b1b6-219e4ae18f15:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Megalithic'' restored on Amphitheatre of Mérida', 'a24e4d9fab376128c1957ab52b3d3ac2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Monuments from site_type ''Amphitheatre'' is in group ''Monuments''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28052", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0059:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Amphitheatre''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43979", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0084:chunk-0001: description ''A Roman amphitheatre completed in 8 BC in the colony of Emerita Augusta, capital of the province of Lusitania in modern Merida, Spain. The structure held approximately 15,000 spectators and was used for gladiatorial combats and beast hunts. Its design features three sections of seating: the ima, media, and summa cavea surrounding a central arena. Now part of the Archaeological Ensemble of Merida, declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1993, it stands as one of the most extensive Roman archaeological complexes on the Iberian Peninsula.'' -> ''The Amphitheatre of Mérida is a Roman amphitheatre in the Roman colonia of Emerita Augusta –present-day Mérida, Spain–, capital of the Roman province of Lusitania [1]. It was completed in the year 8 BC, and is currently in ruins [1]. It was used for gladiatorial fights and combats between beasts or men and beasts during ancient Rome [1]. The amphitheatre was built as part of an entertainment complex together with the Roman Theatre [1]. The sand-covered arena in the centre had a fossa bestiaria in the center, which was covered with wood and sand [1]. This fossa was used to house animals before they were released into the arena [1]. The stands had a capacity of approximately 15,000 spectators and had supporting stairs and hallways that connected the different parts internally [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
