    ('21660e33-c3b7-462f-aaec-cbaded7b4a8d'::uuid, 'civilization', 'Georgia', 'Georgia (country)', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:21660e33-c3b7-462f-aaec-cbaded7b4a8d:civilization', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: civilization ''Georgia (country)'' restored on Armazi', 'c374783a1a45de8c315e3b3d08072af6', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "civilization = Georgia from country ''Georgia''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27249", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-21_mechanical-country: country ''Georgia (country)'' -> ''Georgia''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44013", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0085:chunk-0001: description ''Ancient capital of the Georgian kingdom of Iberia (Kartli), founded in the 3rd century BC by King Pharnavaz I near modern Mtskheta. Three cultural layers span the 4th century BC to 6th century AD. Excavations from 1943 under Andria Apakidze uncovered 30 hectares of fortified ruins, a 2nd-3rd century bathhouse, and noble burial grounds. The famous Armazi Bilingual stele (c. 150 AD), inscribed in Greek and the extinct Armazic script, was discovered in 1940. The city was destroyed by Arab invasion in the 730s.'' -> \"Armazi is a locale in Georgia, 4 km southwest of Mtskheta and 22 km northwest of Tbilisi [1]. A part of historical Greater Mtskheta, it is a place where the ancient city of the same name and the original capital of the early Georgian kingdom of Kartli or Iberia was located [1]. It particularly flourished in the early centuries AD and was destroyed by the Arab invasion in the 730s [1]. From 1943 to 1948 large-scale excavation was undertaken under Andria Apakidze of the Georgian Academy of Sciences, resumed in 1985 and continuing [1]. The three major cultural layers have been identified: the earliest dates back to the 4th-3rd century BC (Armazi I), the middle one is from the 3rd-1st century BC (Armazi II), and the relatively newer structure belongs to the 1st-6th century AD (Armazi III) [1]. Archaeological evidences testify that the ancient Armazi was far more extensive than it is today [1]. Armazi''s strategic situation was dictated by its ready access to the Daryal Pass, the main road over the Greater Caucasus [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
