    ('21d63980-32dc-43cc-baa1-06c9e2f15335'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:21d63980-32dc-43cc-baa1-06c9e2f15335:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Silbury Hill', 'd467e0ac4596a94e1322df064166f779', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -3000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33421", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0018:chunk-0001: description ''Silbury Hill is a prehistoric artificial chalk mound near Avebury in Wiltshire. At 39.3 metres (129 ft) high, it is the second tallest prehistoric man-made mound in Europe and similar in volume to contemporary Egyptian pyramids[1]. Part of the Stonehenge, Avebury and Associated Sites UNESCO World Heritage Site, the site is in the care of English Heritage[1].'' -> ''Silbury Hill is a prehistoric artificial chalk mound near Avebury in the English county of Wiltshire [1]. At 39.3 metres high, the hill is the tallest and second largest prehistoric man-made mound in Europe and one of the largest in the world; it is similar in volume to contemporary Egyptian pyramids [1]. Silbury Hill is part of the complex of Neolithic monuments around Avebury, which includes the Avebury Ring and West Kennet Long Barrow [1]. The mound is composed mainly of chalk and clay excavated from the surrounding area and stands 39.3 metres high and covers about 2 hectares [1]. The hill was constructed in several stages between c.\\u20092400 and 2300 BC and displays immense technical skill and prolonged control over labour and resources [1]. Few prehistoric artifacts have been found on Silbury Hill: at its core there is only clay, flints, turf, moss, topsoil, gravel, freshwater shells, mistletoe, oak, hazel, sarsen stones, ox bones, and antler tines [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
