cription about the works of Augustus [1]. It is the most complete copy of Res Gestae Divi Augusti that has survived to the present day [1]. An earlier, 2nd century BCE Phrygian temple on the site was destroyed [1]. Only the side walls and the ornamented door frame remain; the positions of six columns can still be recognized [1]. After the death of Augustus in AD 14, a copy of the text of the Res Gestae Divi Augusti was inscribed on both walls inside the pronaos in Latin with a Greek translation on an exterior wall of the cella [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
