 at sites up to 120 km apart, indicating north-south mobility along the Mediterranean coast of central Iberia [4].'' -> ''The Abrigo de la Quebrada is an archaeological site in the municipality of Chelva, in the Spanish Province of Valencia, ca. 65 km northwest of the city of Valencia [1]. It shows evidence of frequent occupation by Neanderthals [1]. The site is on the left bank of a small ravine, the Barranco de Ahillas; it is a rock shelter between 2 and 9 meters deep, and 38 meters long, facing the northwest [1]. Because of the lack of direct sunshine and its exposure, the site was occupied only from June to October, according to Phytoliths and other evidence, including the reuse and resharpening of stone tools [1]. The deposit is around 3 meters thick, and is divided into eight units by archaeologists [1]. Flint tools found at the site give evidence of extensive traffic of Neanderthal populations in the area [1]. While much of the flint was procured locally, some of it came from over 100 kilometers away; knapping was done also by way of the Levallois technique [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('277be4f6-02bc-4dd4-b1a2-1898e529faf9'::uuid, 'mystery', '8', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:277be4f6-02bc-4dd4-b1a2-1898e529faf9:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on Gournia', 'd8fb60b3c24a0c4b66cf9d0fb3bfb7a6', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 8 from (''Castle/palace'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 4 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33459", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0019:chunk-0001: description ''Gournia is a Minoan town and palace complex in Lasithi, eastern Crete, often called the \"Pompeii of Crete\" for its exceptional preservation. It flourished from roughly 1550-1450 BC, was destroyed by an earthquake around 1450 BC, and finally abandoned about 1200 BC. First excavated by American archaeologist Harriet Boyd-Hawes (1901-1904), the site revealed about 50 houses, a cobbled street system, a central court, a small hilltop palace, and a snake goddess temple. Gournia served as a regional trade hub with overseas connections to the Aegean and Near East, and as the palatial administrative center of the Mirabello region.'' -> ''Gournia is the site of a Minoan palace complex in the Lasithi regional unit on the island of Crete, Greece [1]. Its modern name originated from the many stone troughs that are at the site and its original name for the site is unknown [1]. Harriet Boyd Hawes first excavated the Minoan village for three field seasons in 1901, 1903 and 1904 [1]. Boyd and her team were able to expose nearly the entire town, uncovering sixty houses, the cemetery, a road system connecting everything and a central building which she called \"the palace\" [1]. The town itself is centered around the palatial complex which is located on the central-West side of the town [1]. During the Middle Minoan IB period (1925–1875 B.C.E.) substantial construction began which was later destroyed in the Middle Minoan II period (1875–1700 B.C.E.) [1]. Rebuilding occurred in the Middle Minoan III period, which included the palace complex [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('277be4f6-02bc-4dd4-b1a2-1898e529faf9'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '38', '35', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:277be4f6-02bc-4dd4-b1a2-1898e529faf9:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: rarity_score ''35'' restored on Gournia', 'd8fb60b3c24a0c4b66cf9d0fb3bfb7a6', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 38 from mystery 8, cultural_influence 7"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33459", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0019:chunk-0001: description ''Gournia is a Minoan town and palace complex in Lasithi, eastern Crete, often called the \"Pompeii of Crete\" for its exceptional preservation. It flourished from roughly 1550-1450 BC, was destroyed by an earthquake around 1450 BC, and finally abandoned about 1200 BC. First excavated by American archaeologist Harriet Boyd-Hawes (1901-1904), the site revealed about 50 houses, a cobbled street system, a central court, a small hilltop palace, and a snake goddess temple. Gournia served as a regional trade hub with overseas connections to the Aegean and Near East, and as the palatial administrative center of the Mirabello region.'' -> ''Gournia is the site of a Minoan palace complex in the Lasithi regional unit on the island of Crete, Greece [1]. Its modern name originated from the many stone troughs that are at the site and its original name for the site is unknown [1]. Harriet Boyd Hawes first excavated the Minoan village for three field seasons in 1901, 1903 and 1904 [1]. Boyd and her team were able to expose nearly the entire town, uncovering sixty houses, the cemetery, a road system connecting everything and a central building which she called \"the palace\" [1]. The town itself is centered around the palatial complex which is located on the central-West side of the town [1]. During the Middle Minoan IB period (1925–1875 B.C.E.) substantial construction began which was later destroyed in the Middle Minoan II period (1875–1700 B.C.E.) [1]. Rebuilding occurred in the Middle Minoan III period, which included the palace complex [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
