 or Wiracochapampa (possibly from Quechua wiraqucha: mister, sir, gentleman, god; or Wiracocha: one of the greatest Andean divinities; and pampa: plain) is an archaeological site with the remains of a building complex of ancient Peru of pre-Inca times [1]. It was one of the administrative centers of the Wari culture [1]. Viracochapampa is located about 3.5 km north of Huamachuco in the region of La Libertad at an elevation of 3,070 metres [1]. The site was occupied from the late Middle Horizon 1B time to the first decades of period 2A, according to the chronology established by Dorothy Menzel [1]. These correspond to the 7th and 8th centuries of our era [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('28d08d27-d959-42d0-922c-8e91c795d432'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '5', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:28d08d27-d959-42d0-922c-8e91c795d432:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''5'' restored on Griffin Warrior Tomb', 'c9e055597bb9f9fd9dfff2fbd9d9fa19', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Tomb'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 6 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28412", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0128:chunk-0001: site_type ''Necropolis/tombs complex'' -> ''Tomb''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33467", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0019:chunk-0001: description ''The Griffin Warrior Tomb is a Bronze Age shaft grave dating to c. 1450 BC, discovered in 2015 by University of Cincinnati archaeologists Jack Davis and Sharon Stocker near the Palace of Nestor in Pylos, Greece. The undisturbed burial yielded over 2,000 artifacts including gold jewelry, bronze weapons, ivory plaques, and approximately 50 decorated seal stones. Among them, the Pylos Combat Agate -- a 1.4-inch sealstone depicting warriors in extraordinary detail -- is considered one of the finest works of prehistoric Greek art. The predominantly Minoan-style grave goods suggest deep cultural exchange between Minoan and Mycenaean civilizations rather than sharp division.'' -> ''The Griffin Warrior Tomb is a Bronze Age shaft tomb dating to around 1450 BC, near the ancient city of Pylos in Greece [1]. The grave was discovered by a research team sponsored by the University of Cincinnati and led by husband-and-wife archaeologists Jack L. Davis and Sharon Stocker [1]. The tomb site was excavated from May to October 2015 [1]. During the initial six month excavation, the research team uncovered an intact adult male skeleton and excavated 1400 objects including weapons, jewels, armour and silver and gold artifacts [1]. Since 2015, the number of artifacts recovered from the grave has reached over 3500 items, including a historically significant Minoan sealstone called the Pylos Combat Agate and four signet gold rings with detailed images from Minoan mythology [1]. The gravesite was discovered in an olive grove near the ancient Palace of Nestor, within the Bronze Age city of Pylos, in southwest Greece [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
