    ('29440d54-9b26-4f92-9f0c-b7573373d12c'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:29440d54-9b26-4f92-9f0c-b7573373d12c:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Cave of La Pasiega', '2c35eca2eeec0d98121b0bbfe260ce0f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -64800..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33469", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0019:chunk-0001: description ''The Cave of La Pasiega in Puente Viesgo, Cantabria, was discovered in 1911 by Hugo Obermaier and Paul Wernert. Spanning approximately 400 metres with four main galleries, it contains more than 700 images including paintings, engravings, and petroglyphs. About 40% depict animals such as deer, horses, ibex, and bison, while the rest are abstract symbols including tectiforms and a notable ladder-shaped sign dated to around 64,000 years ago, attributed to Neanderthals. A UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2008 as part of the Cave of Altamira and Paleolithic Cave Art of Northern Spain, the cave remains permanently closed to preserve its art.'' -> \"Cueva de La Pasiega, or Cave of La Pasiega, situated in the Spanish municipality of Puente Viesgo, is one of the most important monuments of Paleolithic art in Cantabria [1]. The recorded remains belong mainly to the Upper Solutrean and the Lower Magdalenian ages, although older objects are also found [1]. Throughout the cave are many ''walls'' with paintings and with engraved or incised images [1]. There are representations of equines (horses), cervids (deer, male and female) and bovines (cattle) [1]. There are also many abstract symbols (idiomorphs), suggesting patterns of repeated characters [1]. The scientific discovery of the La Pasiega can be credited to Werner and Hugo Obermaier [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
