    ('2b2d0e62-5cbc-4427-be06-542064fb5076'::uuid, 'antiquity', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:2b2d0e62-5cbc-4427-be06-542064fb5076:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: antiquity ''7'' restored on Poor Lot Barrow Cemetery', '48cd884558f7d25b10cc0b43f3f4d2ac', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 6 from period_start -1500"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30911", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:gap-0008:chunk-0004: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33505", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0020:chunk-0001: description ''A Bronze Age barrow cemetery dating to approximately 1500 BC near the A35 road west of Winterbourne Abbas in Dorset, England. The site contains 44 round barrows including bowl, bell, disc, and pond types -- the largest measuring 35 metres in diameter and 2.5 metres high. Unusually situated on low-lying ground rather than a hilltop, the cemetery lies in a region with the highest density of Bronze Age barrows in the British Isles. Two pond barrows excavated in 1952-53 revealed pits and flint paving but no burials. Managed by English Heritage.'' -> ''Poor Lot Barrow Cemetery is an archaeological site, a group of Bronze Age round barrows, near the A35 road about 2 miles west of Winterbourne Abbas, in Dorset, England [1]. Although the barrows are situated not on a ridge or hilltop, as is usually found, but on relatively low-lying ground, the site is near the head of a valley descending to the east [1]. Most are south of the A35 road, with a few north of the road [1]. They date from about 1500 BC [1]. The barrows, individually or in groups, are separately listed as ancient monuments [1]. There is a minor plateau on the west of the site, from which all the barrows can be seen [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
