    ('2cbc1c11-5750-4b96-973b-7f1f8c847003'::uuid, 'legacy', '4', '1', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:2cbc1c11-5750-4b96-973b-7f1f8c847003:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''1'' restored on Skara Brae', '4c02781be1ffa92097fd7f10b24ac12d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 4 from period -4000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28456", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0136:chunk-0002: period_start ''-4000'' -> ''-3180''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35710", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-3180'' -> ''-4000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44089", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0087:chunk-0001: description ''Skara Brae is a stone-built Neolithic settlement on the Bay of Skaill, Orkney, Scotland, dating to 4500–3000 BC[1]. The village consisted of ten clustered houses constructed from local flagstones, supported by earthen dams[1]. Each dwelling featured stone-built beds, hearths, and cupboards, demonstrating sophisticated domestic planning[1]. Notably, the settlement possessed an advanced sanitation system with individual toilets and drains that channeled waste to the sea[1].'' -> ''Skara Brae is a stone-built Neolithic settlement located along the Bay of Skaill on the west coast of Mainland, Orkney [1]. The site was discovered following a storm exposing the presence of stone structures within the coastal sand dunes [1]. The site was occupied from roughly 3180 BC to around 2500 BC and is Europe\\''s most complete Neolithic village [1]. Skara Brae gained UNESCO World Heritage Site status as one of four sites making up \"The Heart of Neolithic Orkney\" [1]. The inhabitants of Skara Brae were makers and users of grooved ware, a distinctive style of pottery that had recently appeared in northern Scotland [1]. The buildings follow a similar layout with a central hearth, beds on either side of the hearth, and a dresser opposite the entrance [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
