0 BC and is Europe\\''s most complete Neolithic village [1]. Skara Brae gained UNESCO World Heritage Site status as one of four sites making up \"The Heart of Neolithic Orkney\" [1]. The inhabitants of Skara Brae were makers and users of grooved ware, a distinctive style of pottery that had recently appeared in northern Scotland [1]. The buildings follow a similar layout with a central hearth, beds on either side of the hearth, and a dresser opposite the entrance [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
