uring the rule of the Labeates and Ardiaei, whose capital was Scodra [1]. Within the castle there are the ruins of a 13th-century Venetian Catholic church, considered by scholars as the St. Stephen''s Cathedral, which after the siege of Shkodër in the 15th century, when the Ottoman Empire captured the city, was transformed into the Fatih Sultan Mehmet Mosque [1]. The castle has been the site of several famous sieges, including the siege of Shkodër of 1478-79 and the siege of Shkodër of 1912-13 [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('332d3d4c-4dfd-4a7a-8f79-0db277b1f4c6'::uuid, 'rarity_tier', '4', '3', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:332d3d4c-4dfd-4a7a-8f79-0db277b1f4c6:rarity_tier', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: rarity_tier ''3'' restored on Rozafa Castle', 'b472d77318112afa1a9d4e383827ab8e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_tier = 4 from rarity_score 46"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33583", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0022:chunk-0001: description ''Rozafa Castle stands on a rocky hill at the confluence of the Buna and Drin rivers near Shkoder, Albania, with origins tracing back to the Early Bronze Age, roughly 4,000 years ago. The earliest fortification walls date to the 4th-3rd century BC, when it served as the citadel of the Illyrian city of Scodra, capital of the Labeatae tribe under King Gentius. Cyclopean walls of massive arranged rocks dating to c. 350 BC survive between the first two castle gates. The visible walls are mostly Venetian construction. A famous Albanian legend tells of a woman named Rozafa immured in the walls during construction. The castle and its surroundings form an Archaeological Park of Albania.'' -> \"Rozafa Castle or Shkodër Castle is a castle in the city of Shkodër, in northwestern Albania [1]. It rises imposingly on a rocky hill, 130 metres above sea level, surrounded by the Buna and Drin rivers [1]. The hill was settled since the Early Bronze Age [1]. Nevertheless, the visible walls of the castle are mostly of Venetian construction [1]. Due to its strategic location, the hill had a prominent role in antiquity [1]. There was an Illyrian stronghold during the rule of the Labeates and Ardiaei, whose capital was Scodra [1]. Within the castle there are the ruins of a 13th-century Venetian Catholic church, considered by scholars as the St. Stephen''s Cathedral, which after the siege of Shkodër in the 15th century, when the Ottoman Empire captured the city, was transformed into the Fatih Sultan Mehmet Mosque [1]. The castle has been the site of several famous sieges, including the siege of Shkodër of 1478-79 and the siege of Shkodër of 1912-13 [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
