    ('388a75e4-cdec-4123-b545-2eb6d9b0fa14'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:388a75e4-cdec-4123-b545-2eb6d9b0fa14:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Castro of Zambujal', '96ceb8727a2a561d7be37563773a88ef', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 45 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44153", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0089:chunk-0001: description ''The Castro of Zambujal is a Chalcolithic fortified settlement near Torres Vedras, about 50 km northwest of Lisbon, Portugal, first occupied at the beginning of the 3rd millennium BC and inhabited for at least 800 years until around 1700 BC. One of the largest Copper Age settlements in Western Europe, it features walls still standing up to 4 metres high with oval-shaped adobe houses approximately 6 m in diameter. Discovered by Leonel Trindade in 1938, systematic excavations by the German Archaeological Institute began in 1964, making it one of the best-documented sites of the period on the Iberian Peninsula. Key finds include gold ornaments, copper tools, ivory items, Bell Beaker pottery, and over 1,000 arrowheads found between defensive lines, suggesting armed conflict.'' -> ''The Castro of Zambujal is a Chalcolithic age archeological site in the municipality of Torres Vedras in the western litoral area of the Centro Region [1]. The site was discovered and interpreted in 1932 by the Portuguese archaeologist Leonel Trindade, who made the first investigation in 1944 and, together with Aurélio Ricardo Belo, from 1959 to 1961 [1]. The site was first occupied in the Chalcolithic period, at the beginning of the 3rd millennium BC [1]. The site and surrounding land was specifically tied to a chalcolithic settlement that constructed a wall around itself to protect its community from attack [1]. The houses on-site were oval shaped, approximately 6 metres in diameter, and made from adobe [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
