    ('3962491a-58ec-4e7c-a1be-3c456f9818a6'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:3962491a-58ec-4e7c-a1be-3c456f9818a6:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''5'' restored on Longstone of Minchinhampton', '22efeb28c4383eea1e4ed5b0c5f86f3a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 24 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33639", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0023:chunk-0001: description ''A late Neolithic or early Bronze Age standing stone on Minchinhampton Common in Gloucestershire, standing 2.3 m high and made of oolitic limestone. The stone is perforated by two large and several smaller natural holes caused by weathering, and is believed to be the closing slab from a destroyed burial chamber or portal dolmen. Local folklore held that the holes had curative powers, and children with whooping cough or rickets were passed through them. A fallen stone 11 m to the southwest, now built into a dry stone wall, may have formed a pair with the Longstone. It is a Scheduled Ancient Monument.'' -> ''The Longstone of Minchinhampton is a standing stone on Minchinhampton Common, Minchinhampton in Gloucestershire, England [1]. The Longstone is a large piece of oolitic limestone standing upright in a field, known as Longstone Field, 1 mile east of the Minchinhampton village [1]. It is 7.5 feet high and 6 feet wide, with natural holes through its 15 inches width [1]. Nearby, 12 yards to the south west, there is a fallen standing stone which has been built into a dry stone wall, and may have once formed a stone pair with the Longstone [1]. The area has numerous barrows and near the Longstone ornaments, flint and arrowheads have all been found [1]. The stone was believed to be able to cure infants of maladies such as measles and whooping cough, by passing the child through one of the natural holes [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
