    ('3cb65471-1fc1-4055-9cfa-63d7514aeaee'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:3cb65471-1fc1-4055-9cfa-63d7514aeaee:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Aššur', '59816685df6c44f4be325965cb672346', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -3000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:33681", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0024:chunk-0001: description ''First capital of the Assyrian Empire on the western bank of the Tigris in modern Iraq, serving as the religious and political heart of Assyria from roughly 2500 BC to 614 BC. The site features three ziggurats, including one built from six million mud bricks that once stood 170 feet tall, and double temples dedicated to the god Ashur. Excavated by Walter Andrae from 1903-1914 using pioneering stratigraphic methods. A UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2003, it was simultaneously placed on the Endangered list due to a proposed dam project.'' -> \"Aššur, also known as Ashur and Qal''at Sherqat, was the capital of the Middle Assyrian Empire for a time [1]. The remains of the city lie on the western bank of the Tigris River, north of the confluence with its tributary, the Little Zab, in what is now Iraq [1]. Occupation of the city itself continued for approximately 3,000 years, from the Early Dynastic Period to the mid-3rd century AD, when the city was sacked by the Sasanian Empire, after which it was sparsely populated until the massacres of Assyrian Christians conducted by Tamurlane in the 14th century AD after which the remaining population relocated to the countryside [1]. The city lies on a south facing mountain spur with a triangular layout [1]. The northern, higher, area held public buildings including the palace and temples of Ashur, Anu, Adad, Sin, Shamash, and Ishtar [1]. Temples to the moon god Sin and the sun god Shamash were built and dedicated through the 15th century BC [1]. Tukulti-Ninurta I (1244–1208 BC) also constructed a new temple to the goddess Ishtar in the location of the original temple [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
