    ('3cf8d34b-fa9a-421f-8e8a-f21d910affe2'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:3cf8d34b-fa9a-421f-8e8a-f21d910affe2:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Barbara Baths', '4270cfd8478127abbfe53e6c8a896283', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period 1..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44161", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0089:chunk-0001: description ''The Barbara Baths (Barbarathermen) are the remains of a massive Roman bath complex in Trier (Augusta Treverorum), Germany, built in the 2nd century AD. Sprawling over 42,000 square metres, they were the largest Roman baths north of the Alps. The complex featured heated rooms with a two-storey hypocaust system, underground service tunnels, furnaces, and pools. The baths remained in use through the 4th century when Trier had roughly 75,000 inhabitants, but fell into disuse after Frankish attacks around 410-435 AD. The site was later repurposed as a medieval monastery dedicated to Saint Barbara, then demolished in 1610 for materials to build a Jesuit college. Now a UNESCO World Heritage Site, the underground tunnels remain accessible to visitors.'' -> ''The Barbara Baths are a large Roman bath complex in Augusta Treverorum, modern-day Trier [1]. Stretching over 42,000 square meters, it is the largest Roman bath north of the Alps [1]. The Barbara Baths were built in the second half of the 2nd century C.E. along with a burst of building activity including a new bridge, an amphitheater and large forum [1]. The heated rooms of the bathhouse are notable for using a two-story hypocaust [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
