    ('403e3c53-6a70-49cc-81b8-2014542e53d3'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:403e3c53-6a70-49cc-81b8-2014542e53d3:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Stadium at Olympia', '4f7a741bb07b7e4e0c461d6dab763f8c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -1500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44167", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0089:chunk-0001: description \"The Stadium at Olympia, east of the sanctuary of Zeus, hosted the athletic competitions of the ancient Olympic Games from 776 BC onward. It evolved through three phases: an Archaic stadium within the Altis, a second relocated eastward, and the final Classical form (5th century BC) with a track measuring 192.28 m by 30.74 m. Spectators -- estimated at 40,000 -- sat on earthen embankments, with stone seats reserved only for judges (hellanodikai). The late Hellenistic Crypte tunnel served as the athletes'' entrance. Excavation was completed in 1960 and the site holds UNESCO World Heritage status since 1989.\" -> ''The Stadium at Olympia is an ancient stadium at the archaeological site of Olympia, Greece, located to the east of the sanctuary of Zeus [1]. It was the location of many of the sporting events at the Ancient Olympic Games [1]. The track was made of hard-packed clay to serve as traction for the contestants in the running events [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
