    ('40a33fe3-d4c6-4eda-ab0e-45bac8de7bdb'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:40a33fe3-d4c6-4eda-ab0e-45bac8de7bdb:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on Jewry Wall', '27d5d97253999e43c84c5de155d59de7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Wall'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44169", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0089:chunk-0001: description \"The Jewry Wall is a substantial ruined wall of 2nd-century Roman masonry with two large archways, located in Leicester, England [1]. It formed the west wall of a public building in Ratae Corieltauvorum (Roman Leicester), alongside public baths whose foundations were excavated in the 1930s and are open to view [1]. The wall stands alongside St Nicholas'' Circle and St Nicholas'' Church and gives its name to the adjacent Jewry Wall Museum [1].\" -> ''The Jewry Wall is a substantial ruined wall of 2nd-century Roman masonry, with two large archways, in Leicester [1]. The wall is an impressive example of standing Roman masonry [1]. It dates to approximately 125–130 AD [1]. It measures 23 m long, 8 m high and 2.5 m thick [1]. It is among the largest pieces of surviving civil Roman architecture in Britain, and is comparable to the \"Old Work\" at Wroxeter [1]. The structure comprises alternate bands of Roman brick and coursed masonry, of local granite, limestone and sandstone [1]. In the centre of the wall are two large arched openings about 3 m wide and 4 m high, and there are further arched alcoves on the eastern side [1]. The wall appears to have formed the long western side of a large rectangular basilica-like structure [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
