    ('4115430c-0702-4c66-81ba-a6f1b580633a'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Monuments', 'Megalithic', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4115430c-0702-4c66-81ba-a6f1b580633a:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Megalithic'' restored on Flavian Amphitheatre, Pozzuoli', '7375657154fbfe23b14c615a43fc7145', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Monuments from site_type ''Amphitheatre'' is in group ''Monuments''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29404", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0283:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Amphitheatre''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44171", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0089:chunk-0001: description ''The Flavian Amphitheatre of Pozzuoli, built in the second half of the 1st century AD under emperors Nero, Vespasian, and Titus, is the third-largest Roman amphitheatre ever constructed. It could accommodate up to 40,000 spectators across three tiers of seating. The underground areas, located 7 metres below ground, contained an elaborate system of gears and pulleys for raising animals and scenery to the arena, connected to the Phlegrean aqueduct for sophisticated water drainage. While medieval marble stripping damaged the exterior, the interior and subterranean chambers remain exceptionally well preserved.'' -> ''The Flavian Amphitheater, located in Pozzuoli, is the third-largest Roman amphitheater in Italy [1]. Only the Roman Colosseum and the Amphitheatre of Capua are larger [1]. It was likely built by the same architects who previously constructed the Roman Colosseum [1]. The elliptical structure measures 147 by 117 meters, with the arena floor measuring 72.22 by 42.33 meters [1]. The arena can hold up to 50,000 spectators [1]. The interior is mostly intact and one can still see parts of gears, which were used to lift cages up to the arena floor [1]. In 305, the arena was the setting for the persecutions of the patron saint of Pozzuoli, Saint Proculus, and the patron saint of Naples, Saint Januarius [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
