    ('424a8ef0-74a3-48ab-bdaf-8df3d957e36c'::uuid, 'antiquity', '10', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:424a8ef0-74a3-48ab-bdaf-8df3d957e36c:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: antiquity ''4'' restored on Rouffignac Cave', 'f3f75de82bb0cd32f28a1cc7c24c412e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 10 from period_start -11000"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28163", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0085:chunk-0001: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-11000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30384", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34373", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0026:chunk-0001: description ''Rouffignac Cave, known as the \"Cave of the Hundred Mammoths,\" is located in the Dordogne department of France and contains over 250 engravings and paintings dating to approximately 13,000 years ago during the Magdalenian period. The cave features 158 mammoth depictions, comprising roughly 30% of all mammoths in Paleolithic art worldwide. The Grand Plafond ceiling gathers 66 animals in a single composition. Extending over 8 km, it is one of Europe\\''s largest decorated caves. Classified as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1979, visitors explore via electric train to preserve the artwork.'' -> ''The Rouffignac cave, in the French commune of Rouffignac-Saint-Cernin-de-Reilhac in the Dordogne département, contains over 250 engravings and cave paintings dating back to the Upper Paleolithic [1]. The Rouffignac cave was mentioned in 1575 by François de Belleforest [1]. Famous archeologists such as Henri Breuil, André Glory and Édouard-Alfred Martel had visited the cave in the early 20th century, but it was only in 1956 that Louis-René Nougier and Romain Robert, two prehistorians from the Pyrenees, rediscovered and confirmed the cave art [1]. The artistic representations in the cave were mainly executed as engravings or black contour drawings [1]. Reconnaissance excavations in front of the entry of the cave found traces of human occupations (several fireplaces, animal bones and stone tools) dating back to the Mesolithic and to the Neolithic [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
