    ('43bb69c7-5c0e-4b99-baed-f23b921b0ca4'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:43bb69c7-5c0e-4b99-baed-f23b921b0ca4:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Kilmashogue', '981827f269f649daa5e21f34cba2f3fb', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Necropolis/tombs complex'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 47 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28149", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0081:chunk-0001: site_type ''Necropolis/tombs complex'' -> ''Natural feature''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34377", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0026:chunk-0001: description ''A wedge tomb on the northern slopes of Kilmashogue Mountain at 213 m elevation in the Dublin Mountains, dating to c. 2500-2000 BC. The gallery features two chambers aligned northeast-southwest with unusual triple-lined outer walls and three rows of orthostats on each side. Excavated by H.E. Kilbride Jones in 1953, the main chamber yielded fragments of cremated bone and charcoal, while later Bronze Age cists contained inverted pottery food vessels with cremated human remains. One of a cluster of three wedge tombs in South Dublin.'' -> ''Kilmashogue or Kilmashoge is a mountain in Dún Laoghaire–Rathdown county in Ireland [1]. It is 408 metres high and forms part of the group of hills in the Dublin Mountains which comprises Two Rock, Three Rock, Kilmashogue and Tibradden Mountains [1]. A number of prehistoric monuments can be found on the slopes of the mountain [1]. On the northern slopes is the remains of a wedge tomb [1]. It was excavated in 1953 by H. E. Kilbride-Jones, aided by Rúaidhrí de Valera and Seán Ó Nualláin, of the Royal Irish Academy [1]. The tomb consists of two chambers which were originally covered by a cairn of stones [1]. The main chamber is 4 metres long and 1.5 metres wide and is formed by a triple-walled gallery [1]. On the northwestern slopes, in a field near the village of Rockbrook, are two standing stones, square in section and approximately 1.8 metres tall [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35754", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: site_type ''Natural feature'' -> ''Necropolis/tombs complex''"}]'::jsonb),
