he tomb of Philip II were a golden larnax emblazoned with the sixteen-rayed \"Sun of Vergina\" on its lid, containing the king\\''s bones, an intricate gold burial wreath, a silver and gold diadem with Heracles knot, silver and bronze vessels from the funeral feast, and carved ivory ornaments from the funeral couch [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('474c3e7d-ee4a-4c29-a62e-9ef87ca12ea0'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:474c3e7d-ee4a-4c29-a62e-9ef87ca12ea0:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Lauriacum', '8df7eb7110abb6d2b27f657514524ad2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period 1..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34421", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0027:chunk-0001: description \"Lauriacum (modern Lorch-Enns) was a major Roman legionary fortress and civilian town at the confluence of the Danube and Enns rivers in the province of Noricum. The Legio II Italica was permanently garrisoned here from around 205 AD under Septimius Severus, and the adjacent civilian settlement achieved municipal status under Caracalla in 212 AD, making it the last town founded by Romans in Noricum. The rhomboid-shaped camp measured roughly 539 x 398 metres with defensive walls, towers, barracks, and a valetudinarium. Now a UNESCO World Heritage component of the Danube Limes, its museum houses one of Austria''s most important Roman-period collections.\" -> \"Lauriacum (or Laureacum) was an important legionary Roman town on the Danube Limes in Austria [1]. Initially only a small Roman settlement was located at a ford over the Enns [1]. The legionary camp was subsequently also part of the fortifications of the limes and probably from the 3rd to the 5th century continuously occupied with Roman troops [1]. In the north and south-west was an extensive civilian settlement, which was probably raised to the municipality in the early third century and rose to the bishop''s seat of the northern Noricum in the 5th century [1]. Grave fields could also be found at numerous places inside and outside the settlement area [1]. In the late period, it became the base for a patrol boat fleet and the production site of a state shield factory [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
