    ('4a87bc78-0813-4110-aa1e-3818326bee00'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '10', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4a87bc78-0813-4110-aa1e-3818326bee00:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''10'' restored on Cave of the Guanches', '554853630a3457012e0f684de0f82655', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Archaeological site'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 7 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44347", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0091:chunk-0001: description ''A protected archaeological site in the municipality of Icod de los Vinos on the north coast of Tenerife, Canary Islands, Spain, designated as a Bien de Interes Cultural. The cave complex of connected chambers preserves the earliest known Guanche settlements on Tenerife, with ceramics dated to the 6th century BCE providing the oldest chronologies in the Canary Islands. Researchers believe it served as a residence for the menceyes, the traditional Guanche leaders. Excavations yielded hand-made pottery, bone awls, caprid remains, marine shells, and a human mandible. Finds are held in the Museo Guanche in Icod de los Vinos and the British Museum.'' -> ''The Cave of the Guanches, or Archaeological area of the Cave of the Guanches, is an important archaeological site located in the north of the island of Tenerife (Canary Islands, Spain) [1]. The protected archaeological environment is in the municipality of Icod de los Vinos and revolves around the Cave of the Guanches [1]. This archaeological area is where the earliest Guanche autochthonous settlements on the island of Tenerife have been found [1]. It has provided the oldest chronologies of the Canary Islands [1]. These have been dated to the 6th century BCE, according to analysis carried out on ceramics that were found inside [1]. The area was in aboriginal times a village of natural caves and huts (now disappeared), which were located at the top of a cliff, about 125 m above sea level [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4a87bc78-0813-4110-aa1e-3818326bee00'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '33', '42', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4a87bc78-0813-4110-aa1e-3818326bee00:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: rarity_score ''42'' restored on Cave of the Guanches', '554853630a3457012e0f684de0f82655', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 33 from mystery 7, cultural_influence 6"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44347", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0091:chunk-0001: description ''A protected archaeological site in the municipality of Icod de los Vinos on the north coast of Tenerife, Canary Islands, Spain, designated as a Bien de Interes Cultural. The cave complex of connected chambers preserves the earliest known Guanche settlements on Tenerife, with ceramics dated to the 6th century BCE providing the oldest chronologies in the Canary Islands. Researchers believe it served as a residence for the menceyes, the traditional Guanche leaders. Excavations yielded hand-made pottery, bone awls, caprid remains, marine shells, and a human mandible. Finds are held in the Museo Guanche in Icod de los Vinos and the British Museum.'' -> ''The Cave of the Guanches, or Archaeological area of the Cave of the Guanches, is an important archaeological site located in the north of the island of Tenerife (Canary Islands, Spain) [1]. The protected archaeological environment is in the municipality of Icod de los Vinos and revolves around the Cave of the Guanches [1]. This archaeological area is where the earliest Guanche autochthonous settlements on the island of Tenerife have been found [1]. It has provided the oldest chronologies of the Canary Islands [1]. These have been dated to the 6th century BCE, according to analysis carried out on ceramics that were found inside [1]. The area was in aboriginal times a village of natural caves and huts (now disappeared), which were located at the top of a cliff, about 125 m above sea level [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4a87bc78-0813-4110-aa1e-3818326bee00'::uuid, 'total_power', '26', '29', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4a87bc78-0813-4110-aa1e-3818326bee00:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: total_power ''29'' restored on Cave of the Guanches', '554853630a3457012e0f684de0f82655', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 26 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 5 + 7 + 6 + 7 + 1"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44347", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0091:chunk-0001: description ''A protected archaeological site in the municipality of Icod de los Vinos on the north coast of Tenerife, Canary Islands, Spain, designated as a Bien de Interes Cultural. The cave complex of connected chambers preserves the earliest known Guanche settlements on Tenerife, with ceramics dated to the 6th century BCE providing the oldest chronologies in the Canary Islands. Researchers believe it served as a residence for the menceyes, the traditional Guanche leaders. Excavations yielded hand-made pottery, bone awls, caprid remains, marine shells, and a human mandible. Finds are held in the Museo Guanche in Icod de los Vinos and the British Museum.'' -> ''The Cave of the Guanches, or Archaeological area of the Cave of the Guanches, is an important archaeological site located in the north of the island of Tenerife (Canary Islands, Spain) [1]. The protected archaeological environment is in the municipality of Icod de los Vinos and revolves around the Cave of the Guanches [1]. This archaeological area is where the earliest Guanche autochthonous settlements on the island of Tenerife have been found [1]. It has provided the oldest chronologies of the Canary Islands [1]. These have been dated to the 6th century BCE, according to analysis carried out on ceramics that were found inside [1]. The area was in aboriginal times a village of natural caves and huts (now disappeared), which were located at the top of a cliff, about 125 m above sea level [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4ab1bd6e-a7fd-41c3-b341-9959d77d5a6e'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4ab1bd6e-a7fd-41c3-b341-9959d77d5a6e:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Great Ziggurat of Ur', 'd0df3c50554e223203e43b073e569293', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43501", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0074:chunk-0001: description \"A Neo-Sumerian step pyramid built c. 2100 BC by King Ur-Nammu of the Third Dynasty of Ur, dedicated to the moon god Nanna. The massive structure measured 64 metres long, 45 metres wide, and over 30 metres high, with three tiers connected by buttressed staircases leading to a summit temple. Construction was completed by Ur-Nammu''s son Shulgi. Excavated in the 1920s by Sir Leonard Woolley, the ziggurat is the best-preserved in Mesopotamia. It crumbled by the 6th century BC before being restored by King Nabonidus, and was partially reconstructed under Saddam Hussein in the 1980s. Damaged during the 1991 Gulf War, it received UNESCO World Heritage status in 2016.\" -> ''The Ziggurat (or Great Ziggurat) of Ur is a Neo-Sumerian ziggurat in what was the city of Ur near Nasiriyah, in present-day Dhi Qar Province, Iraq [1]. The structure was built during the Early Bronze Age (21st century BC) by King Ur-Nammu of the Third Dynasty of Ur [1]. It had crumbled to ruins by the 6th century BC of the Neo-Babylonian period, when it was restored by King Nabonidus [1]. Its remains were excavated in the 1920s and 1930s by international teams led by Sir Leonard Woolley [1]. The ziggurat was a piece in a temple complex that served as an administrative center for the city, and which was a shrine of the moon god Nanna, the patron deity of Ur [1]. The remains of the ziggurat consist of a three-layered solid mass of mud brick faced with burnt bricks set in bitumen [1]. The lowest layer corresponds to the original construction of Ur-Nammu, while the two upper layers are part of the Neo-Babylonian restorations [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
