by Sir Leonard Woolley [1]. The ziggurat was a piece in a temple complex that served as an administrative center for the city, and which was a shrine of the moon god Nanna, the patron deity of Ur [1]. The remains of the ziggurat consist of a three-layered solid mass of mud brick faced with burnt bricks set in bitumen [1]. The lowest layer corresponds to the original construction of Ur-Nammu, while the two upper layers are part of the Neo-Babylonian restorations [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
