    ('4b4a9785-3993-4bc1-91f2-6df76949b100'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4b4a9785-3993-4bc1-91f2-6df76949b100:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Woerden', '1e401747d042f317ad150b88d4ee3ed2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period 1..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44349", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0091:chunk-0001: description ''Site of Castellum Laurium, a Roman limes fort built c.40 AD on the Lower Rhine frontier in the Netherlands. The garrison of approximately 500 soldiers guarded the Grecht river crossing through multiple phases, from timber construction under Caligula to stone rebuilding in the mid-2nd century. Seven Roman ships were discovered, including the 30-meter Woerden 7 freighter (wood dated to 163 AD). Finds include a parade helmet, a dedication altar to the Syrian sun-god Elagabal, and catapult stones. Now part of the UNESCO Lower Germanic Limes World Heritage Site.'' -> ''Woerden is situated on the river Oude Rijn, near the confluence with the former Linschoten (water) stream [1]. Near the former confluence was an area that was slightly more elevated than the surroundings, a natural levee, which — in an area that is prone to flooding — made it an attractive location for settlement [1]. Here, at the highest spot, the Romans built a castellum (Castellum Laurum), as part of the limes of the Roman Empire and thus part of the defense lines of the northern border of the Roman Empire [1]. The first castellum was built in the 40s AD and was destroyed in 69 AD during the Batavian rebellion [1]. In 70 AD, the castellum was rebuilt, and the Romans remained until 402 AD, with an interruption lasting from about 275-300 AD [1]. A Frankish sword dated from the 8th century has been found on the site of the Roman Castellum in 2012 [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
