    ('4c40a3eb-e009-4b8b-b507-18053a6538a1'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Religious', 'Settlements', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4c40a3eb-e009-4b8b-b507-18053a6538a1:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Settlements'' restored on Heraion at Foce del Sele', '8da3d13e6bf40cb6cf24286065dbd8ab', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Religious from site_type ''Sanctuary'' is in group ''Religious''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28364", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0117:chunk-0001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Sanctuary''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43503", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0074:chunk-0001: description \"The Heraion at Foce del Sele is an ancient Greek sanctuary dedicated to the goddess Hera in Magna Graecia, situated at the mouth of the Sele River approximately 8 km north of the Greek city of Poseidonia[1]. Due to centuries of riverine sediment deposition, the site now lies about 2.3 km from the modern coastline rather than at the water''s edge[1]. The sanctuary is located in the modern comune of Capaccio-Paestum in southern Italy[1].\" -> ''The Heraion at Foce del Sele is an archaeological site consisting of an Ancient Greek sanctuary complex dedicated to the goddess Hera in what was Magna Graecia [1]. When built, the complex was located at the mouth of the Sele, approximately 8 km north of the Greek city of Poseidonia that was famous for its three standing Greek temples [1]. Due to the deposition of alluvial sediment by the river, the site now is approximately 2.3 km from the modern coast [1]. Construction at the complex is dated from the sixth to at least the third centuries BC [1]. The ancient sanctuary was founded at the beginning of the sixth century BC by the Greek colony at Paestum who originally were from further south at Sybaris [1]. Approximately seventy metope reliefs carved in local sandstone have been recovered during excavations [1]. Artefacts found during excavations of the sanctuary have included a large number of buried votive offerings (mostly terracotta statuettes of the goddess) that were buried some time after being left at the shrine [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
