    ('4c555421-7d9c-4921-b5c1-f5e386c6f6bd'::uuid, 'total_power', '31', '30', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4c555421-7d9c-4921-b5c1-f5e386c6f6bd:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: total_power ''30'' restored on Asklepion, Kos', 'c121604cad2b8e80bff4c97a990cf109', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 31 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 6 + 8 + 10 + 6 + 1"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4c6ce4c1-8c8c-4982-a0fc-8df781a35ef6'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4c6ce4c1-8c8c-4982-a0fc-8df781a35ef6:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''8'' restored on Avebury', '9b92a533d900999b5bea503cb5f86f49', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Henge'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 7 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44357", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0091:chunk-0001: description ''Avebury is a Neolithic henge monument in Wiltshire, England. It encloses three stone circles and contains the largest megalithic stone circle in the world. One of the best-known prehistoric sites in Britain, it remains a place of religious importance to contemporary pagans and a popular tourist attraction [1].'' -> ''Avebury is a Neolithic henge monument containing three stone circles, around the village of Avebury in Wiltshire, in south-west England [1]. The Avebury monument is a part of a larger prehistoric landscape containing several older monuments nearby, including West Kennet Long Barrow, Windmill Hill and Silbury Hill [1]. By the Iron Age, the site had been effectively abandoned, with some evidence of human activity on the site during the Roman period [1]. The Avebury monument is a henge, a type of monument consisting of a large circular bank with an internal ditch [1]. The henge is not perfectly circular and measures 347.4 metres in diameter and over 1,000 metres in circumference [1]. Radiocarbon dating of some stone settings indicate a construction date of around 2870–2200 BC [1]. The West Kennet Avenue, an avenue of paired stones, leads from the southeastern entrance of the henge; and traces of a second, the Beckhampton Avenue, lead out from the western entrance [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
