    ('4d5e5614-2bef-4d1c-85be-8fbe423bac32'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4d5e5614-2bef-4d1c-85be-8fbe423bac32:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Kong Svends Høj', 'c6ac52d3ccd7771745898a21f5e83cc1', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Necropolis/tombs complex'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 47 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34453", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0028:chunk-0001: description \"Kong Svends Hoj (King Svend''s Mound) is a Neolithic passage grave on the island of Lolland in Denmark, north of Pederstrup, dating to around 3200 BC. Attributed to the Funnel Beaker culture, it features a burial chamber 12.5 meters long constructed from 17 supporting stones and 6 covering stones, making it one of Denmark''s largest and most famous passage graves. The rectangular mound is surrounded by kerbstones reaching approximately 1.5 meters in height, among the tallest recorded in any Neolithic tomb. First excavated in 1780 by a Danish prime minister, the entrance was not discovered until restoration work in 1942.\" -> ''Kong Svends Høj is a passage grave on the island of Lolland in Denmark, immediately north of Pederstrup [1]. Kong Svends Høj consists of a large, rectangular mound covering a chamber 12.5 metres in length [1]. The mound is surrounded by kerbstones, which, with a height of around 1.5 metres, are among the tallest recorded in a Neolithic tomb [1]. The gable stones are almost 4 metres high [1]. The north-western façade originally had two outliers, known as \"horns\" or guard stones [1]. The first excavations were attempted in 1780 by a Danish prime minister and a pastor\\''s son who later became bishop of Copenhagen [1]. The tomb was first restored in 1942, when the entrance was discovered [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
