    ('4dc7086c-f261-4f38-85f2-10a20eeda1bc'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4dc7086c-f261-4f38-85f2-10a20eeda1bc:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on La Garma Cave Complex', '1b84fe8d6b5ad28f70f6b1f43481c216', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -400000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44359", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0091:chunk-0001: description ''The La Garma Cave Complex is a parietal art-bearing paleoanthropological cave system in Cantabria, Spain, located just north of the village of Omoño in the municipality of Ribamontán al Monte [1]. The cave complex contains more than 4,000 fossils and over 500 graphical units, including 109 signs, 92 animal figures, and 40 hand stencils [2]. It is noted for having one of the best preserved floors from the Paleolithic period in Europe [1]. La Garma is part of the Cave of Altamira and Paleolithic Cave Art of Northern Spain World Heritage Site [1].'' -> ''The La Garma cave complex is a parietal art-bearing paleoanthropological cave system in Cantabria, Spain [1]. The cave complex is noted for one of the best preserved floors from the Paleolithic containing more than 4,000 fossils and more than 500 graphical units [1]. The Lower Gallery of La Garma is situated at 59 m above sea level and is around 300 m in length [1]. It was discovered in November 1995 [1]. Its original entrance was sealed during the Pleistocene by a rockslide about 16,000 years ago, preserving the cave floor in a pristine manner [1]. La Garma is notable for its rich repository of Magdalenian portable art found in The Lower Gallery [1]. The most outstanding artefact is a backward-facing ibex depiction carved onto a bovine rib spatula [1]. The peculiar remains of five Visigothic youths were found deep in the cave system [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
