    ('4fbef8ee-1d95-429f-ae1d-bf525dab7ab1'::uuid, 'mystery', '8', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:4fbef8ee-1d95-429f-ae1d-bf525dab7ab1:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on Schwarzenacker Roman Museum', 'b6d2f7f818b8ce1d6bb78d1b39aa6352', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 8 from (''Museum'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 5 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34481", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0029:chunk-0001: description ''Schwarzenacker Roman Museum is an archaeological open-air museum in Homburg, Saarland, Germany, displaying the remains of a Gallo-Roman vicus founded under Emperor Augustus and destroyed by the Alamanni around 275 AD. The settlement housed approximately 2,000 inhabitants and flourished for about 300 years as a commercial center. Excavations led by archaeologist Alfons Kolling uncovered foundations, cellars, roads, and canals. Highlights include the reconstructed House of the Ophthalmologist, a bakery, and residential streets with canopied pavements.'' -> ''Römermuseum Schwarzenacker is an archaeological open-air museum in Schwarzenacker, a district of Homburg, Saarland, Germany [1]. The museum was constructed by archaeologist Alfonso Kolling, who also led the archaeological excavations at the site [1]. The Museum shows the remains of a Roman vicus of approximately 2000 inhabitants which existed from the time of the birth of Christ until its destruction by the Alemanni in 275 A.D. Visitors can view the excavated buildings, grounds, roads and culverts [1]. In the adjoining 18th century villa and the reconstructed houses of the vicus important finds from the everyday life of the Roman population are exhibited, found either at the settlement itself or in the surrounding area [1]. At the front steps of the villa stand life-size replicas of Roman equestrian statues which were discovered in 1887 in nearby Breitfurt [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
