here were transferred to the National Archaeological Museum of Athens [1]. The pottery in the tomb dates to around 1500 to 1450 BC, but the gold and carved gem seals might have been old when buried [1]. The objects include a large number of carved gem seals and amethyst beads, together with articles in gold, silver, bronze, iron, lead, amber and crystal [1]. By far the finest of the grave goods are a pair of golden cups decorated with scenes in relief, showing two different methods of capturing bulls, perhaps for the bull-leaping activities practised by the Minoan civilization of Crete, or for sacrifice [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
