    ('50e2e3a4-4f3e-4b1e-bd7a-8b97404f59c8'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:50e2e3a4-4f3e-4b1e-bd7a-8b97404f59c8:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Nipisat Island', 'fc3fedb0ad56bdecd69a47b8159b2d01', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -3000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34491", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0029:chunk-0001: description ''A small uninhabited island in central-western Greenland, 15 km south of Sisimiut, containing a major Saqqaq culture archaeological site representing the earliest documented human occupation of Greenland (c. 2500-800 BC). Excavations from 1989 to 1994 uncovered over 70,000 bone fragments, 20,000 flakes, and 1,000 artefacts, with radiocarbon dating revealing nearly 1,500 years of continuous occupation. Caribou comprised 55% of mammal remains, reflecting seasonal inland hunting strategies. In 2018, the site was inscribed as part of the Aasivissuit-Nipisat UNESCO World Heritage Site, recognizing over 4,200 years of Arctic hunting traditions.'' -> ''Nipisat Island is a small, uninhabited island in the Qeqqata municipality in central-western Greenland [1]. The island is notable for its well preserved Saqqaq culture archaeological site, containing some stone artifacts that were previously unknown from the Saqqaq culture [1]. The site, named after the island, was discovered in 1989 by Finn Kramer, curator of the Sisimiut Museum [1]. It lies approximately 50 m from the present coastline, situated on raised beaches with a southeastern slope [1]. The area elevation ranges between 9 m (30 ft)and 13 m (43 ft) above mean sea level [1]. This part of the island that contains the archaeological site, did not show signs of later occupation by Dorset culture or Thule culture [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
