    ('544c05a7-3614-43b8-bf63-a158622e5fdb'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Burial & Death', 'Megalithic', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:544c05a7-3614-43b8-bf63-a158622e5fdb:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Megalithic'' restored on Ħal Saflieni Hypogeum', '75da1119055a6314989fa815dabd1fac', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Burial & Death from site_type ''Necropolis/tombs complex'' is in group ''Burial & Death''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29158", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0247:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Necropolis/tombs complex''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34531", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0030:chunk-0001: description ''(4500-3000 BC) A Neolithic subterranean structure hewn into soft globigerina limestone in Paola, Malta. The Hypogeum features three superimposed underground levels and is thought to have served as both a sanctuary and necropolis, with archeological evidence documenting the remains of over 7,000 individuals [1]. It represents one of the best preserved examples of the Maltese temple building culture that also produced the Megalithic Temples and Xagħra Stone Circle [1].'' -> ''The Hypogeum of Ħal Saflieni is a Neolithic subterranean structure dating to the Saflieni phase (3300 – 3000 BC) in Maltese prehistory, located in Paola, Malta [1]. The Hypogeum is thought to have been a sanctuary and necropolis, with the estimated remains of more than 7,000 people documented by archeologists [1]. The Hypogeum was discovered by accident in 1902 when workers cutting cisterns for a new housing development broke through its roof [1]. The study of the structure was first conducted by Manuel Magri, who directed the excavations on behalf of the Museums Committee, starting from November 1903 [1]. During the excavations, a portion of the contents of the Hypogeum, including grave goods and human remains, were emptied out and discarded without being properly catalogued [1]. The Hypogeum is constructed entirely underground and consists of three superimposed levels hewn into soft globigerina limestone, with its halls and chambers interconnected through a labyrinthine series of steps, lintels and doorways [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
