    ('55ce644f-cbfa-4aab-9f3f-e7cca251f3ad'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:55ce644f-cbfa-4aab-9f3f-e7cca251f3ad:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Chan Chan', 'c26a3b0af806270f84354439818a61f3', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period 500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34535", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0030:chunk-0001: description \"Chan Chan was the capital of the Chimu kingdom (c. 900-1470 AD) and the largest adobe city in the Americas, covering approximately 20 square kilometers near modern Trujillo, Peru. The city contained ten walled citadels with massive adobe walls up to 10 meters high, adorned with intricate friezes depicting geometric patterns, marine motifs, fishes, birds, and waves reflecting the civilization''s deep connection to the ocean. Each compound served distinct administrative, ceremonial, and storage functions, with the preserved Tschudi Palace displaying inner courtyards, water reservoirs, and burial chambers. Designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1986 and simultaneously placed on the List of World Heritage in Danger, Chan Chan remains threatened by erosion and El Nino climate events.\" -> ''Chan Chan, sometimes called Chimor itself, was the capital city of the Chimor kingdom [1]. It was the largest city of the pre-Columbian era in South America [1]. It is now an archeological site in the department of La Libertad five kilometers west of Trujillo, Peru [1]. Chan Chan is located in the mouth of the Moche Valley and was the capital of the historical empire of the Chimor from 900 to 1470, when they were defeated and incorporated into the Inca Empire [1]. The city of Chan Chan spanned 20 square kilometers and had a dense urban center of six square kilometers which contained extravagant ciudadelas [1]. Ciudadelas were large architectural masterpieces which housed plazas, storerooms, and burial platforms for the royals [1]. After the Inca conquered the Chimú around 1470 AD, Chan Chan fell into decline [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
