    ('5c3a138c-7052-4cb4-a482-10df27239b2e'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Fortifications', 'Settlements', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:5c3a138c-7052-4cb4-a482-10df27239b2e:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Settlements'' restored on Padderbury Top', 'f39fbd93d0daea56039ffc9d8278840a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Fortifications from site_type ''Fortress/citadel'' is in group ''Fortifications''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27830", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0015:chunk-0001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Fortress/citadel''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34585", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0032:chunk-0001: description ''Padderbury Top is an Iron Age defended settlement (hillfort) about 3.2 km south-east of Menheniot in Cornwall, England. The roughly circular enclosure has an interior diameter of approximately 73 metres, with an inner rampart 9 metres wide and 3 metres high. Aerial photographs reveal originally up to four lines of defence with associated ditches, now largely preserved as buried features. The monument is scheduled under the Ancient Monuments and Archaeological Areas Act 1979 as being of national importance, occupying a prominent skyline position with commanding views across the surrounding landscape.'' -> ''Padderbury Top is a hillfort about 2 miles (3.2 km) south-east of Menheniot, in Cornwall, England, named after the hill on which it is situated [1]. It is a scheduled monument [1]. The enclosure is roughly circular [1]. The diameter of the interior, which has been ploughed, is about 80 yards (73 m) [1]. There is one surviving rampart, 2–3 feet (0.61–0.91 m) above the interior, and 10 feet (3.0 m) externally [1]. There are entrances on the west and east sides [1]. The rampart is surrounded by a belt of flat ground about 60 feet (18 m) wide and about 3 feet (0.91 m) high: aerial photographs show that this area was originally outer defences, since reduced by ploughing [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
