    ('5d975f0c-bbe4-4bd4-9436-5bf4f2bd59b6'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:5d975f0c-bbe4-4bd4-9436-5bf4f2bd59b6:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on Teotihuacan - Pyramid of the Moon', 'fb18de378b6b801bc68090a42203bf32', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Pyramid complex'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44425", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0093:chunk-0001: description ''Built between 1-500 AD, the Pyramid of the Moon is the second-largest pyramid in Teotihuacan, after the Pyramid of the Sun[1]. Located in modern-day San Martín de las Pirámides, Mexico, it dominates the northern end of the Avenue of the Dead[1]. The pyramid\\''s design mimics the silhouette of the mountain Cerro Gordo, which may have been called Tenan, meaning \"mother or protective stone\" in Nahuatl[1]. Archaeological excavations have revealed that the current structure covers an earlier monument predating the Pyramid of the Sun, which was built before 200 AD[1].'' -> \"The Pyramid of the Moon is the second-largest pyramid in Teotihuacan, after the Pyramid of the Sun, and located in modern-day San Martín de las Pirámides [1]. The Pyramid of the Moon covers a structure older than the Pyramid of the Sun which existed prior to 200 AD [1]. The Pyramid''s construction between 100 and 450 AD completed the bilateral symmetry of the temple complex [1]. The pyramid is located at the end of the Avenue of the Dead, connected by a staircase, and was used as a stage for performing ritual sacrifices of animals and humans upon [1]. It was also a burial ground for sacrificial victims [1]. Archaeologists have found a great number of sacrificed remains in the foundations of the pyramid [1]. Among the sacrificial victims were felines, birds of prey, snakes, humans, and more [1]. Within the seven layers that make up the Pyramid of the Moon, there are five burial complexes labeled two through six [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
