    ('6313cd62-456d-4a85-8782-57613c7fc0e1'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:6313cd62-456d-4a85-8782-57613c7fc0e1:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on The Great Pyramid of Giza', 'bbf275cf53d0d103b52381bf55203d95', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -3000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44451", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0094:chunk-0001: description \"The Great Pyramid of Giza is the largest Egyptian pyramid, built c. 2600 BC as the tomb of Fourth Dynasty pharaoh Khufu [1]. Construction took approximately 26 years [1]. It is the oldest of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World and the only one that has remained largely intact [1]. Originally standing at 146.6 meters (481 feet) [1]. The pyramid sits at the northeastern end of the Giza plateau within the UNESCO World Heritage Site ''Memphis and its Necropolis'' [1].\" -> ''The Great Pyramid of Giza is the largest of the Egyptian pyramids and the most famous landmark of the Giza pyramid complex in Giza, Egypt [1]. It is the oldest of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World, and the only wonder that has remained largely intact [1]. The Great Pyramid served as the tomb of Egyptian Pharaoh Khufu, who ruled during the Fourth Dynasty of the Old Kingdom [1]. It was built c.\\u20092600 BC over a period of about 26 years [1]. The Great Pyramid was built by quarrying an estimated 2.3 million large blocks, weighing 6 million tonnes in total [1]. The funerary complex around the pyramid consisted of two mortuary temples connected by a causeway; tombs for the immediate family and court of Khufu, including three smaller pyramids for Khufu\\''s wives; an even smaller \"satellite pyramid\"; and five buried solar barques [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
