    ('64d602b0-c9e8-4313-aa2b-a96ed1d7cf5f'::uuid, 'antiquity', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:64d602b0-c9e8-4313-aa2b-a96ed1d7cf5f:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: antiquity ''8'' restored on El Paraíso, Peru', '3389011031714dabba2afac7db9df2be', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 7 from period_start -2540"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28407", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0127:chunk-0001: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-2540''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30420", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34825", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0034:chunk-0001: description ''3500-1800 BC: El Paraíso (also called Chuquitanta) is a Late Preceramic archaeological site in the Chillón Valley on the central coast of Peru, located several kilometers north of Lima in the San Martin de Porres District [1]. One of the largest Preceramic settlements in the Americas, it encompasses over 58 hectares of land [1].'' -> ''Approximately two kilometers inland on the Rio Chillón flood plain, El Paraiso is surrounded by over 90 hectares of arable land and 150 hectares of lowlands capable of sustaining irrigation supported agriculture [1]. In 1965, Engle identified seven major structures and designated them Units I-VII [1]. The structure referred to as Unit I is located at the southern extremity of the site [1]. It is built of rough trimmed stones quarried from a nearby hill side, assembled with coarse fill and mortar, and then covered in plaster [1]. The unit consists of four tiered levels [1]. Unique engaged columns were found in two of the walls of one of the smaller rooms in the northwest quadrant of Unit II [1]. Analysis of faunal remains showed that fish was the primary source of protein with anchovy as the most common fish [1]. The diet of the inhabitants of El Paraíso consisted of a mix of domesticated and gathered resources [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
