    ('65050d8b-7a91-4121-8384-84e4b7cea557'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Monuments', 'Megalithic', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:65050d8b-7a91-4121-8384-84e4b7cea557:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Megalithic'' restored on Amphitheatre of the Three Gauls', 'a8b9fd354394a6b02a12ecee8a8b9a1d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Monuments from site_type ''Amphitheatre'' is in group ''Monuments''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28532", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0155:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Theatre''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35802", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: site_type ''Theatre'' -> ''Megalithic structures''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37667", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-wrong-both: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Amphitheatre''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44455", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0094:chunk-0001: description ''The Amphitheatre of the Three Gauls was a Roman arena in Lugdunum (modern Lyon), France, part of the Sanctuary of the Three Gauls dedicated to the cult of Rome and Augustus[1]. It was built at the confluence of the Rhône and Saône rivers, where the 60 Gallic tribes gathered at Lugdunum[1]. In 1961, the site was classified as a monument historique[1].'' -> ''The Amphitheatre of the Three Gauls of Lugdunum (Lyon) was part of the Sanctuary of the Three Gauls dedicated to the cult of Rome and Augustus celebrated by the 60 Gallic tribes when they gathered at Lugdunum [1]. The amphitheatre was built at the foot of the La Croix-Rousse hill at what was then the confluence of the Rhône and Saône  [1]. An inscription on one of the blocks found in 1957 connects the amphitheatre with the sanctuary of Rome and Augustus and allows its origins to be identified [1]. The amphitheatre was expanded at the start of the 2nd century, according to J. Guey by C. Julius Celse [1]. The first excavations between 1818 and 1820 revealed the perimeter of the arena before re-covering it [1]. From 1956 serious excavations were begun, followed by 1966/67, 1971/72 and 1976/78 campaigns [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
