racteristics that distinguishes Tamtoc is the remarkable feminine presence [1]. To date, 90% of the burials discovered have been women [1]. In addition, women are depicted in most of the clay and ceramic figurines found here and are thought to have held a high rank in the social divisions of the community [1]. The ancient city of Tamtoc settled on the Tamuín plain, in the meander of the Tampaón River [1]. Until now, at least three periods of occupation have been identified: the oldest occurred around 600 BC, which corresponds to the time of the emergence of the city [1]. More than seventy structures have been discovered at the archaeological site [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('68677772-b461-49a3-bf2b-376e2b0e970b'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:68677772-b461-49a3-bf2b-376e2b0e970b:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Lake Mungo', '4a6c354a1f6b0a0648dd27d107e401d7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32719", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0001:chunk-0001: description ''Lake Mungo is a dry lake in New South Wales, Australia, approximately 760 km west of Sydney and 90 km northeast of Mildura[1]. It is the central feature of Mungo National Park and one of seventeen lakes in the World Heritage-listed Willandra Lakes Region[1]. The site is famous for the discovery of Mungo Man, the oldest human remains found in Australia, and Mungo Woman[1]. It also marks the location of the Lake Mungo geomagnetic excursion, the first convincing evidence that geomagnetic excursions are a geomagnetic phenomenon rather than sedimentological[1].'' -> ''Lake Mungo is a dry lake located in New South Wales [1]. It is about 760 km due west of Sydney and 90 km north-east of Mildura [1]. Sediments at Lake Mungo have been deposited over more than 120,000 years [1]. On the eastern side of the Mungo lake bed are the \"Walls of China,\" a series of crescent-shaped sand dunes or lunettes, up to 40 m in height, that stretch for more than 33 km, where most archaeological material has been found [1]. The most publicized findings at Lake Mungo have been Mungo Man and Mungo Woman [1]. Mungo Woman was only partially cremated before the remainder of her bones were crushed [1]. The first evidence of human occupation in Lake Mungo was revealed after an excavation of two Lake Mungo trenches contained 11 silcrete flakes [1]. Fish otoliths have been recovered from hearths in Lake Mungo [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
