    ('6d1ada8b-dd0e-4f0b-a586-1e5ac33ba9dc'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Monuments', 'Megalithic', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:6d1ada8b-dd0e-4f0b-a586-1e5ac33ba9dc:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Megalithic'' restored on Amphitheatre of Pompeii', 'f135e01a6024c7c220e5f2c8d045107a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Monuments from site_type ''Amphitheatre'' is in group ''Monuments''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28270", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0103:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Amphitheatre''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44489", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0095:chunk-0001: description ''One of the oldest surviving Roman stone amphitheatres, built around 70 BC with private funds from Gaius Quinctius Valgus and Marcus Porcius shortly after Pompeii became a Roman colony. The arena held approximately 20,000 spectators. In 59 AD, a notorious riot between Pompeians and Nocerians during a gladiatorial show caused numerous deaths, prompting the Roman Senate to ban events there for a decade. The amphitheatre was buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 AD, preserving it remarkably intact beneath volcanic ash.'' -> \"The Amphitheatre of Pompeii is one of the oldest surviving Roman amphitheatres [1]. It is located in the ancient city of Pompeii, near Naples, and was buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 AD, along with the city of Pompeii and the neighbouring town of Herculaneum [1]. Built around 70 BC, the amphitheatre is one of the earliest Roman amphitheatres built of stone; previously, they had been built out of wood [1]. The amphitheatre was designed to host around 20,000 people [1]. One of the most notable events in the amphitheatre''s history occurred around 59 AD, when a deadly brawl occurred between Pompeiians and residents of Nuceria during games in the amphitheatre, resulting in a 10-year ban on such events there [1]. The amphitheatre was damaged by an earthquake in 62 AD [1]. The amphitheatre was first partially excavated in 1748 [1]. It was later completely excavated between 1813 and 1816 [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
