    ('6fc1cbb2-3926-4ef2-b59f-0e3e4337fd45'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:6fc1cbb2-3926-4ef2-b59f-0e3e4337fd45:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Amman Citadel', '3cbf179e9eab0017746cf1f0cda7d712', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 45 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43537", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0075:chunk-0001: description \"The Amman Citadel (Jabal al-Qal''a) is an archaeological acropolis in central Amman, Jordan, occupied since the Neolithic period and fortified during the Bronze Age around 1800 BC [1][2]. The site was later developed by the Ammonites, Romans, Byzantines, and Umayyads [1][2]. Major visible remains include the massive Temple of Hercules, a Byzantine church, and the Umayyad Palace complex [1]. The Jordan Archaeological Museum, built on the hill in 1951 CE, houses artifacts from the site''s long occupation history [1]. The name ''Amman'' derives from ''Rabbath Ammon,'' the ancient capital of the Ammonites who settled the region after 1200 BC [2].\" -> \"The Amman Citadel is considered to be among the world''s oldest continuously inhabited places [1]. While archaeological discoveries have been made at many sites within modern Amman, Citadel Hill still holds particularly high potential [1]. The Great Temple, better but inaccurately known as the Temple of Hercules, is thought to be the most significant Roman structure within the Amman Citadel [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
