. The EES came to the conclusion that it was built during the reign of Seti I, as detailed in its report published in 1933 [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('7356fc90-8d0c-4554-b51d-f6b65c926244'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:7356fc90-8d0c-4554-b51d-f6b65c926244:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Murujuga', '855375be0f587ccdaef06f93a9e91131', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -40000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34947", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0037:chunk-0001: description \"Murujuga, also known as the Burrup Peninsula, is located in the Dampier Archipelago of Western Australia''s Pilbara region and contains the world''s largest and most significant collection of petroglyphs, estimated at over one million images spanning up to 50,000 years. The rock art includes depictions of the extinct thylacine and documents an unbroken record of human existence through the last ice age. Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2025, the cultural landscape is custodially managed by the Ngarluma, Yindjibarndi, Yaburara, Mardudhunera, and Wong-Goo-Tt-Oo peoples. Industrial development since 1963 has destroyed an estimated 24% of rock art on the peninsula.\" -> ''The Burrup Peninsula, previously known as Dampier Island, is a former island of the Dampier Archipelago that is now connected to the mainland via a causeway [1]. The peninsula and islands together are also known as Murujuga [1]. The peninsula is located in the Pilbara region of Western Australia and contains the town of Dampier as well as the Murujuga National Park [1]. The traditional owners of Murujuga are an Aboriginal nation known as the Yaburara people [1]. Some of the Aboriginal rock carvings have been dated to more than 45,000 years old, and some of the collection is generally estimated to be 40,000–50,000 years old [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
