    ('7895564a-2414-4bd5-a9c7-61e611f9159a'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Monuments', 'Settlements', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:7895564a-2414-4bd5-a9c7-61e611f9159a:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Settlements'' restored on Lees Hall Roman Camp', 'f6eb8226950fdd36e8bc0bffffe4e5cb', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Monuments from site_type ''Archaeological site'' is in group ''Monuments''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28833", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0200:chunk-0001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:34979", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0038:chunk-0001: description \"Lees Hall Roman Camp is a Roman temporary camp near Hadrian''s Wall in Northumberland, approximately 1 km south of the Wall and 680 metres south of the Vallum. One of over 40 temporary camps recorded near the Wall, it is uniquely distinguished by an outwork surrounding the main defences, suggesting prolonged occupation of at least one season rather than a short-lived marching camp. The site survives as earthworks with ramparts averaging 0.3 metres high and external ditches up to 0.4 metres deep. It may have been a predecessor to the nearby fortlet guarding the Haltwhistle Burn crossing, known to have been unoccupied before 105 AD.\" -> \"Lees Hall Roman Camp is an archaeological site, a Roman camp near Hadrian''s Wall, in Northumberland, England [1]. It is near Haltwhistle and adjacent to the B6318 road [1]. The camp is one of over 40 temporary Roman camps recorded near Hadrian''s Wall; they may have been marching camps, or were occupied by troops constructing the wall, or were used during military training [1]. This camp is unusual in having an outwork surrounding the main defences, suggesting that it was used for more than one campaign season [1]. The camp, a scheduled monument, is about 1 kilometre south of Hadrian''s Wall, and is 680 metres south of the Vallum [1]. There are extensive views to the north and east; a small stream lies within the fort [1]. It survives as earthworks and ditches; the rampart has an average height of 0.3 metres with an external ditch up to 0.4 metres deep [1]. It is rectangular, measuring 203 metres west to east by 140 metres north to south, enclosing an area of 1.7 hectares [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
