    ('7be75434-ab13-4f9b-883d-3897bb86cb47'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:7be75434-ab13-4f9b-883d-3897bb86cb47:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Heraion of Samos', '209c6f7580c00b0163d00668fcd73d83', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -1500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35007", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0039:chunk-0001: description \"The Heraion of Samos was a massive sanctuary dedicated to the goddess Hera on the island of Samos, situated 6 km southwest of the ancient city in the marshy Imbrasos river basin where it meets the sea [1]. The sanctuary''s late Archaic temple (c. 530 BC) was the first of the gigantic free-standing Ionic temples, pioneering a new architectural scale in Greek religious structures [1]. Cult activities at the site date to c. 1700 BC, making it one of the earliest continuous worship sites in the Greek world [1]. The ruins, along with the nearby Pythagoreion, were designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1992 [1].\" -> ''The Heraion of Samos was a large sanctuary to the goddess Hera, on the island of Samos, Greece, 6 km southwest of the ancient city of Samos [1]. It was located in the low, marshy basin of the Imbrasos river, near where it enters the sea [1]. The late Archaic temple in the sanctuary was the first of the gigantic free-standing Ionic temples, but its predecessors at this site reached back to the Geometric Period of the 8th century BC, or earlier, and there is evidence of cult activities on the site from c. 1700 BC onwards [1]. The Sacred Way was a road running from the city of Samos to the sanctuary, which was first laid out around 600 BC [1]. A large number of monumental statues and statuary groups were dedicated in the sanctuary, mainly in the sixth century BC [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
