    ('7bfb0158-bf33-40c2-aff0-3af9efcd5193'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:7bfb0158-bf33-40c2-aff0-3af9efcd5193:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Caves and Ice Age Art in the Swabian Jura', 'aeba3829f44728cb13770386bca27745', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35009", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0039:chunk-0001: description ''The Caves and Ice Age Art in the Swabian Jura are a collection of six caves in southern Germany used by Ice Age humans for shelter approximately 33,000 to 43,000 years ago [1]. These caves contain the oldest known non-stationary works of human art, including carved animal and humanoid figurines, the oldest musical instruments ever discovered, and a female statuette [1]. Inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2017, these caves provide crucial testimony to the development of Paleolithic art and culture in Europe [1].'' -> ''The Caves and Ice Age Art in the Swabian Jura are a collection of six caves in southern Germany which were used by Ice Age humans for shelter about 33,000 to 43,000 years ago [1]. Within the caves were found the oldest non-stationary works of human art yet discovered, in the form of carved animal and humanoid figurines, in addition to the oldest musical instruments ever found [1]. Some of the figurines depict creatures that are half animal, half human [1]. Bone flutes from the Geissenklösterle cave, dated around c.\\u200943,150–39,370 BP, are the oldest musical instruments ever found [1]. The 41,000 to 39,000-year-old Lion-man and the 42,000 to 41,000-year-old Venus of Hohle Fels are the oldest confirmed sculptures in the world [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
