    ('7cf17ac2-f8e5-43a4-a31d-3c89370c7cea'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:7cf17ac2-f8e5-43a4-a31d-3c89370c7cea:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Woodbury Hill, Dorset', 'a1992df22e884d798f4e37a3ffa8dbc1', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 45 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35019", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0039:chunk-0001: description ''Woodbury Hill is an Iron Age hillfort near Bere Regis in Dorset, England [1]. The site was also the location of an annual fair from medieval times until the mid-20th century [1].'' -> ''Woodbury Hill is a hill near Bere Regis in Dorset, England [1]. It is the site of an Iron Age hillfort, and the location of an annual fair from medieval times until the mid 20th century [1]. The hill overlooks the Winterbourne Valley to the north and the Piddle Valley to the south; immediately to the west is Bere Stream [1]. The irregularly-shaped hillfort has an area of about 5 hectares (12 acres) [1]. It is about 272 metres (892 ft) west to east and 410 metres (1,350 ft) north to south, and is enclosed by a single rampart of width 6 to 12 metres (20 to 39 ft); outside this is a ditch of width 9 metres (30 ft) and a counterscarp [1]. The ground outside the defences falls steeply to the east, south and south-west [1]. To the north, where there is a gently sloping strip of land leading to a northern ridge, a second rampart about 60 metres (200 ft) north of the main rampart provides additional defence [1]. There is an original entrance on the south-west, through which a track leads into the fort, and an original entrance on the north-east; other breaks in the defences are more recent [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
