    ('7d45e357-77ff-40f6-a4b4-7e16e015996a'::uuid, 'civilization', 'Georgia', 'Georgia (country)', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:7d45e357-77ff-40f6-a4b4-7e16e015996a:civilization', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: civilization ''Georgia (country)'' restored on Anacopia Fortress', 'c9cf098df8f0d3cde4293fe84ae661cc', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "civilization = Georgia from country ''Georgia''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27262", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-21_mechanical-country: country ''Georgia (country)'' -> ''Georgia''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35053", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0040:chunk-0001: description ''Anacopia Fortress is an ancient military citadel in New Athos in the disputed Abkhazia region of Georgia, located 22 km along the coast from Sukhumi. The site measures approximately 450 × 150 meters and sits 1.6 km inland atop Iverian Mountain. It represents the most complete surviving structure of ancient Anacopia, which served as the capital of the Kingdom of Abkhazia [1].'' -> \"Anakopia Fortress is an ancient military citadel in New Athos in the disputed Abkhazia, Georgia located some 22 km by road along the coast from Sukhumi [1]. The site, approximately 450 × 150 meters in dimensions, is located a mile or so inland, at the top of the Iverian Mountain [1]. It is the most complete surviving building of ancient Anakopia, the former capital of the Kingdom of Abkhazia [1]. A military structure was constructed here between the second and fourth centuries [1]. At the end of the seventh century, walls were constructed around the site of the citadel, with support from the Byzantines who had become alarmed by the Islamic Caliphate''s expansionism [1]. The walls, up to 60 centimeters thick, are constructed of largely tightly assembled and carefully hewn limestone blocks [1]. At the centre lies a Roman-styled tower, four storeys high, with excellent views in all directions across the surrounding landscape and, to the south-west of the fortress, the Black Sea [1]. A small Christian basilica was also constructed in the centre of the fortress during or before the eighth century [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('7d7e62e6-943f-4a2d-b5fa-8afd589ccf80'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:7d7e62e6-943f-4a2d-b5fa-8afd589ccf80:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Castro of Chibanes', 'b52d113f570841ab50f3eb8aab997e35', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 45 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44555", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0097:chunk-0001: description \"The Castro of Chibanes is a fortified hilltop settlement in the Arrábida Nature Park, on Serra do Louro in Palmela municipality, Setúbal District, Portugal, approximately 25 km south of Lisbon [1]. First occupied around 2900 BCE during the Chalcolithic (Copper Age), the site covers roughly one hectare and occupies the hilltop, with natural protection provided by a steep scarp to the south [1]. Archaeological excavations conducted between 2012 and 2017 documented the site''s occupation history [2]. The site is legally protected as a Zona Especial de Protecção and classified as a Sitio de Interesse Público [1].\" -> ''The Castro of Chibanes is located in the Arrábida Nature Park, on the Serra do Louro (Louro Hill), in the municipality of Palmela in the Setúbal District of Portugal, about 25 km south of the capital of Lisbon [1]. The Castro, or hill fort, is located at the top of the hill, naturally defended by a scarp to the south [1]. It covers about one hectare and was first occupied in about 2900 BCE, during the Chalcolithic or Copper Age [1]. The site was identified and first excavated by António Inácio Marques da Costa in 1906 [1]. The first phase of development took place from the Copper Age until the early Bronze Age (about 2000 BC) [1]. The occupants practised agriculture and livestock rearing, although hunting and gathering of molluscs from the nearby sea were also carried out [1]. Remains of fauna discovered from the Chalcolithic included bones of the European rabbit [1]. Butchery marks on the bones suggest the animals were used for food [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
