    ('81ee931d-9549-4cdb-a508-d06ae8971ba4'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Fortifications', 'Settlements', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:81ee931d-9549-4cdb-a508-d06ae8971ba4:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Settlements'' restored on Milseburg', '19284e042a9d4e15119f3677da7a8fb5', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Fortifications from site_type ''Fortification'' is in group ''Fortifications''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28996", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0223:chunk-0001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Fortification''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44733", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0098:chunk-0001: description \"Milseburg is an extinct volcano rising 835 metres (2,740 ft) in the Hessian Rhön Mountains, making it the region''s second-highest peak[1]. The hill, located east of Fulda near Kleinsassen and Danzwiesen, contains the remains of a major Celtic oppidum dating to 500–1500 BC[1][2]. This Iron Age fortified settlement ranks among the largest in central Germany[2]. Today the mountain is a popular hiking and biking destination[1]. The summit also features a chapel dedicated to St Gangulphus[1].\" -> ''The Milseburg is an extinct volcano and at 835 metres above sea level the second highest elevation in the Hessian part of the Rhön Mountains, Germany [1]. The hill is located east of Fulda [1]. The Milseburg is most notable for having been the site of an important Celtic oppidum [1]. It was likely occupied from around 1200 to 800 BC and again from 450 BC to around the turn of the eras [1]. The Ringwall (circular rampart) was one of the largest and most elevated in eastern Hesse [1]. It originally had three gates, only one of which — the Tor am Kälberhutstein — was passable by wagons [1]. The main fortification was a 1,300 metres long stone wall that surrounded the north, east and south sides of the hill at its base [1]. It was discovered as ancient in origin by R. Virchow (1821–1902) [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
