ne of the largest and most elevated in eastern Hesse [1]. It originally had three gates, only one of which — the Tor am Kälberhutstein — was passable by wagons [1]. The main fortification was a 1,300 metres long stone wall that surrounded the north, east and south sides of the hill at its base [1]. It was discovered as ancient in origin by R. Virchow (1821–1902) [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8230ee02-ce60-4b34-b0cd-3beca70274fb'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:8230ee02-ce60-4b34-b0cd-3beca70274fb:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''5'' restored on Dolni Glavanak Cromlech', 'b3e9d42613905774ddf438bde9506c4b', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic stones'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 24 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29504", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0299:chunk-0001: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-800''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35089", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0041:chunk-0001: description ''The Dolni Glavanak Cromlech is an oval stone circle in the Eastern Rhodope Mountains, 2 km west of Dolni Glavanak village, Bulgaria -- the best-preserved cromlech in the country and the only known structure of its kind there. Dated to the Early Iron Age (8th-6th centuries BC), it consists of 15 roughly trapezoidal blocks of local rhyodacite volcanic rock, approximately 1.2 m high, forming a circle about 10 m in diameter with a southeast entrance. Excavated by Georgi Nehrizov in 1998-1999, finds included decorated pottery and bronze artifacts. Two nearby oval enclosures yielded burnt human bone, suggesting funereal use.'' -> ''The Dolni Glavanak Cromlech is an oval stone circle located 2 kilometres west of village of Dolni Glavanak, some 12 kilometres from town of Madzharovo, in Bulgaria [1]. The site is situated on a low ridge-top above the left bank of the Arda river [1]. The circle consists of vertical, roughly-shaped, blocks of local hard volcanic rock (rhyodacite) [1]. Nine blocks still stand and three are now fallen [1]. The site was discovered and excavated by Georgi Nehrizov in around 1998–1999 [1]. The excavations uncovered decorated pottery, as well as bronze artefacts (a fibula and a pin) [1]. These date the construction of the cromlech to the second phase of the Early Iron Age (8th–6th centuries BC) [1]. The site continued in use into the late Iron Age [1].''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35857", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-800'' -> ''-1500''"}]'::jsonb),
