    ('82f23c96-d8b2-41f0-a3e5-59ae9b89880c'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:82f23c96-d8b2-41f0-a3e5-59ae9b89880c:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Arc de Berà', 'd4a1be65cf6cb0e8d6dd1e34dc7d6e0b', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32721", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0001:chunk-0001: description ''The Arc de Bera is a Roman triumphal arch erected around 13 BC during the reign of Augustus, located 20 km northeast of Tarragona, Catalonia, Spain. Built on the Via Augusta through the will of Lucius Licinius Sura, it features a single opening with fluted pilasters crowned by Corinthian capitals atop a podium. The arch likely marked the boundary of the Tarraco district. It forms part of the Archaeological Ensemble of Tarraco, inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2000.'' -> ''The Arc de Berà (sometimes erroneously written Barà) is a triumphal arch some 20 km north-east of the city of Tarragona, Spain, close to Roda de Berà [1]. This monument is part of the Archaeological Ensemble of Tarraco [1]. It stands on the line of what was the Via Augusta, now the N-340 road [1]. Its name derives from the count Berà [1]. It is a triumphal arch with a single opening consisting of a central body on a podium, decorated with fluted pilasters crowned by Corinthian capitals [1]. The upper part of the construction is an entablature made up of architrave, frieze and cornice [1]. The monument was erected in the reign of Augustus, around 13 BC [1]. It was later restored at the beginning of the 2nd century AD pursuant to terms of the will of Lucius Licinius Sura [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
