rected in the reign of Augustus, around 13 BC [1]. It was later restored at the beginning of the 2nd century AD pursuant to terms of the will of Lucius Licinius Sura [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('82f2d8e5-e3fa-4782-b580-a7eca3ecad45'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:82f2d8e5-e3fa-4782-b580-a7eca3ecad45:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Main Limes', '1479b8653d954584e97e0efd6a746d86', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period 1..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35091", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0041:chunk-0001: description ''The Main Limes (Nasser Limes) was established around 90 AD as part of the Upper Germanic-Rhaetian Limes, forming the Roman frontier along approximately 50 km of the River Main between Grosskrotzenburg and Burgstadt. The river served as a natural boundary, eliminating the need for the palisades and ditches used elsewhere on the limes. Security relied on individual watchtowers and nine or ten forts at locations including Stockstadt, Obernburg, Trennfurt, and Miltenberg. The broader Upper Germanic-Rhaetian Limes was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2005.'' -> ''The Main Limes, also called the Nasser Limes, was built around 90 AD and, as part of the Upper Germanic-Rhaetian Limes, formed the frontier of the Roman Empire in the area between the present day villages of Großkrotzenburg and Bürgstadt [1]. In this section the limes adjoined the River Main (Moenus), which forms a natural boundary for about 50 kilometres here, so \"Main\" refers to the river [1]. However, of the many watchtowers that probably stood along the Main, to date only one south of Obernburg am Main has been identified [1]. North of the Main the limes initially runs through the marshy terrain of the Schifflache and Bulau before linking up with the Wetterau Limes [1]. At the crossing of the Main at Großkrotzenburg a Roman bridge has been identified from post sockets [1]. In the south it extended in its early period to Obernburg or Wörth [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
