    ('87dcefc2-025d-458b-9418-16e4bac7d17f'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:87dcefc2-025d-458b-9418-16e4bac7d17f:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Hod Hill', '9c067f5dac644c713370c6cbf02e8af7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 45 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36773", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0042:chunk-0001: description ''Hod Hill is one of the largest Iron Age hillforts in Dorset, England, enclosing 22 hectares on a 143-metre chalk hill overlooking the River Stour. Fortified no later than the 5th century BC by the Durotriges tribe, the interior contained over 200 round houses with bivallate defences featuring ramparts up to 4.5 metres high. Following the Roman invasion in 43 AD, the Second Augustan Legion under future emperor Vespasian captured the site, leaving numerous ballista bolts as evidence of the assault. The Romans built a 4.4-hectare fort in the northwest corner, housing approximately 500 legionaries and 250 cavalry until its abandonment around 51 AD. The site was partially excavated by Ian Richmond between 1951 and 1958.'' -> ''Hod Hill is a large hill fort in the Blackmore Vale, 3 miles north-west of Blandford Forum, Dorset, England [1]. The fort sits on a 143 m chalk hill of the same name that lies between the adjacent Dorset Downs and Cranborne Chase [1]. The fort is roughly rectangular, with an enclosed area of 22 ha [1]. There is a steep natural slope down to the River Stour to the west, the other sides have an artificial rampart, ditch and counterscarp [1]. The hillfort was inhabited by the Durotriges in the late Iron Age; whether this is the same tribe who fortified the hilltop in the middle Iron Age (radiocarbon analysis suggests a date of 500 BC for the main rampart) is unknown [1]. The Romans built a camp in the north-west corner of the original fort, possibly occupied by a mixed force of legionaries and auxiliaries [1]. The fort was used as a base for about 5 or 6 years, but passed out of use by about AD 50, when troops were withdrawn, after a fire, for the campaigns against Caractacus in Wales [1]. The site was excavated in the 1950s by Sir Ian Richmond and his final report was published in 1969 [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
