    ('8958a97d-425b-4f9c-a503-897815a44756'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:8958a97d-425b-4f9c-a503-897815a44756:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Castro of Vila Nova de São Pedro', '6d5d23545d5750d51d33711742277a61', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 45 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44771", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0099:chunk-0001: description ''The Castro of Vila Nova de São Pedro is a Chalcolithic fortified settlement in Azambuja, Portugal, dating to 2600-1300 BCE [1]. The site is renowned for the discovery of thousands of arrowheads within its defensive walls [1]. It was a contemporary of the major southeastern Spanish settlements of Los Millares and El Argar [1]. The settlement is associated with the castro of Zambujal near Torres Vedras, one of the most important fortified towns of the Portuguese Chalcolithic period [1].'' -> ''The Castro of Vila Nova de São Pedro is a Chalcolithic archaeological site in the civil parish of Vila Nova de São Pedro, municipality of Azambuja [1]. It is important for the discovery of thousands of arrowheads within its fortified settlement, associated with the Chalcolithic period of human settlement, and in particular with the long-lived fortified town, or castro, of Zambujal [1]. Within its perimeter are the vestiges of a semi-circular domed oven/kiln, alongside a limestone space and cistern [1]. The oven and ancillary space constitutes a group associated with the industrial fabrication of ceramics, receiving water from the cistern for its operation [1]. The site was discovered in 1936 by Raposo [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
