omb built in c. 1250 BC [1]. The Tomb of Clytemnestra with its imposing façade is together with the Treasury of Atreus the most monumental tomb of that type [1]. The entrance to the tomb consists of a dromos with walls of ashlar in conglomerate [1]. The exterior façade of the stomion is decorated with half-columns made of gypsum which were carefully carved with ornate capitals and vertical fluting [1]. A large relieving triangle overlaps the door lintel [1]. It was originally decorated by a colored relief sculpture [1]. Through the lintel block a small drain is cut in the rock, lined with low rubble walls and roofed with small slabs [1]. During the Ottoman rule of Greece the tomb was raided by Veli Pasha [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8a3a0f0e-6656-4b50-92ff-7f02f9f1548e'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:8a3a0f0e-6656-4b50-92ff-7f02f9f1548e:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Kong Asgers Høj', 'a982ea9247f46d111f85d173a68f046a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Necropolis/tombs complex'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 47 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36801", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0043:chunk-0001: description \"Kong Asgers Høj is Denmark''s largest passage grave, located on the island of Mon and dating to the Neolithic Funnel Beaker culture around 3000 BC. The megalithic structure consists of a burial chamber 10 meters long and 2 meters wide, accessed through a narrow passage approximately 10 meters in length. Built from boulders weighing up to four tonnes each, the grave functioned as a communal burial site that was repeatedly opened for new interments and later reused for Corded Ware culture burials. Archaeological investigation began in 1839 when merchant Gustav Hage attempted to find treasure within.\" -> ''Kong Asgers Høj is a large passage grave on the island of Møn in Denmark [1]. The megalithic structure dated to Neolithic Funnel Beaker culture [1]. The grave consists of a chamber (10 m long by 2 m wide) with a long passage (7.5 m long) [1]. Study of King Asger Høj began in 1839, when the Danish merchant Gustav Hage tried to find a treasure but found it empty [1]. The grave is structurally untouched since ancient times, but may have been cleared then [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
