    ('8a4ea255-abed-47fe-9c37-636756e90450'::uuid, 'antiquity', '4', '6', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:8a4ea255-abed-47fe-9c37-636756e90450:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: antiquity ''6'' restored on Amphipolis', 'd99fcc91d0bc8f4c2823dea1f4848fa3', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 4 from period_start -437"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28887", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0210:chunk-0001: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-437''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30448", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36803", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0043:chunk-0001: description ''Amphipolis (Greek: Αμφίπολη) was an ancient Greek polis and later Roman city in Macedonia, Greece, founded as an Athenian colony in 438/437 BC on the strategic Strymon River near valuable gold mines at Pangaion [1][2]. The city controlled key trade routes between Thrace and the Aegean, making it a major military prize—it was the site of a pivotal battle between Sparta and Athens in 422 BC, and later Alexander the Great used it as a staging point for his Asian campaigns [1]. Today, extensive archaeological remains survive, with finds displayed in the Archaeological Museum of Amphipolis and Archaeological Museum of Kavala [2].'' -> ''Amphipolis was an important ancient Greek polis, and later a Roman city, whose large remains can still be seen [1]. Amphipolis was originally a colony of ancient Athens and was the site of the battle between the Spartans and Athenians in 422 BC [1]. It was later the place where Alexander the Great prepared for campaigns leading to his invasion of Asia in 335 BC [1]. Excavations in and around the city have revealed important buildings, ancient walls and tombs [1]. The Athenians founded a first colony at Ennea Hodoi in 465 BC, but the first ten thousand colonists were massacred by the Thracians [1]. A second attempt took place in 437 BC on the same site under general Hagnon which was successful [1]. The city and its first impressive and elaborately built walls of 7.5 km length date from this time [1]. After the final victory of Rome over Macedonia in the Battle of Pydna in 168 BC, Amphipolis became the capital of one of the four mini-republics, or merides [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8a4ea255-abed-47fe-9c37-636756e90450'::uuid, 'total_power', '21', '23', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:8a4ea255-abed-47fe-9c37-636756e90450:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: total_power ''23'' restored on Amphipolis', 'd99fcc91d0bc8f4c2823dea1f4848fa3', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 21 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 4 + 5 + 10 + 1 + 1"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28887", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0210:chunk-0001: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-437''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30448", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36803", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0043:chunk-0001: description ''Amphipolis (Greek: Αμφίπολη) was an ancient Greek polis and later Roman city in Macedonia, Greece, founded as an Athenian colony in 438/437 BC on the strategic Strymon River near valuable gold mines at Pangaion [1][2]. The city controlled key trade routes between Thrace and the Aegean, making it a major military prize—it was the site of a pivotal battle between Sparta and Athens in 422 BC, and later Alexander the Great used it as a staging point for his Asian campaigns [1]. Today, extensive archaeological remains survive, with finds displayed in the Archaeological Museum of Amphipolis and Archaeological Museum of Kavala [2].'' -> ''Amphipolis was an important ancient Greek polis, and later a Roman city, whose large remains can still be seen [1]. Amphipolis was originally a colony of ancient Athens and was the site of the battle between the Spartans and Athenians in 422 BC [1]. It was later the place where Alexander the Great prepared for campaigns leading to his invasion of Asia in 335 BC [1]. Excavations in and around the city have revealed important buildings, ancient walls and tombs [1]. The Athenians founded a first colony at Ennea Hodoi in 465 BC, but the first ten thousand colonists were massacred by the Thracians [1]. A second attempt took place in 437 BC on the same site under general Hagnon which was successful [1]. The city and its first impressive and elaborately built walls of 7.5 km length date from this time [1]. After the final victory of Rome over Macedonia in the Battle of Pydna in 168 BC, Amphipolis became the capital of one of the four mini-republics, or merides [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8a66f7db-94df-45d9-919f-760b966192be'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:8a66f7db-94df-45d9-919f-760b966192be:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Gunung Padang Megalithic Site', 'cf59e79a82e9ec338e7baf0207e339af', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic structures'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 22 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36805", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0043:chunk-0001: description \"Indonesia''s largest megalithic site, located on an extinct volcano in West Java with five terraces, stone retaining walls, and roughly 370 steps built from andesite columnar joints. First recorded by Dutch colonizers in 1914, rediscovered by farmers in 1979, and declared a National Site in 2014. Archaeologist Lutfi Yondri''s 30-year excavation program dated charcoal samples to around 50-117 BCE, suggesting construction roughly 2,000 years ago. Claims of 20,000+ year dating by geologist Natawidjaja were retracted in 2024 after samples were found to be natural soil, not anthropogenic.\" -> ''Gunung Padang is an archaeological site located in Karyamukti, West Java, Indonesia, 50 kilometres southwest of Cianjur [1]. Located at 885 metres above sea level, the site covers a hill—an extinct volcano—in a series of five terraces bordered by retaining walls of stone that are accessed by 370 successive andesite steps rising about 95 metres [1]. It is covered with massive hexagonal stone columns of volcanic origin [1]. Gunung Padang consists of a series of five artificial terraces—one rectangular and four trapezoidal—that occur, in succession, at progressively higher elevations [1]. The terraces are aligned along a central longitudinal NW–SE axis [1]. They are artificial platforms created by lowering elevated areas and filling in depressions until a flat surface was achieved [1]. The perimeters of the terraces feature retaining walls constructed from volcanic polygonal columns [1]. Pottery fragments found at the site were dated by the bureau of archaeology in the range 45 BCE–22 CE [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8a66f7db-94df-45d9-919f-760b966192be'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '40', '34', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:8a66f7db-94df-45d9-919f-760b966192be:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: rarity_score ''34'' restored on Gunung Padang Megalithic Site', 'cf59e79a82e9ec338e7baf0207e339af', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 40 from mystery 6, cultural_influence 10"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36805", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0043:chunk-0001: description \"Indonesia''s largest megalithic site, located on an extinct volcano in West Java with five terraces, stone retaining walls, and roughly 370 steps built from andesite columnar joints. First recorded by Dutch colonizers in 1914, rediscovered by farmers in 1979, and declared a National Site in 2014. Archaeologist Lutfi Yondri''s 30-year excavation program dated charcoal samples to around 50-117 BCE, suggesting construction roughly 2,000 years ago. Claims of 20,000+ year dating by geologist Natawidjaja were retracted in 2024 after samples were found to be natural soil, not anthropogenic.\" -> ''Gunung Padang is an archaeological site located in Karyamukti, West Java, Indonesia, 50 kilometres southwest of Cianjur [1]. Located at 885 metres above sea level, the site covers a hill—an extinct volcano—in a series of five terraces bordered by retaining walls of stone that are accessed by 370 successive andesite steps rising about 95 metres [1]. It is covered with massive hexagonal stone columns of volcanic origin [1]. Gunung Padang consists of a series of five artificial terraces—one rectangular and four trapezoidal—that occur, in succession, at progressively higher elevations [1]. The terraces are aligned along a central longitudinal NW–SE axis [1]. They are artificial platforms created by lowering elevated areas and filling in depressions until a flat surface was achieved [1]. The perimeters of the terraces feature retaining walls constructed from volcanic polygonal columns [1]. Pottery fragments found at the site were dated by the bureau of archaeology in the range 45 BCE–22 CE [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8a66f7db-94df-45d9-919f-760b966192be'::uuid, 'total_power', '28', '26', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:8a66f7db-94df-45d9-919f-760b966192be:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: total_power ''26'' restored on Gunung Padang Megalithic Site', 'cf59e79a82e9ec338e7baf0207e339af', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 28 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 3 + 8 + 10 + 6 + 1"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36805", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0043:chunk-0001: description \"Indonesia''s largest megalithic site, located on an extinct volcano in West Java with five terraces, stone retaining walls, and roughly 370 steps built from andesite columnar joints. First recorded by Dutch colonizers in 1914, rediscovered by farmers in 1979, and declared a National Site in 2014. Archaeologist Lutfi Yondri''s 30-year excavation program dated charcoal samples to around 50-117 BCE, suggesting construction roughly 2,000 years ago. Claims of 20,000+ year dating by geologist Natawidjaja were retracted in 2024 after samples were found to be natural soil, not anthropogenic.\" -> ''Gunung Padang is an archaeological site located in Karyamukti, West Java, Indonesia, 50 kilometres southwest of Cianjur [1]. Located at 885 metres above sea level, the site covers a hill—an extinct volcano—in a series of five terraces bordered by retaining walls of stone that are accessed by 370 successive andesite steps rising about 95 metres [1]. It is covered with massive hexagonal stone columns of volcanic origin [1]. Gunung Padang consists of a series of five artificial terraces—one rectangular and four trapezoidal—that occur, in succession, at progressively higher elevations [1]. The terraces are aligned along a central longitudinal NW–SE axis [1]. They are artificial platforms created by lowering elevated areas and filling in depressions until a flat surface was achieved [1]. The perimeters of the terraces feature retaining walls constructed from volcanic polygonal columns [1]. Pottery fragments found at the site were dated by the bureau of archaeology in the range 45 BCE–22 CE [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
