    ('8f34775c-ca69-41e8-9517-35005cdc21b8'::uuid, 'civilization', 'Northern Ireland', 'Ireland', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:8f34775c-ca69-41e8-9517-35005cdc21b8:civilization', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: civilization ''Ireland'' restored on Aughlish', '34aefaaaa13eb04eb6e721f8a141a125', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "civilization = Northern Ireland from country ''Northern Ireland''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30801", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-uk-parts: country ''Ireland'' -> ''Northern Ireland''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36837", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0044:chunk-0001: description ''Bronze Age ritual complex in the Sperrin Mountains of County Londonderry, Northern Ireland, comprising at least six stone circles and two stone rows. The largest circle contains 41 small stones with a fallen 1.5 m stone at the south, while one alignment stretches for 18 metres. Aubrey Burl considers the site typical of Bronze Age ritual sites in the Sperrins, with the layout indicating connections to circle-building traditions in England, Scotland, and wider Ireland. A stone row aligns with the southernmost setting point of the Moon, suggesting a lunar observational purpose.'' -> ''Aughlish (also Auglish) is a townland and the site of at least six stone circles and two stone rows, in County Londonderry, Northern Ireland, 3.6 km from Feeny [1]. The site features a group of circles including one with 41 small stones and a fallen stone 150 cm high at the south, with another of the same height outside the circle to the north [1]. There are three other circles (or parts of circles) with alignments, one of which stretches for 18m [1]. Aubrey Burl considers the site to be typical of Irish Bronze Age ritual sites around the Sperrins, the local range of hills [1]. He argues that the layout of the stone circles and rows indicates connections with circle builders in England, Scotland and elsewhere in Northern Ireland [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8f8d1ce5-3527-4c35-925f-579e6dc51784'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:8f8d1ce5-3527-4c35-925f-579e6dc51784:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Pella', '6d5bd586899c03d46f0da998694de6db', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -7500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32791", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0006:chunk-0001: description \"Pella (Tabaqat Fahl) is an ancient city in the eastern foothills of the north Jordan Valley, continuously inhabited for over 6,000 years with the earliest settlers arriving around 7500 BC. First mentioned in 19th-century BCE Egyptian execration texts, it became part of the Decapolis league of Hellenistic cities from 63 BC. The Romans resettled Pella in the 2nd century AD and developed it into a thriving economic center. Archaeological remains include Middle and Late Bronze Age temples, a Roman theatre, baths, Byzantine churches, and an Islamic quarter with a mosque. The site is on Jordan''s UNESCO tentative list.\" -> \"Pella was an ancient city in what is now northwest Jordan, and contains ruins from the Neolithic, Chalcolithic, Bronze Age, Iron Age, Canaanite, Hellenistic and Islamic periods [1]. It is located near a rich water source within the eastern foothills of the Jordan Valley, close to the modern village of Ṭabaqat Faḥl some 27 km south of the Sea of Galilee [1]. The University of Sydney''s Pella Excavation Project discovered at Tabaqat Fahl the remains of Neolithic housing dated to ca. 6000 BCE [1]. The Australian teams also found storage complexes from the Chalcolithic period (ca. 4200 BCE) [1]. The Middle Bronze Age IIA city of ca. 1800 BCE boasted massive mud-brick city walls [1]. Australian archaeologists also discovered Middle and Late Bronze Age temples and palatial residences (ca. 1800-1200 BCE) [1]. The city was first mentioned in the 19th century BCE in Egyptian execration texts, and it continued to flourish throughout the Bronze Age [1]. By the Byzantine era, Pella had reached its maximum size and, probably, prosperity [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
