    ('936b5a30-e1dd-4524-95c2-e7214ecf3090'::uuid, 'total_power', '28', '26', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:936b5a30-e1dd-4524-95c2-e7214ecf3090:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: total_power ''26'' restored on Archaeological Site of Grand', 'c2994dc4a469e15dfdc7bd651b6cc32f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 28 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 3 + 8 + 10 + 6 + 1"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44823", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0100:chunk-0001: description \"Gallo-Roman sanctuary of Apollo Grannus, the healing god, in the Vosges department of northeastern France, identified with Andesina on the Peutinger Table. Its 1st-century amphitheatre seated 17,000 spectators, making it one of the largest in the Roman Empire. A 232-square-metre floor mosaic from an ancient basilica depicts four wild animals around a central theatrical scene from Menander''s Phasma. The town was enclosed by a rampart and developed as a major pilgrimage centre for healing rituals dedicated to Apollo Grannus.\" -> ''The archaeological site of Grand contains a wide amphitheater and a mosaic of 232 m2 [1]. It is located in the French department of Vosges, in the region Grand Est, in France [1]. It was an ancient Gallo-Roman city named Andésina (15,000-20,000 inhabitants) [1]. The amphitheater was built at the end of the 1st century and can contain 17,000 people [1]. At its center, the mosaic shows a theater scene of the Phasma of Menander and four animals at the four corners [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('941b3241-8cc0-408b-a1be-2ee5cd8ad6f7'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:941b3241-8cc0-408b-a1be-2ee5cd8ad6f7:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Kamyana Mohyla', 'e6ec01738031517ef8e43478083c4bb0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -3000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43567", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0076:chunk-0001: description \"Kamyana Mohyla is an archaeological site in the Molochna River valley, about 1.6 km from Terpinnia village in Zaporizhzhia Oblast, Ukraine[1]. The site features petroglyphs spanning from the Upper Paleolithic to the Medieval period, with Stone Age depictions being of greatest archaeological interest[1]. It comprises a group of isolated sandstone blocks forming a natural monument[1]. Kamyana Mohyla is listed on Ukraine''s Tentative List of World Heritage Sites[1].\" -> ''Kamyana Mohyla is an archaeological site in the Molochna River valley, about a mile from the village of Terpinnia, Zaporizhzhia Oblast, Ukraine [1]. The site encompasses a group of isolated blocks of sandstone, up to twelve meters in height, scattered around an area of some 3,000 sq m [1]. Petroglyphs are found only inside the caves and grottoes of Kamyana Mohyla, many of them still filled up with sand [1]. Faint traces of red paint remain on parts of the surface [1]. In 1889, the Russian archaeologist Nikolay Veselovsky was called upon to explore the enigmatic site and started excavations the following year [1]. As soon as he concluded that the site was a burial mound, excavations were terminated [1]. In the 1930s, the site was investigated by a team of scholars from Melitopol under Valentin Danylenko [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
