    ('952e9faf-49f9-4fe9-8cee-f0934167b9df'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:952e9faf-49f9-4fe9-8cee-f0934167b9df:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Axum', '704e22fad5c432169dfb9dba1bfdf4b7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44829", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0100:chunk-0001: description ''Capital of the Aksumite Empire, a major naval and trading power that controlled Red Sea commerce from the 1st to 8th centuries AD. The site contains exquisitely carved monolithic stelae from the 3rd-4th centuries, with the largest fallen stela measuring 33 m -- possibly the biggest monolith ever attempted to be erected. Christianity was adopted in the 4th century, and the Church of St. Mary of Zion is said to house the Ark of the Covenant. Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1980.'' -> \"Axum likely had a prosperous agricultural vicinity, evident from wheat depicted on Aksumite coins, abundant livestock, local forests supplying firewood for centuries, and various industries such as metalworking, glassware, and pottery [1]. The major Aksumite monuments in the town are steles [1]. These obelisks are around 1,700 years old and have become a symbol of the Ethiopian people''s identity [1]. The largest number are in the Northern Stelae Park, ranging up to the 33-metre-long Great Stele, believed to have fallen and broken during construction [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
