    ('964d2a78-6209-4f94-be74-761d59bfd0cc'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:964d2a78-6209-4f94-be74-761d59bfd0cc:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on Chûn Quoit', '458b1a449e30d066a91263fcd14c4c92', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Dolmen'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 16 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44831", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0100:chunk-0001: description ''Chûn Quoit is one of the best-preserved Neolithic quoits in western Cornwall [1]. The structure features a capstone supported by upright stones, creating a chamber typical of Neolithic funerary monuments in southwestern Britain.'' -> ''Chûn Quoit is one of the best preserved of all Neolithic quoits (also called dolmens or cromlechs) in western Cornwall, England, United Kingdom [1]. Chûn Quoit is located in open moorland near Pendeen and Morvah [1]. Standing on a ridge, near the much later constructed Chûn Castle hill fort, it overlooks heather moorland and the open sea [1]. Like the other quoits, the quoit was probably covered by a round barrow, of which much evidence abounds [1]. It was a closed chamber and its mushroom-domed capstone measures 3.3 m (11 ft) by 3 m (10 ft), with a maximum thickness of 0.8 m (2 ft 7 in) [1]. There is a cup mark on top of the capstone [1]. It is supported about 2 m (7 ft) from the ground by four substantial slabs [1]. The site was examined in 1871 but no significant finds were made [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
