    ('9737ca03-ad30-46d2-8740-3dec20bed1ea'::uuid, 'civilization', 'Georgia', 'Georgia (country)', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:9737ca03-ad30-46d2-8740-3dec20bed1ea:civilization', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: civilization ''Georgia (country)'' restored on Grakliani Hill', '994eaa616339633befe42508ad6f8101', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "civilization = Georgia from country ''Georgia''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27264", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-21_mechanical-country: country ''Georgia (country)'' -> ''Georgia''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36911", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0046:chunk-0001: description ''Grakliani Hill is a 7th-century BC temple complex in eastern Georgia near Kaspi, featuring a fertility goddess shrine with a disputed inscription that may predate other known regional scripts by over a millennium. Excavations revealed human presence potentially spanning 300,000 years [1].'' -> \"Grakliani Hill is an archaeological excavation site in eastern Georgia near Kaspi, showing evidence of human presence possibly going back 300,000 years [1]. The site was discovered in 2007, during work to widen the Tbilisi-Senaki-Leselidze highway [1]. The site contains a temple to a fertility goddess from the seventh century BCE, a pit-type burial cemetery from the early Bronze Age, and the remains of a building from around 450-350 BCE; the building consists of three rooms with three storage rooms [1]. The site had been occupied between the Chalcolithic and the Late Hellenistic periods [1]. The excavation of layers yielded artifacts including children''s toys, weapons, icons, and pharmacological devices [1]. Within the first two months of digging, archaeologists had excavated over 35,000 pieces from hundreds of graves and ruins of settlements that date back to the eighth century BCE [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
