    ('9789eadf-a133-43dc-9f8e-2d7f7818ecc2'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:9789eadf-a133-43dc-9f8e-2d7f7818ecc2:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Necropolis of Li Muri', '08df6be8784891752921cb788f138d4a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Necropolis/tombs complex'', ''4500 - 3000 BC'') is shared by 47 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36915", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0046:chunk-0001: description ''The Necropolis of Li Muri is a Neolithic burial complex in Arzachena, Sardinia, about 10 km from the Gulf of Arzachena. Discovered in 1939 and excavated by Salvatore Puglisi in 1939-1940, it comprises five stone cists surrounded by concentric stone circles measuring 5.30 to 8.50 m in diameter. Now attributed to the San Ciriaco culture (c. 3400-3200 BC), the quadrangular cists held single burials covered by earth mounds. Artifacts include steatite cups with Aegean influences, flint blades, small axes, and necklace beads with traces of red ochre.'' -> \"The Necropolis of Li Muri is an archaeological site located in the municipality of Arzachena, Sardinia [1]. The necropolis, thought to be a product of the Neolithic Arzachena culture and dating from the second half of the fourth millennium BC, is composed of five stone cists [1]. Four of the cists are surrounded by stone circles that originally marked the limits of the mound of earth and rubble that was erected over the burial [1]. Bodies were interred inside the cists, probably individually (unlike in the rest of Sardinia where the graves were usually collective) [1]. The dead were accompanied by grave goods including pottery, stone vessels, hatchets and beads necklace of steatite and gemstones [1]. The architecture of the necropolis shows strong similarities with contemporary sites of Corsica, Provence and the Pyrenees [1]. The necropolis forms part of the Arzachena Archaeological Park along with two other antiquities: the Nuraghe Albucciu and the Giants'' grave of Coddu Vecchiu [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
