    ('99c67365-3376-46ae-9e60-0d2ec03bfff4'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '6', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:99c67365-3376-46ae-9e60-0d2ec03bfff4:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''6'' restored on Coventina', '7aa9bf0b1370d488b04bd6d67a819f41', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Rock relief/carving'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 8 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:32793", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0006:chunk-0001: description \"Coventina was a Romano-British goddess of wells and springs venerated at a sacred well near the Roman fort of Brocolitia (Carrawburgh) on Hadrian''s Wall in Northumberland. The shrine, excavated in 1876, consisted of a rectangular stone basin surrounded by a walled enclosure approximately 12 m square over a natural spring. Excavations uncovered extraordinary votive deposits including about 16,000 coins, ten inscribed altars to Coventina and Minerva, two dedication slabs, a sculpture of three water nymphs, and clay incense burners. Evidence suggests the shrine was abruptly closed around AD 388, possibly linked to the anti-pagan edicts of Theodosius I.\" -> ''Dedications to Coventina and votive deposits were found in a walled area which had been built to contain the outflow from a spring now called \"Coventina\\''s Well\" [1]. The well itself was a spring in a rectangular basin 2.6m x 2.4m in the centre of a walled enclosure 11.6m x 12.2m within a wall 0.9m thick [1]. The contents of the well included 13,487 coins from Mark Anthony to Gratian, a relief of three water nymphs, the head of a male statue, two dedication slabs to the goddess Coventina, ten altars to Coventina and Minerva, two clay incense burners, and a wide range of votive objects [1]. Excavation of the site revealed several inscribed altars, some with depictions of Coventina in typical Roman nymph form: reclining, partially clothed and associated with water [1]. At least ten inscriptions to Coventina are recorded from Carrawburgh [1]. Several stone altars contained dedications to Coventina as did two pottery incense burners [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
