    ('9adf8063-9af0-4610-b021-0e9f0cef4c02'::uuid, 'mystery', '9', '8', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:9adf8063-9af0-4610-b021-0e9f0cef4c02:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''8'' restored on Vale Maria do Meio Cromlech', '74d658882ffc8c5b8c074a54e6ec26e9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 9 from (''Stone circle'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 2 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36947", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0047:chunk-0001: description \"The Vale Maria do Meio Cromlech is a megalithic stone circle in the Evora district of Portugal''s Alentejo region, dating to the 6th-5th millennium BC. Identified in 1993 by Manuel Calado''s team from the University of Lisbon, excavations in 1995 revealed 34 granite menhirs arranged in an open horseshoe roughly 37 by 25 metres. At least two stones bear engravings of lunar crescents and staffs, similar to those at the nearby Almendres Cromlech. Classified as a National Monument, the stones were re-erected in 1995 using Neolithic-era techniques with ropes, logs, and 70 volunteers.\" -> ''The Vale Maria do Meio Cromlech is a megalithic stone circle situated in Évora district in the Alentejo region of Portugal [1]. It is believed to date back to the fifth millennium BCE or earlier [1]. The cromlech was identified in 1993 by a team of students from the University of Lisbon, led by Manuel Calado [1]. Excavations in 1995, sponsored by the local authority and led by Prof. Calado, identified 34 granite menhirs, which are predominantly oval in shape and have an average height of 1.74 meters [1]. Suggesting an astronomical purpose, the stones form an east-west elongated arch, which is about 37 meters long and 25 meters wide, with the largest monoliths being placed at the highest point of the site, to the west [1]. Engravings of circles, horseshoes and lunar crescents have been found on two of the menhirs, closely resembling engravings found on the Almendres Cromlech [1]. The arrangement of the menhirs suggested to the archaeologists that this monument was built in two distinct phases [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9adf8063-9af0-4610-b021-0e9f0cef4c02'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '49', '46', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:9adf8063-9af0-4610-b021-0e9f0cef4c02:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: rarity_score ''46'' restored on Vale Maria do Meio Cromlech', '74d658882ffc8c5b8c074a54e6ec26e9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 49 from mystery 9, cultural_influence 10"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36947", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0047:chunk-0001: description \"The Vale Maria do Meio Cromlech is a megalithic stone circle in the Evora district of Portugal''s Alentejo region, dating to the 6th-5th millennium BC. Identified in 1993 by Manuel Calado''s team from the University of Lisbon, excavations in 1995 revealed 34 granite menhirs arranged in an open horseshoe roughly 37 by 25 metres. At least two stones bear engravings of lunar crescents and staffs, similar to those at the nearby Almendres Cromlech. Classified as a National Monument, the stones were re-erected in 1995 using Neolithic-era techniques with ropes, logs, and 70 volunteers.\" -> ''The Vale Maria do Meio Cromlech is a megalithic stone circle situated in Évora district in the Alentejo region of Portugal [1]. It is believed to date back to the fifth millennium BCE or earlier [1]. The cromlech was identified in 1993 by a team of students from the University of Lisbon, led by Manuel Calado [1]. Excavations in 1995, sponsored by the local authority and led by Prof. Calado, identified 34 granite menhirs, which are predominantly oval in shape and have an average height of 1.74 meters [1]. Suggesting an astronomical purpose, the stones form an east-west elongated arch, which is about 37 meters long and 25 meters wide, with the largest monoliths being placed at the highest point of the site, to the west [1]. Engravings of circles, horseshoes and lunar crescents have been found on two of the menhirs, closely resembling engravings found on the Almendres Cromlech [1]. The arrangement of the menhirs suggested to the archaeologists that this monument was built in two distinct phases [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9adf8063-9af0-4610-b021-0e9f0cef4c02'::uuid, 'total_power', '37', '36', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:9adf8063-9af0-4610-b021-0e9f0cef4c02:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: total_power ''36'' restored on Vale Maria do Meio Cromlech', '74d658882ffc8c5b8c074a54e6ec26e9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 37 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 9 + 8 + 10 + 9 + 1"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36947", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0047:chunk-0001: description \"The Vale Maria do Meio Cromlech is a megalithic stone circle in the Evora district of Portugal''s Alentejo region, dating to the 6th-5th millennium BC. Identified in 1993 by Manuel Calado''s team from the University of Lisbon, excavations in 1995 revealed 34 granite menhirs arranged in an open horseshoe roughly 37 by 25 metres. At least two stones bear engravings of lunar crescents and staffs, similar to those at the nearby Almendres Cromlech. Classified as a National Monument, the stones were re-erected in 1995 using Neolithic-era techniques with ropes, logs, and 70 volunteers.\" -> ''The Vale Maria do Meio Cromlech is a megalithic stone circle situated in Évora district in the Alentejo region of Portugal [1]. It is believed to date back to the fifth millennium BCE or earlier [1]. The cromlech was identified in 1993 by a team of students from the University of Lisbon, led by Manuel Calado [1]. Excavations in 1995, sponsored by the local authority and led by Prof. Calado, identified 34 granite menhirs, which are predominantly oval in shape and have an average height of 1.74 meters [1]. Suggesting an astronomical purpose, the stones form an east-west elongated arch, which is about 37 meters long and 25 meters wide, with the largest monoliths being placed at the highest point of the site, to the west [1]. Engravings of circles, horseshoes and lunar crescents have been found on two of the menhirs, closely resembling engravings found on the Almendres Cromlech [1]. The arrangement of the menhirs suggested to the archaeologists that this monument was built in two distinct phases [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9aedc6a3-1681-4ee3-8d6c-4386de4bba1d'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Monuments', 'Megalithic', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:9aedc6a3-1681-4ee3-8d6c-4386de4bba1d:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Megalithic'' restored on Roman Amphitheatre of Cagliari', '909eefbb94097b30a9beb2445c2eef6a', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Monuments from site_type ''Amphitheatre'' is in group ''Monuments''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28448", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0135:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Amphitheatre''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44861", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0101:chunk-0001: description ''(2nd - 5th c. AD) The Roman Amphitheatre of Cagliari is an ancient Roman amphitheatre located in Cagliari, southern Sardinia, Italy[1]. Built in the 2nd century AD, the structure was half carved directly into the rock of the hill, while the remaining half was constructed using local white limestone, with a facade reaching over 20 metres (65.6 ft) in height[1]. The arena hosted gladiatorial combat and fights between men and animals, with fighters recruited from within and outside Sardinia[1]. It also served as the seat of public executions[1].'' -> ''The Roman Amphitheatre of Cagliari is an ancient Roman amphitheatre, located in the city of Cagliari, southern Sardinia [1]. The structure, built in the 2nd century AD, was half carved in the rock, while the rest was built in local white limestone, with a façade surpassing 20 m in height [1]. The surface of the arena was 1124.27 square meters [1]. It housed fights between men and animals, of gladiators and other specialized fighters recruited in and outside Sardinia [1]. It was also the seat of public executions [1]. It could house up to 8,000 spectators, some one fourth of the Roman Caralis [1]. The amphitheatre was no longer in use starting from the 5th century AD and was subsequently used as a free stone quarry by the rulers of the area, from the Byzantines, the Republic of Pisa, the House of Aragon and others [1]. The area was acquired by the comune of Cagliari in the 19th century and excavated under the direction of a clergyman, Giovanni Spano [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
