when it was replaced by South Stoa II [1]. The stoa stretched along most of the southern edge of the Classical Agora, facing north onto it [1]. The Stoa was 80.45 metres long east-west and 14.89 metres north-south [1]. It consisted of a two-aisled colonnade facing north and a set of fifteen rooms running along the back wall [1]. This became a common design for stoas, but South Stoa I appears to have been the earliest example [1]. The first traces were identified and excavated in 1936 under the direction of Eugene Vanderpool [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
