    ('9cd5b89c-3734-4dc0-9181-a23eaaffac23'::uuid, 'antiquity', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:9cd5b89c-3734-4dc0-9181-a23eaaffac23:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: antiquity ''7'' restored on Cahal Pech', '94559089d5fd99c18581492fb1256416', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 6 from period_start -1200"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28731", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0185:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-1200''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30465", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43601", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0077:chunk-0001: description \"Cahal Pech is a Maya palace complex perched on a hill near San Ignacio in Belize''s Cayo District [1]. Though most major construction occurred during the Classic period (250-900 AD), excavations have uncovered continuous habitation dating to 1200 BCE during the Early Middle Formative period, making it one of the oldest recognizably Maya sites in Western Belize [1]. The complex served as a palatial residence for an elite Maya family [1].\" -> ''Cahal Pech is a Maya site located near the town of San Ignacio in the Cayo District of Belize [1]. The site rests high above the banks of the Macal River and is strategically located to overlook the confluence of the Macal River and the Mopan River [1]. The site is a collection of 34 structures, with the tallest temple being about 25 meters in height, situated around a central acropolis [1]. The site was abandoned in the 9th century CE for unknown reasons [1]. The name Cahal Pech, meaning \"Place of the Ticks\" in the Yucatec Maya language, was given when the area was used as pasture during the first archaeological studies in the 1950s, led by Linton Satterthwaite from the University of Pennsylvania Museum [1]. The primary excavation of the site began in 1988 [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9cd5b89c-3734-4dc0-9181-a23eaaffac23'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:9cd5b89c-3734-4dc0-9181-a23eaaffac23:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''5'' restored on Cahal Pech', '94559089d5fd99c18581492fb1256416', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Pyramid complex'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28731", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0185:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-1200''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30465", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43601", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0077:chunk-0001: description \"Cahal Pech is a Maya palace complex perched on a hill near San Ignacio in Belize''s Cayo District [1]. Though most major construction occurred during the Classic period (250-900 AD), excavations have uncovered continuous habitation dating to 1200 BCE during the Early Middle Formative period, making it one of the oldest recognizably Maya sites in Western Belize [1]. The complex served as a palatial residence for an elite Maya family [1].\" -> ''Cahal Pech is a Maya site located near the town of San Ignacio in the Cayo District of Belize [1]. The site rests high above the banks of the Macal River and is strategically located to overlook the confluence of the Macal River and the Mopan River [1]. The site is a collection of 34 structures, with the tallest temple being about 25 meters in height, situated around a central acropolis [1]. The site was abandoned in the 9th century CE for unknown reasons [1]. The name Cahal Pech, meaning \"Place of the Ticks\" in the Yucatec Maya language, was given when the area was used as pasture during the first archaeological studies in the 1950s, led by Linton Satterthwaite from the University of Pennsylvania Museum [1]. The primary excavation of the site began in 1988 [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9cd5b89c-3734-4dc0-9181-a23eaaffac23'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '40', '37', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:9cd5b89c-3734-4dc0-9181-a23eaaffac23:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: rarity_score ''37'' restored on Cahal Pech', '94559089d5fd99c18581492fb1256416', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 40 from mystery 6, cultural_influence 10"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28731", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0185:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-1200''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30465", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43601", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0077:chunk-0001: description \"Cahal Pech is a Maya palace complex perched on a hill near San Ignacio in Belize''s Cayo District [1]. Though most major construction occurred during the Classic period (250-900 AD), excavations have uncovered continuous habitation dating to 1200 BCE during the Early Middle Formative period, making it one of the oldest recognizably Maya sites in Western Belize [1]. The complex served as a palatial residence for an elite Maya family [1].\" -> ''Cahal Pech is a Maya site located near the town of San Ignacio in the Cayo District of Belize [1]. The site rests high above the banks of the Macal River and is strategically located to overlook the confluence of the Macal River and the Mopan River [1]. The site is a collection of 34 structures, with the tallest temple being about 25 meters in height, situated around a central acropolis [1]. The site was abandoned in the 9th century CE for unknown reasons [1]. The name Cahal Pech, meaning \"Place of the Ticks\" in the Yucatec Maya language, was given when the area was used as pasture during the first archaeological studies in the 1950s, led by Linton Satterthwaite from the University of Pennsylvania Museum [1]. The primary excavation of the site began in 1988 [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('9d58f665-8cc3-4438-9dc5-99885deb90d0'::uuid, 'antiquity', '10', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:9d58f665-8cc3-4438-9dc5-99885deb90d0:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: antiquity ''4'' restored on Bruniquel Cave', 'a46a2b334db3ec89ef3eb4719907fb79', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 10 from period_start -176500"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28545", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0157:chunk-0001: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-176500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30466", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''< 4500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36963", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0047:chunk-0001: description ''Bruniquel Cave is an archaeological site near the town of Bruniquel in southwestern France, famous for mysterious stone circle structures built by Neanderthals around 176,500 years ago [1][2]. Located 336 metres from the cave entrance, the annular (ring) and accumulation (pile) structures were constructed from broken stalagmites, with traces of fire found nearby [1]. The discovery challenged previous assumptions about Neanderthal cognitive abilities, showing they were capable of building elaborate, purpose-built structures deep underground [2][3].'' -> ''Bruniquel Cave is an archeological site near Bruniquel, in an area which has many paleolithic sites, east of Montauban in southwestern France [1]. Annular (ring) and accumulation (pile) structures made of broken stalagmites have been found 336 metres from the cave entrance [1]. Traces of fire were also found [1]. In a letter to Nature reporting the discovery in 2016, Jacques Jaubert and his co-authors state the structures are of anthropogenic origin, and as early Neanderthals were the only humans in the area at that time, they must have been the builders [1]. Bruniquel Cave was closed naturally during the Pleistocene [1]. The artificial ring structures made of broken stalagmites now have been dated by uranium series dating as 176.5 thousand years old [1]. Approximately 400 stalagmite pieces were used in the construction of the structure, with a total length of 112.4 metres and weight of approximately 2.2 tons [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
