    ('a0f4690e-06d0-457b-92da-40207bfeec25'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:a0f4690e-06d0-457b-92da-40207bfeec25:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Old Winchester Hill', 'd8a7977f7ba4e2d2f9dceb0cbf477207', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 45 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29333", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0272:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-600''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30468", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35919", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35920", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-600'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43607", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0077:chunk-0001: description ''Old Winchester Hill is an Iron Age hillfort and nature reserve on the South Downs chalk ridge in Hampshire, England. The fort was probably built in the Early to Middle Iron Age (600-300 BC) and fell out of use around 150-100 BC. Within the fort are Bronze Age barrows dating to 2100-750 BC, all excavated during the Victorian period. The hilltop site features well-defined rampart entrances at east and west, with evidence of nearly 70 hut platforms identified through geophysical survey. Celtic field systems and trackways lie to the south. The chalk ridgeway passing the site has been used as a walkway for approximately 8,000 years. Since 2009 it has been part of the South Downs National Park.'' -> ''Old Winchester Hill is a 66.2-hectare biological Site of Special Scientific Interest in Hampshire [1]. Despite its name, the hill is around 11 miles from Winchester; it is, however, part of the Winchester–East Meon Anticline [1]. It lies east of Corhampton on the eastern side of the Meon Valley, opposite Beacon Hill [1]. On the summit of the hill is an Iron Age hill fort [1]. Within the hill fort can also be found Bronze Age barrows dating between 2100 and 750 BCE [1]. The fort itself was probably built in the Early or early–Middle Iron Age (600–300 BCE) and fell out of use around the beginning of the Late Iron Age (150–100 BCE), as this is the general pattern for hill forts in the south-east of England [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
