    ('a2b988c2-368a-44ac-a95b-32d73d74a116'::uuid, 'mystery', '8', '9', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:a2b988c2-368a-44ac-a95b-32d73d74a116:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''9'' restored on Oldbury Camp', '19b9630e2a3eee178870d612019590a5', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 8 from (''Fortification'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 5 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28313", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0109:chunk-0001: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-100''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35924", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-100'' -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37031", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0049:chunk-0001: description \"Oldbury Camp is the largest Iron Age multivallate hillfort in south-eastern England, built around 100 BC by Celtic British tribes on a hilltop west of Ightham, Kent. The defences form a rough diamond shape measuring 1,350 m north-south by 700 m east-west, enclosing about 50 hectares with entrances at the north-east and south. A V-shaped ditch was cut and spoil heaped into a bank as the primary defence. The fort was likely a temporary refuge rather than permanent settlement. It was abandoned around the time of Caesar''s invasions of Britain (55-54 BC), with evidence of burning at the northern gate suggesting possible Roman assault. The site is now owned by the National Trust.\" -> \"Oldbury Camp is the largest Iron Age hill fort in south-eastern England [1]. It was built in the 1st century BC by Celtic British tribes on a hilltop west of Ightham, Kent [1]. The fort comprises a bank and ditch enclosing an area of about 50 hectares, with entrances at the north-east and south ends [1]. Wooden gates barred the entrances [1]. Archaeological excavations carried out in the 1930s and 1980s found that the hill fort''s interior had probably not been permanently occupied [1]. Oldbury Camp has been excavated archaeologically twice: once in 1938 by John Bryan Ward-Perkins and again in 1983–84 [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
