    ('a42d9ff4-6d8d-4cb2-bb1d-64b1c66cb290'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '8', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:a42d9ff4-6d8d-4cb2-bb1d-64b1c66cb290:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''8'' restored on Llyn Cerrig Bach', '4c2b92baf8e4bc76bf449b82b8a06cf9', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Archaeological site'', ''500 BC - 1 AD'') is shared by 13 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37033", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0049:chunk-0001: description ''Llyn Cerrig Bach is a small lake on Anglesey, Wales, where approximately 180 Iron Age metal objects were discovered in 1943 during construction of RAF Valley airfield. The assemblage includes 11 swords, 8 spearheads, parts of a parade shield, and equipment from up to 22 chariots, deposited as votive offerings between c. 300 BC and AD 60. It represents the most important British example of Celtic ritual deposition of prestige metalwork into sacred waters, a practice described by Caesar. The collection is held at the National Museum of Wales in Cardiff.'' -> ''Llyn Cerrig Bach is a small lake located between Rhosneigr and Valley in the west of Anglesey [1]. Whilst it is not one of the largest lakes on the island (around 1.8 acres), its claim to fame is the group of over 150 Iron Age metal objects discovered there in 1942, apparently placed in the lake as votive offerings [1]. These finds are considered to be one of the most important collections of La Tène style metalwork, or Early Celtic art discovered in the British Isles, and the most important in Wales [1]. The discovery was made by William Owen Roberts, head groundsman of RAF Valley, when ground was being cleared for a runway extension [1]. This involved spreading peat over the sandy ground, and the items were discovered during the extraction of peat from the Cors yr Ynys bog on the southern margin of Llyn Cerrig Bach [1]. The first object to be found was an iron gang chain, used for slaves [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
