    ('aaa12d23-d666-4a40-9f3c-17d861f4e143'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:aaa12d23-d666-4a40-9f3c-17d861f4e143:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Gobustan Rock Art', '0c7ea2a201823c9f77316089f8e8903d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -18000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37227", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0050:chunk-0001: description ''Gobustan Rock Art features thousands of petroglyphs depicting prehistoric life, including hunting scenes with lance-bearers, ritual dances, animal figures, bullfighting, camel caravans, and celestial symbols like the sun and stars [1]. These engravings date from approximately 5,000 to 20,000 years ago, spanning the Upper Paleolithic through medieval periods [1]. The rock art provides insight into the spiritual life and daily activities of early human populations in the Caucasus region [1]. Recognized as a UNESCO World Heritage Site, it forms part of the Gobustan State Historical and Cultural Reserve [1].'' -> ''The Gobustan Rock Art Cultural Landscape is a historic site in Azerbaijan, comprising a large collection of petroglyphs that reflect the flora, fauna, hunting practices, social structures, and cultural expressions of human societies from prehistoric to medieval times [1]. These engravings provide detailed depictions of anthropomorphic figures, such as lance-bearers and ritualistic dance scenes, as well as various animal species, bullfighting events, camel caravans, and celestial symbols such as the sun and other stars [1]. The Gobustan Rock Art Cultural Landscape reflects the history of human development in Eurasia from the Upper Paleolithic era through the Middle Ages [1]. It is situated at the southeastern end of the Greater Caucasus ridge, approximately 70 km southwest of central Baku, and covers an area of 537 hectares [1]. The rock art was accidentally discovered by quarry workers in the 1930s [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
