    ('aad935a4-efbd-4133-94ac-ce7d12789e9c'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:aad935a4-efbd-4133-94ac-ce7d12789e9c:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Torre del Greco', 'e526f5e607aed8bdcfa14b94b1be2955', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period 1..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37229", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0050:chunk-0001: description ''Torre del Greco is a coastal town in the Metropolitan City of Naples, Italy, situated on the slopes of Mount Vesuvius between Herculaneum and Pompeii. In Roman times it served as a suburb of Herculaneum, with patrician villas lining the coast. The most significant archaeological site is Villa Sora, a grand residence constructed in the mid-1st century BC that stretched nearly 500 feet along the Bay of Naples before being buried by Vesuvius in AD 79. Recent excavations uncovered rooms with black and red frescoes depicting herons, griffins, and a centaur. The area falls within the UNESCO buffer zone for Pompeii and Herculaneum.'' -> \"Based on archaeological evidence, in Roman times Torre del Greco was probably a suburb of Herculaneum and, like elsewhere on the Bay of Naples such as at Oplontis and Stabiae, many patricians'' villas would have lined the coast [1]. In 79 AD the eruption of Vesuvius buried the area under volcanic ash [1]. Later two villages are known to have been established in the area, Sora and Calastro [1]. The Byzantine general Belisarius moved their inhabitants to Naples in 535 [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
