    ('ae64dd4f-971f-4fd7-8540-08af5649e67a'::uuid, 'antiquity', '4', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:ae64dd4f-971f-4fd7-8540-08af5649e67a:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: antiquity ''7'' restored on Aeclanum', '486895eed8a1cdc2aa52ee4dd0cde749', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 4 from period_start -300"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29537", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0304:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-300''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30481", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37255", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0051:chunk-0001: description ''An ancient Samnite and Roman town on the Via Appia in southern Italy, about 25 km east of Beneventum near modern Mirabella Eclano. Originally a principal settlement of the Hirpini tribe, it was sacked by Sulla in 89 BC and elevated to a Roman colony under Hadrian in 120 AD. Excavations revealed a macellum, early Christian church, Roman baths preserved several metres high, and a large imperial marble statue likely of Marcus Aurelius. The city suffered an earthquake in 346 AD and burial under the Vesuvian eruption of 472 AD. Now an archaeological park.'' -> ''Aeclanum was an ancient town of Samnium, Southern Italy, about 25 km east-southeast of Beneventum, on the Via Appia [1]. Today there are ruins of the city walls, of an aqueduct, baths and a macellum; nearly 400 inscriptions have also been discovered [1]. Excavation has revealed a long history of pre-Roman settlement [1]. Aeclanum was a town of the Hirpini, although it was never mentioned during the Samnite Wars [1]. Sulla captured it in 89 BC by setting on fire the wooden breastwork by which it was defended, and sacked it [1]. It quickly recovered, new fortifications were erected, and it became a municipium [1]. Hadrian made it a colonia [1]. With the Lombard invasion of Italy, in the 6th century AD, it was annexed to the Duchy of Benevento, but was captured and destroyed by Eastern Roman forces under Constans II in 663 and never recovered, being reduced to a small hamlet known as Quintodecimo, a name that referred to its distance of 15 Roman miles from Benevento [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
