    ('b0676488-4c32-4b8c-857c-eba8d6db4f59'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Monuments', 'Settlements', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:b0676488-4c32-4b8c-857c-eba8d6db4f59:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Settlements'' restored on Wichqana', 'ce7b9bb7872bb3822c37f3306b23931f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Monuments from site_type ''Archaeological site'' is in group ''Monuments''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28340", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0114:chunk-0004: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37267", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0051:chunk-0001: description ''Wichqana is an Early Horizon ceremonial center in the Ayacucho Valley, Peru, active from approximately 1200-800 BC during the Kishka Pata period. Located on a western slope of Uma Urqu north of Ayacucho, the site features U-shaped architecture built of alternating sized stones, similar to the coastal center at Cerro Sechin. The ceremonial complex displays Chavinoid features, indicating that the Wichqana people participated in the broader Chavin cultural sphere that extended from Cajamarca to Ayacucho during this period. It represents important evidence of highland ceremonial traditions in pre-Columbian Peru.'' -> ''Wichqana is an archaeological site in Peru [1]. It is located in the Ayacucho Region, Huamanga Province, Ayacucho District [1]. Wichqana lies on a western slope of Uma Urqu north of Ayacucho [1]. Wichqana was active from around 1200-800 BC during the Kishka Pata Period and was a ceremonial center on the central highlands of Peru, on the Ayacucho Valley [1]. The ceremonial center was similar to Cerro Secchin and the centers were built in a U-shape of alternating sized stones [1]. On the site there were semicircle temple structures on artificial mounds which indicates some sort of priestly figure was used [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
