    ('b3a11168-712b-445a-b453-c945be7900f3'::uuid, 'antiquity', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:b3a11168-712b-445a-b453-c945be7900f3:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: antiquity ''8'' restored on Arbor Low', 'ec819b5a5a52b331585a6271f71261b5', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 7 from period_start -3000"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28284", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0105:chunk-0003: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30486", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37307", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0052:chunk-0001: description ''Arbor Low is a Neolithic henge monument in the Derbyshire Peak District, England, considered the most important prehistoric site in the East Midlands. Its bank and ditch were constructed in the Late Neolithic period, with approximately 50 limestone blocks added before 2000 BC forming an egg-shaped circle. All stones now lie flat. A central cove of six smaller blocks once stood upright. Linked by an earthen ridge to the earlier Gib Hill barrow 320 metres away, it was among the first monuments protected under the 1882 Ancient Monuments Act.'' -> ''Arbor Low is a well-preserved Neolithic henge in the Derbyshire Peak District, England [1]. It lies on a Carboniferous Limestone plateau known as the White Peak area [1]. The monument consists of a stone circle surrounded by earthworks and a ditch [1]. The monument includes about 50 large limestone blocks, quarried from a local site, which form an egg-shaped circle [1]. In the centre of the circle lie at least six smaller blocks known as the cove, originally believed to have been set in a rectangle [1]. Human skeletal remains were discovered close to the cove during excavations between 1901 and 1902 [1]. Other finds have included flint scrapers, arrowheads, and bone and antler tools [1]. The bank and ditch of the henge, as well as its two entrances, were probably established in the Late Neolithic period, with the stones added later, sometime before 2000 BCE [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
