    ('b596bc7e-15ff-4dfd-b052-710626210123'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:b596bc7e-15ff-4dfd-b052-710626210123:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Monkodonja', '8717a07c7be96da9012688113935e330', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 45 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37345", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0053:chunk-0001: description ''Monkodonja is a Bronze Age hillfort near Rovinj in Istria, Croatia, occupied from approximately 1800 to 1200 BC. Situated on a hill 81 m above sea level, the settlement forms an irregular ellipse 250 m long with an acropolis, upper town, and lower town enclosed by three concentric drystone walls up to 3 m high and wide. Discovered in 1953, major international excavations from 1997-2009 revealed multiple cultural layers, defensive gates, and evidence of its strategic role in monitoring trade routes. Today it is an open archaeological park.'' -> ''Monkodonja is a Bronze Age hillfort located near the city of Rovinj in the Croatian region of Istria [1]. It was occupied between about 1850/1750 and 1500/1450 BC [1]. Monkodonja lies on a small hill, 81 m above sea level, and forms an irregular ellipse stretching east–west that is 250 m long and 160 m wide [1]. The settlement was surrounded by three concentric walls and has two entrances that have been explored so far (the so-called Western and Northern Gates) [1]. The walls are dry stone constructions, with Cyclopean masonry reminiscent of Mycenean architecture [1]. Bronze Age cist graves were found in the western gate [1]. The defensive wall surrounding the settlement was about 1 km long, about 3 m wide and at least 3 m tall [1]. The hillfort was discovered in 1953 by B. Baćić and B. Marušić, and the first research was carried out in 1953–1955 [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
