    ('b6ecca26-a3f9-437e-bda5-aa52368598d7'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:b6ecca26-a3f9-437e-bda5-aa52368598d7:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Yagul', 'a6d5f16b842eb5e7766b0eb55019e82f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -1500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28858", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0205:chunk-0001: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30490", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''1500 - 500 BC'' -> ''500 BC - 1 AD''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35964", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_name ''500 BC - 1 AD'' -> ''1500 - 500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:35965", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-500'' -> ''-1500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37347", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0053:chunk-0001: description \"Yagul, meaning ''Old Tree'' in Zapotec, is an archaeological site in Oaxaca, Mexico, first occupied around 500-100 BC, with most visible structures dating to the Postclassic period (1250-1521 AD). Designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2010, it features one of the finest ballcourts in Latin America and the labyrinthine Palace of Six Patios, a complex of elite residences and administrative rooms. A hilltop fortress (fortaleza) provides panoramic views of the Tlacolula Valley. Excavations in the 1950s-60s by Ignacio Bernal and John Paddock uncovered underground tombs including the notable Triple Tomb (Tumba Triple).\" -> ''Yagul is an archaeological site and former city-state associated with the Zapotec civilization of pre-Columbian Mesoamerica, located in the Mexican state of Oaxaca [1]. Yagul was first occupied around 500-100 BC [1]. Around 500-700 AD, residential, civic and ceremonial structures were built at the site [1]. However, most of the visible remains date to 1250-1521 AD, when the site functioned as the capital of a Postclassic city-state [1]. The site is situated on a volcanic outcrop surrounded by fertile alluvial land, in the Tlacolula arm of the Valley of Oaxaca [1]. In the Late Postclassic, immediately prior to the Spanish Conquest, Yagul had a population of more than 6000 people [1]. The construction stone at Yagul is mainly river cobbles formed from volcanic rock such as basalt [1]. About 30 tombs have been found at Yagul, sometimes located in pairs [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
