    ('baf13030-1578-4edc-a351-ca627828a326'::uuid, 'civilization', 'Chile', 'Chile, Easter Island', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:baf13030-1578-4edc-a351-ca627828a326:civilization', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: civilization ''Chile, Easter Island'' restored on Easter Island', '0ae000b13a8cacc8fbbea82e82a85eec', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "civilization = Chile from country ''Chile''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27269", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-21_mechanical-country: country ''Chile, Easter Island'' -> ''Chile''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:44987", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0105:chunk-0001: description ''Easter Island (Rapa Nui) is a Chilean island in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, famous for its nearly 1,000 monumental moai statues created by the Rapa Nui people. The island became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1995, with much of the island protected within Rapa Nui National Park. It is the only territory in Polynesia where Spanish is an official language.'' -> ''Easter Island is an island and special territory of Chile in the southeastern Pacific Ocean, at the southeasternmost point of the Polynesian Triangle in Oceania [1]. The first recorded European contact with the island was on April 5, 1722, Easter Sunday [1]. Easter Island is a volcanic island, consisting mainly of three extinct coalesced volcanoes: Terevaka forms the bulk of the island, while two other volcanoes, Poike and Rano Kau, form the eastern and southern headlands and give the island its roughly triangular shape [1]. Almost all moai were carved from compressed, easily worked solidified volcanic ash or tuff, found at a single site on the side of the extinct volcano Rano Raraku [1]. The native islanders who carved them used only stone hand chisels, mainly basalt toki, which lie in place all over the quarry [1]. Of the 313 known ahu, 125 carried moai – usually just one, probably because of the shortness of the moai period and transportation difficulties [1]. Ahu Tongariki, one km from Rano Raraku, had the most and tallest moai, 15 in total [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
