    ('bc89e53c-a9e6-465e-9cc9-4a6f844b9589'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:bc89e53c-a9e6-465e-9cc9-4a6f844b9589:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on Lang Rongrien Rock Shelter', '42c6fd0064368418305fcef51d9ac02d', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Archaeological site'', ''< 4500 BC'') is shared by 14 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37391", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0054:chunk-0001: description \"Lang Rongrien is a rock shelter and Hoabinhian habitation site in the upland Krabi River valley of southern Thailand, dating to 39,000–28,000 years before present [1]. Excavated in 1974 under Douglas Anderson''s direction, the site yielded rich Pleistocene archaeological finds despite some disturbance from soil collection [1][2]. Access remains difficult, requiring either a steep trail below the shelter or a narrow ridge approach from the north [1].\" -> ''The Hoabinhian hunter-gatherers who lived at the Lang Rongrien rock shelter used the site as a temporary habitation site [1]. The oldest use for the rock shelter was as a campground for hunters in the late Pleistocene epoch [1]. The excavated areas of the Lang Rongrien rock shelter have been divided into ten stratigraphic layers, or cultural deposit layers [1]. The eighth and ninth layers are similar to each other, with charcoal remains, stone artifacts and flakes, and animal bones that are scattered about among these layers [1]. The oldest pieces of charcoal found in the lower most layers where human habitation is seen have been dated to around 39,000 years ago [1]. Charcoal pieces from the upper layers have been dated to about 9,000 years ago [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
