    ('bdf88679-e9b7-4344-a3ed-212184e26102'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Other', 'Settlements', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:bdf88679-e9b7-4344-a3ed-212184e26102:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Settlements'' restored on Palagruža', '8632431df329df350134d43ad74af5bd', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Other from site_type ''Natural feature'' is in group ''Other''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28685", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0179:chunk-0001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Natural feature''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37425", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0055:chunk-0001: description ''Palagruža is a small Croatian archipelago in the middle of the Adriatic Sea [1]. It is uninhabited, except by lighthouse staff and occasional summer tourists [1]. The archipelago can be reached only by a chartered motorboat, requiring a journey of several hours from nearby islands like Lastovo, Korčula, or Vis [1]. It is administratively part of the municipality of Komiža [1].'' -> ''Palagruža is a small Croatian archipelago in the middle of the Adriatic Sea [1]. Authentic archaeological finds from the Neolithic period have been discovered on Palagruža, including a small number of early Neolithic Impressed Ware pottery dated to the 6th–4th millennia BC, as well as a larger quantity of Ljubljana-Adriatic culture and Cetina culture artifacts [1]. Artifacts indicating human presence on Palagruža from the 2nd millennium BC are rarer, but there are substantial finds from ancient Greek seafarers, including material from the Late Archaic, Classical, Hellenistic, and Early Roman periods [1]. There are also recorded archaeological finds from the Late Roman and early medieval periods [1]. Palagruža consists of one larger island, called Vela or Velika Palagruža, and a smaller one, Mala Palagruža, as well as a dozen nearby rocks and reefs composed of dolomite [1]. The archipelago is 123 km south of Split on the Croatian mainland, 60 km south-west of Lastovo, Croatia, and 53 km north-north-east of the Gargano peninsula in Italy [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
