    ('c015ceea-eeb0-4296-9913-390eb795727b'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Monuments', 'Settlements', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:c015ceea-eeb0-4296-9913-390eb795727b:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Settlements'' restored on Cerro Sechín', 'f190043c0c41bb9299908bbcd6da0629', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Monuments from site_type ''Archaeological site'' is in group ''Monuments''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29129", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0243:chunk-0001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Archaeological site''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:37461", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0056:chunk-0001: description \"Cerro Sechín is an archaeological site in Casma Province, Ancash Region, northern Peru, dating to 1600 BC[1]. Discovered by Peruvian archaeologists Julio C. Tello and Toribio Mejía Xesspe on July 1, 1937[1]. The site features megalithic architecture with carved figures in bas-relief that graphically dramatize human sacrifices[1]. Tello hypothesized it was the capital of what is now called the Casma/Sechin culture or Sechin complex[1]. The site is situated within the Sechin Alto Complex alongside Sechin Bajo and Taukachi-Konkan[1]. The carved stone slabs at Cerro Sechin may represent the central Andes'' oldest known monumental sculpture[1]. A small on-site museum displays artifacts from the site[1].\" -> ''Cerro Sechín is an archaeological site in the Casma Province of the Ancash Region in northern Peru [1]. Notable features include megalithic architecture with carved figures in bas-relief, which graphically dramatize human sacrifices [1]. Cerro Sechín is situated within the Sechin Alto Complex, as are Sechin Bajo, and Taukachi-Konkan [1]. Cerro Sechín sits on a granitic hill, in the Casma Valley [1]. The site contains walled enclosures around dwellings as well as temple platforms [1]. Although the archaeological site occupies approximately 5 acres, the monuments are grouped in a single hectare [1]. Rigorous excavation and research occurred at Cerro Sechin after its 1937 discovery by Tello and Xesspe [1]. The etched bas-reliefs number approximately 300, from axe-wielding warriors, to body parts, and victims [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
