nd. First excavated by A.E.P. Collins in 1952 as part of the Archaeological Survey of Northern Ireland, the dig uncovered remains of 34 individuals, both inhumed and cremated, along with mammal bones and charcoal traces. It is among more than 390 court cairns built across Ireland and Scotland during this period.'' -> ''The Audleystown Court Tomb is a Neolithic dual court tomb located in Ballyculter parish, near the southern shore of Strangford Lough in County Down [1]. The tomb was built around 3900–3500 BCE [1]. The Audleystown court tomb has a double courtyard-double burial chamber layout, which is unique to Ireland [1]. In dual-court tombs a court and a gallery are placed at each end of the cairn, as in Audleystown and Cohaw [1]. In 1952, as part of the Archaeological Survey of Northern Ireland, A.E.P Collins, conducted the first archeological excavation of the Audleystown tomb and published his findings in 1954 [1]. After the 1952 excavation, the burial galleries were filled with sand to protect the monument [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
