ite itself is located in a collapsed cave, nestled in a sandstone bluff overlooking the Krapinica River in Hrvatsko Zagorje [1]. More recently, it was determined that the site was about 120–130 kyr [1]. Researchers determined its date by using ESR and U-series dating [1]. 1191 lithic artifacts were discovered during the century of research at the site [1]. Eight white-tailed eagle talons and one foot phalanx were found at the site, and were dated by uranium–thorium dating to 130 000 BP [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('c81634b0-969c-484b-aa1f-5b5926e05fd3'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:c81634b0-969c-484b-aa1f-5b5926e05fd3:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Las Médulas', '1206ca4599ad880eaefa3bc16230161f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period 1..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30971", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-23_owner-case-coordinates: lat ''42.46954715096093'' -> ''42.458699''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30972", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-23_owner-case-coordinates: lon ''-6.770728545613804'' -> ''-6.759002''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:42038", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0058:chunk-0001: description ''Las Medulas is a historic gold-mining site near Ponferrada in Leon province, Spain, and was the most important and largest open-pit gold mine in the Roman Empire. Beginning in the 1st century AD, Romans used ruina montium, a hydraulic mining technique employing pressurized water channeled through long gravity canals to collapse mountainsides and expose gold deposits. After two centuries of operations, the Romans withdrew, leaving a dramatic landscape of sheer red-rock formations. The site was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1997.'' -> ''Las Médulas is a historic gold-mining site near the town of Ponferrada in the comarca of El Bierzo [1]. It was the most important gold mine, as well as the largest open-pit gold mine, in the entire Roman Empire [1]. The spectacular landscape of Las Médulas resulted from ruina montium, a Roman mining technique described by Pliny the Elder in 77 AD [1]. The technique employed was a type of hydraulic mining which involved undermining a mountain with large quantities of water [1]. Large-scale production did not begin until the second half of the 1st century AD [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
