    ('cb0a55c2-48c5-4340-afb2-a5c0d5eec70b'::uuid, 'civilization', 'Georgia', 'Georgia (country)', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:cb0a55c2-48c5-4340-afb2-a5c0d5eec70b:civilization', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: civilization ''Georgia (country)'' restored on Dzalisi', 'b1a22c72e0fefaf64ec60ac5dd80a32b', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "civilization = Georgia from country ''Georgia''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27273", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-21_mechanical-country: country ''Georgia (country)'' -> ''Georgia''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:42056", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0058:chunk-0001: description ''Dzalisi is a major archaeological site in the Mukhrani Valley, Georgia, 50 km northwest of Tbilisi, identified as the Zalissa mentioned by Ptolemy as one of the principal towns of the ancient Kingdom of Iberia. Excavations conducted from 1971 to 1990 uncovered four palatial structures, hypocaustic baths, an acropolis, barracks, and a water supply system spanning an area of 70 hectares. The largest palatial complex is a Roman-style building with approximately 30 rooms. A notable 2nd-century AD mosaic depicting Ariadne and Dionysus at a feast is among the oldest mosaics found in the Caucasus.'' -> ''Dzalisi is a historic village in Georgia, located in the Mukhrani valley, 50 km northwest of Tbilisi, and 20 km northwest of Mtskheta [1]. It is the Zalissa of Ptolemy (AD 90–168) who mentions it as one of principal towns of Iberia, an ancient Georgian kingdom [1]. Archaeological digs have revealed the remains of four palaces and hypocaust baths, acropolis, swimming pool, administrative part, barracks for soldiers, water supply system and burial grounds [1]. One of the villas is notable for its floor mosaics, which, together with the mosaics of Pityus, are, by far, the oldest ones found in the Caucasus [1]. The style of the mosaic dates to around AD 300 [1]. Its central part depicts Ariadne and Dionysus in a banquet scene [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
