    ('cb659fbe-0f1c-43dc-9c40-4517c0974a79'::uuid, 'civilization', 'Switzerland', 'Sweden', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:cb659fbe-0f1c-43dc-9c40-4517c0974a79:civilization', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: civilization ''Sweden'' restored on Aquae Helveticae', 'df0f57c2c0c07723dbbf900d1b9c7d76', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "civilization = Switzerland from country ''Switzerland''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28393", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0123:chunk-0001: country ''Sweden'' -> ''Switzerland''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:42082", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0059:chunk-0001: description ''Aquae Helveticae was a Roman vicus and mineral spa established in the 1st century AD near the legion camp of Vindonissa at Windisch [1]. The hot mineral springs, reaching 47°C (117°F) and rich in sulfur, were discovered around 20 AD at a bend in the Limmat river [1][2]. The settlement quickly developed around the springs, prized by Romans for their therapeutic waters [2]. The site gave its name to the modern town of Baden, Switzerland, and is a Swiss heritage site of national significance [1][3].'' -> ''Aquae Helveticae was a vicus and mineral spa established in the 1st century AD near the Roman legion camp of Vindonissa [1]. The hot springs, located at a bend in the Limmat river near the legion camp at Vindonissa, were discovered around 20 AD [1]. The hot, mineral rich water was prized by the Romans and a settlement quickly developed around the springs [1]. In 69 AD, during the Year of the Four Emperors, the XXI Legion burned the wooden vicus or settlement to the ground [1]. Aquae Helveticae was rebuilt in stone in the following years [1]. The main road through the vicus was the army road leading to Vindonissa [1]. The ruins of three baths have been discovered, though others may have existed [1]. Much of the settlement of Aquae Helveticae is located under the town of Baden [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
