pproximately 63 kilometres and was about 3 metres high and 5 metres wide [1]. Security was bolstered by a deep ditch on the northern side [1]. The barrier was the second of two \"great walls\" created by the Romans in Great Britain in the second century AD [1]. Construction began in AD 142 at the order of Roman Emperor Antoninus Pius [1]. The Antonine Wall was protected by 16 forts with small fortlets between them; troop movement was facilitated by a road linking all the sites known as the Military Way [1]. The soldiers who built the wall commemorated the construction and their struggles with the Caledonians with decorative slabs, twenty of which survive [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('ce101d86-8313-4fe1-bb90-9c74fba2a76e'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:ce101d86-8313-4fe1-bb90-9c74fba2a76e:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Sanctuary of Asclepius, Epidaurus', 'c1290757fb2c14b911a65638421932d2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -500..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28506", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0151:chunk-0001: site_type ''Temple complex'' -> ''Sanctuary''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:42094", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0059:chunk-0001: description ''The Sanctuary of Asclepius at Epidaurus was the principal healing centre of the ancient Greek world, dedicated to the god of medicine. The main temple (380-375 BC) housed a chryselephantine statue of Asclepius, while the circular Tholos contained mysterious underground chambers. Pilgrims slept in the Abaton awaiting divine healing visions. The famous theatre (340-330 BC) with 55 tiers seats 12,300 spectators and is renowned for its exceptional acoustics. Inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1988, the sanctuary spawned over 200 healing centres across the eastern Mediterranean.'' -> ''The Sanctuary of Asclepius was a sanctuary in Epidaurus dedicated to Asclepius [1]. Especially in the Classical and Hellenistic periods, it was the main holy site of Asclepius [1]. The sanctuary at Epidaurus was the rival of such major cult sites as the Sanctuary of Zeus at Olympia and Apollo at Delphi [1]. During the Achaean War in 146 BC, the Achaians converted the sanctuary into a stronghold [1]. In 87 BC, the sanctuary was looted by the Roman general Sulla [1]. The main sanctuary consisted of the Temple and a number of structures clustered around it [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
