    ('d70dd131-fac7-4c7e-b367-95c2ac945339'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:d70dd131-fac7-4c7e-b367-95c2ac945339:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Valley of the Queens', '756b2ba03a9dd17f5e82236fcf149705', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Necropolis/tombs complex'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 44 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45307", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0109:chunk-0001: description \"The Valley of the Queens is an ancient Egyptian necropolis where queens, princes, princesses, and high-ranking officials were interred from approximately 1560 BC to 1130 BC [1]. Originally called Ta-Set-Neferu, meaning ''The Place of Beauty'' or ''the Place of the Royal Children,'' the site contains approximately 110 tombs across multiple valleys [2]. The main wadi houses 91 tombs, with additional tombs in subsidiary valleys [2]. Italian archaeologist Ernesto Schiaparelli pioneered excavation of the site in the early 1900s [1].\" -> ''The Valley of the Queens is a site in Egypt, in which queens, princes, princesses, and other high-ranking officials were buried from roughly 1560 BC to 1130 BC [1]. The Valley of the Queens was known anciently as Ta-Set-Neferu, which has a double meaning of \"The Place of Beauty\" and/or \"the Place of the Royal Children\" [1]. Excavation of the tombs at the Valley of the Queens was pioneered by Ernesto Schiaparelli and Francesco Ballerini in the early 1900s [1]. The Valley of the Queens consists of the main wadi, which contains most of the tombs, along with the Valley of Prince Ahmose, the Valley of the Rope, the Valley of the Three Pits, and the Valley of the Dolmen [1]. The main wadi contains 91 tombs and the subsidiary valleys add another 19 tombs [1]. The burials in the subsidiary valleys all date to the 18th Dynasty [1]. The Valley was no longer a royal burial site after the close of the 20th Dynasty [1]. Not much is known about the use of the Valley of the Queens during the Ptolemaic Period but during the Roman Period there was a renewed, extensive use of the Valley as a burial site [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
