    ('d9d2906b-4528-42af-9796-678ddf7ed30e'::uuid, 'mystery', '7', '8', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:d9d2906b-4528-42af-9796-678ddf7ed30e:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''8'' restored on Castell Bryn Gwyn', '5ddbf1c8f7386d56391340e814e8351c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 7 from (''Henge'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 7 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27762", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0005:chunk-0001: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-2500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36046", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-2500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:42168", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0062:chunk-0001: description ''Castell Bryn Gwyn (\"White Hill Castle\") is a prehistoric enclosure on the Isle of Anglesey, west of Brynsiencyn. Originating as a late Neolithic henge (c. 2500-2000 BC), excavations in 1959-60 revealed it was later adapted as a defended settlement. The circular clay and gravel bank, still 1.5 m high and revetted by stone walls, encloses an area 54 m in diameter. Finds of pottery, post holes, and flint and bronze tools confirmed habitation rather than purely ceremonial use. The stone wall was rebuilt in the Iron Age and again in Roman times, with pottery dating to the late 1st century AD.'' -> ''Castell Bryn Gwyn, officially spelled Castell Bryngwyn, is a prehistoric site on the Isle of Anglesey, west of Brynsiencyn [1]. It is a circular clay and gravel bank covered with grass, still some 1.5 metres high and revetted externally by stone walls, which surround a level area some 54 metres in diameter [1]. Its name means \"White Hill Castle\" [1]. Later Neolithic pottery indicates use in this period, and it may have been a henge monument at this time [1]. The earliest bank and ditch belong to the end of the Neolithic period (2500-2000 BC) [1]. During the Iron Age, the present wall was built, and it was rebuilt in Roman times and later [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
