    ('dc2dad07-a240-4111-9a03-4093ef65301a'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:dc2dad07-a240-4111-9a03-4093ef65301a:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on Xochitecatl', '127dba198c85fa1d02956972af85d107', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Pyramid complex'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 11 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45347", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0110:chunk-0001: description ''Xochitecatl is a purely ceremonial site atop a volcanic dome in Tlaxcala, Mexico, begun around 800 BC. Its largest structure, the Pyramid of the Flowers, rivals the Pyramid of the Moon at Teotihuacan in size. Over 2,000 clay figurines depicting women, mothers, and birth scenes were found there, suggesting dedication to fertility worship. The site includes the unusual Spiral Pyramid (c. 700 BC), a conical structure climbed via a spiral path, likely dedicated to Ehecatl the Wind God. Abandoned around 150 AD after a Popocatepetl eruption, it was reoccupied 600-950 AD as a secondary ceremonial center linked to nearby Cacaxtla.'' -> ''Xochitecatl [ʃot͡ʃiˈtekat͡ɬ] is a pre-Columbian archaeological site located in the Mexican State of Tlaxcala, 18 km southwest of Tlaxcala city [1]. The major architecture dates to the Middle Preclassic Period (1000–400 BC) but occupation continued, with one major interruption, until the Late Classic, when the site was abandoned [1]. The ruins cover an area of 12 hectares on top of a volcanic dome [1]. By the period spanning 350 BC to 100 AD, Xochitecatl had become the centre of a powerful chiefdom exercising control over a large portion of western Tlaxcala [1]. Between 600 AD and 950 AD Xochitecatl was reoccupied when parts of the site were reused as a secondary ceremonial centre within the extended urban area of Cacaxtla [1]. Major excavations were undertaken at the site in 1993-4 by Mari Carmen Serra Puche with the goal of exploring the principal structures around the site core [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
