    ('de42523e-9322-4dba-9448-016174872c48'::uuid, 'antiquity', '4', '6', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:de42523e-9322-4dba-9448-016174872c48:antiquity', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: antiquity ''6'' restored on Teotihuacan', '7179fcd2dd0c87992be1ea5a2140d58c', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "antiquity = 4 from period_start -100"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30895", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:gap-0004:chunk-0003: period_start ''-1500'' -> ''-100''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43667", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0079:chunk-0001: description \"Teotihuacan is an ancient Mesoamerican city located 40 km northeast of modern Mexico City. Settled by 400 BCE and peaking around 500 CE, it covered 20 sq km with a population estimated at 125,000-200,000, making it one of the world''s largest cities. The site features the Pyramid of the Sun, the Pyramid of the Moon, and the Avenue of the Dead stretching 1.5 miles. Designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987, the city was destroyed by fire around 750 CE, possibly during civil unrest. Over 95% of the ruins remain unexcavated.\" -> ''Teotihuacan is an ancient Mesoamerican city located in a sub-valley of the Valley of Mexico, which is located in the State of Mexico [1]. Teotihuacan is known today as the site of many of the most architecturally significant Mesoamerican pyramids built in the pre-Columbian Americas, namely the Pyramid of the Sun and the Pyramid of the Moon [1]. Apart from the pyramids, Teotihuacan is also anthropologically significant for its complex, multi-family residential compounds, the Avenue of the Dead, and its vibrant, well-preserved murals [1]. Teotihuacan exported fine obsidian tools found throughout Mesoamerica [1]. The first human establishment in the Teotihuacan area dates back to 600 BCE, and until 200 BCE the site consisted of scattered small villages [1]. The city reached its peak in 450 CE when it was the center of a powerful culture whose influence extended through much of the Mesoamerican region [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
