    ('e2bb7b81-bdd5-4713-893f-3c2c516a3d7a'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Fortifications', 'Settlements', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:e2bb7b81-bdd5-4713-893f-3c2c516a3d7a:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Settlements'' restored on Vindobona', 'e9191d9584bda0608da6ef946b80b270', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Fortifications from site_type ''Fortress'' is in group ''Fortifications''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29701", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0327:chunk-0001: site_type ''City/town/settlement'' -> ''Fortress''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45365", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0110:chunk-0001: description \"A Roman legionary fortress established in the 1st century AD on the Danube frontier in the province of Pannonia, on the site of modern Vienna. The camp covered about 20 hectares and housed around 6,000 soldiers, with its wall trace still visible in Vienna''s inner-city streets including Graben, Naglergasse, and Tiefer Graben. Emperor Marcus Aurelius died here on 17 March 180 AD during a campaign against Germanic tribes. A civilian settlement grew southeast of the fort from the 3rd century. The fortress suffered a devastating fire in the early 5th century. Now part of the UNESCO Danube Limes World Heritage Site, remains are displayed at the Romermuseum beneath Hoher Markt.\" -> ''Vindobona was a Roman military camp (or castra) in the province of Pannonia, located on the site of the modern city of Vienna in Austria [1]. Early references to Vindobona are made by the geographer Ptolemy in his Geographica and the historian Aurelius Victor, who recounts that emperor Marcus Aurelius died in Vindobona on 17 March 180 from an unknown illness while on a military campaign against invading Germanic tribes [1]. Vindobona was a military camp contiguous to the civilian city of Canabae [1]. The Danube marked the border of the Roman Empire, and Vindobona was part of a defensive network including the camps of Carnuntum, Brigetio and Aquincum [1]. Over 3,000 stamped bricks, several stone monuments and written sources prove that several legions, cavalry units and marines were stationed in Vindobona [1]. Legio X Gemina from Aquincum arrived in 114 AD and remained in Vindobona until the 5th century [1]. Excavations have revealed that Vindobona received its supply through a 17 km long water pipeline [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
