    ('e50a076d-5550-4c47-a49b-aced289dd4e0'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:e50a076d-5550-4c47-a49b-aced289dd4e0:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Hambledon Hill', 'f5f8f515d8da56b1790638046b909fae', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Fortress/citadel'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 45 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43671", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0079:chunk-0001: description ''Major prehistoric site in Dorset, England, with occupation spanning over 5,000 years. The earliest phase (c. 3680-3630 BC) comprised Neolithic causewayed enclosures used for seasonal gatherings, feasting, and mortuary rituals, where human skulls were placed at ditch bottoms as possible ancestral offerings. Excavations by Roger Mercer in the 1970s-80s uncovered a young man killed by an arrow during the burning of timber defences, evidence of early British conflict. The Iron Age hillfort (c. 600 BC) expanded to 125,000 square metres with multiple circuits of banks and ditches, housing hundreds of small round houses before abandonment around 300 BC. Now owned by the National Trust, it is one of the best-preserved Neolithic landscapes in Europe.'' -> ''Hambledon Hill is a prehistoric hill fort in Dorset, England, in the Blackmore Vale five miles northwest of Blandford Forum [1]. The hill itself is a chalk outcrop, on the southwestern corner of Cranborne Chase, separated from the Dorset Downs by the River Stour [1]. Excavations in the 1970s and 1980s by Roger Mercer produced large quantities of Neolithic material [1]. Environmental analysis indicated the site was occupied whilst the area was still wooded with forest clearances coming later, in the Bronze Age [1]. The hillfort is a Scheduled Monument, as is the adjacent Neolithic causewayed camp [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
