    ('ec838509-a1e6-4f6a-b216-3f1791c16d67'::uuid, 'civilization', 'Northern Ireland', 'Ireland', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:ec838509-a1e6-4f6a-b216-3f1791c16d67:civilization', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: civilization ''Ireland'' restored on Legananny Dolmen', '0467593f0e9440e8531e643816a5696f', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "civilization = Northern Ireland from country ''Northern Ireland''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28787", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0194:chunk-0001: period_start ''-4500'' -> ''-3000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30533", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-period-name: period_name ''4500 - 3000 BC'' -> ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30808", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-uk-parts: country ''Ireland'' -> ''Northern Ireland''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36075", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_name ''3000 - 1500 BC'' -> ''4500 - 3000 BC''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36076", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-3000'' -> ''-4500''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43121", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0066:chunk-0001: description ''A Neolithic portal tomb on the slopes of Slieve Croob in County Down, Northern Ireland, approximately 4,500 years old. Its coffin-shaped capstone, over 3 meters long, is balanced on three upright stones reaching up to 2 meters high, giving it a distinctive tripod form likened to a coffin held high by pallbearers. Reports indicate an urn was found in the chamber beneath the tomb, and boulders under the grass suggest a cairn once covered the structure. A State Care Historic Monument, it is one of the most photographed megalithic sites in Ireland.'' -> ''The Legananny Dolmen is a megalithic dolmen or cromlech nine miles southeast of Banbridge and three miles north of Castlewellan, both in County Down, Northern Ireland [1]. It is on the slopes of Slieve Croob near the village of Leitrim in Drumgooland parish [1]. It is a State Care Historic Monument sited in the townland of Legananny, in Banbridge District [1]. This tripod dolmen has a capstone over 3 m long and 1.8 m from the ground [1]. It dates to the Neolithic period, making the monument around 5,000 years old [1]. Such portal tombs were funerary sites for the disposal of the dead in Neolithic society [1]. The three supporting stones are unusually long and there are slight traces of a cairn which must have been far more extensive [1]. Some urns were found underneath [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
