    ('ed78d548-5409-4909-abc0-793390dfed1c'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:ed78d548-5409-4909-abc0-793390dfed1c:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Valley of the Kings', 'df3e4e36ca62c90156db24864cf2c480', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -3000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45415", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0112:chunk-0001: description \"The Valley of the Kings is a royal necropolis on the west bank of the Nile near Luxor in Upper Egypt, containing 62 documented rock-cut tombs excavated for pharaohs and nobles of the 18th through 20th Dynasties (c. 1539-1075 BC). Designed for concealment unlike the earlier pyramids, the tombs feature elaborate painted scenes from funerary texts to guide pharaohs into the afterlife. Howard Carter''s 1922 discovery of Tutankhamun''s nearly intact tomb, with its golden death mask and thousands of artefacts, remains one of archaeology''s greatest finds. The valley was designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1979 as part of ancient Thebes.\" -> \"The Valley of the Kings, also known as the Valley of the Gates of the Kings, is an area in Egypt where, for a period of nearly 500 years from the Eighteenth Dynasty to the Twentieth Dynasty, rock-cut tombs were excavated for pharaohs and powerful nobles under the New Kingdom of ancient Egypt [1]. It is a wadi sitting on the west bank of the Nile, opposite Thebes and within the heart of the Theban Necropolis [1]. With the 2005 discovery of a new chamber and the 2008 discovery of two further tomb entrances, the Valley of the Kings is known to contain 65 tombs and chambers, ranging in size from the simple pit that is KV54 to the complex tomb that is KV5 [1]. It was the principal burial place for the New Kingdom''s major royal figures as well as a number of privileged nobles [1]. The royal tombs are decorated with traditional scenes from Egyptian mythology and reveal clues to the period''s funerary practices and afterlife beliefs [1]. The Valley of the Kings garnered significant attention following the discovery of the tomb of Tutankhamun in 1922 [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
