    ('ee67e46b-e0ee-4bbe-b7bb-49000bdc5e94'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Monuments', 'Megalithic', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:ee67e46b-e0ee-4bbe-b7bb-49000bdc5e94:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Megalithic'' restored on Arènes de Lutèce', '413c1dbc146b8cf64ffee840bc4d94bf', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Monuments from site_type ''Amphitheatre'' is in group ''Monuments''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28049", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0057:chunk-0001: site_type ''Megalithic structures'' -> ''Amphitheatre''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45417", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0112:chunk-0001: description \"A 1st-century AD Gallo-Roman amphitheatre in Paris, one of the city''s most important ancient remains alongside the Thermes de Cluny. Its hybrid design combined a 41 m stage with terraced seating surrounding more than half the arena, accommodating up to 15,000 spectators for gladiatorial combats and theatrical performances. Rediscovered in the 1860s during Rue Monge construction, the arena was saved from demolition through a campaign led by Victor Hugo. Opened as a public park in 1896, it now hosts petanque and picnics in the 5th arrondissement.\" -> \"The Arènes de Lutèce are among the most important ancient Roman remains in Paris (known in antiquity as Lutetia) [1]. Constructed in the 1st century AD, this theatre could once seat 15,000 people and was used also as an amphitheatre to show gladiatorial combats [1]. Five small rooms were situated beneath the lower terraces, some of which appear to have been animal cages that opened directly into the arena [1]. When Lutetia was sacked during the barbarian raids of 275, some of the structure''s stone work was used to reinforce the city''s defences around the Île de la Cité [1]. Subsequently, the theatre became a cemetery, and was filled in completely following the construction of wall of Philippe Auguste (ca. 1210) [1]. Centuries later, even though the surrounding neighbourhood had retained the name les Arènes, the exact location was lost [1]. It was discovered by Théodore Vaquer during the building of the Rue Monge between 1860 and 1869 [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
