    ('eef7317f-a250-434f-b34d-7933fd546da7'::uuid, 'civilization', 'Northern Ireland', 'Ireland', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:eef7317f-a250-434f-b34d-7933fd546da7:civilization', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: civilization ''Ireland'' restored on Haughey''s Fort', '457a866f92ca4532684640e8d6a3b6d1', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "civilization = Northern Ireland from country ''Northern Ireland''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:30809", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-22_mechanical-uk-parts: country ''Ireland'' -> ''Northern Ireland''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43147", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0067:chunk-0001: description \"Haughey''s Fort is a hill fort in County Armagh, Northern Ireland, situated 3.6 km (2.25 mi) west of Armagh city in the townland of Tray[1]. The massive oval enclosure measures 350 metres (1,150 ft) across at its widest point and is surrounded by two concentric ditches[1]. Within these defenses, an additional ditch encloses an inner area approximately 150 metres (490 ft) in diameter[1]. The site is a Scheduled Historic Monument and takes its name from the farmer who owned the land in the later 19th century[1]. The fort dates to the Middle Bronze Age (1500-500 BC)[1].\" -> \"Haughey''s Fort is a hill fort in County Armagh, Northern Ireland [1]. The large hilltop enclosure that is Haughey''s Fort is a Scheduled Historic Monument in the townland of Tray, in Armagh City and District Council area [1]. It consists of an oval enclosure, 350 metres across at its widest point, surrounded by two concentric ditches [1]. Inside the enclosure another ditch encloses an area 150 metres in diameter [1]. Archaeological excavation shows that it was occupied in the late Bronze Age, ca. 1100 to 900 BC, after which it was abandoned, although some artefacts discovered were of Iron Age date, suggesting that it was later reoccupied [1]. It was contemporary with the nearby artificial pool known as the King''s Stables but was abandoned before Navan Fort, 0.6 miles to the east, was occupied [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
