 as recorded in the Notitia Dignitatum [1]. Sometime round about 400 AD the fort was abandoned [1]. In 1929 some excavations were carried out which recorded the outline of the fort [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('efa6ff4f-1d6a-4121-b1c0-f3d21a6c4ce6'::uuid, 'total_power', '27', '30', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:efa6ff4f-1d6a-4121-b1c0-f3d21a6c4ce6:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: total_power ''30'' restored on Segedunum', '2a15233744574707e760cc3cb1e6ea14', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 27 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 3 + 10 + 10 + 3 + 1"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45423", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0112:chunk-0001: description \"Segedunum was a Roman fortress at Wallsend, North East England, positioned at the eastern terminus of Hadrian''s Wall along the River Tyne[1]. Built around 122 AD during Emperor Hadrian''s reign, the fort served as a military garrison for approximately 300 years until nearly 400 AD[1]. It represents the easternmost point of Hadrian''s Wall and is now the most extensively excavated Roman fort along the wall system[1]. The site operates as Segedunum Roman Fort, Baths and Museum[1]. As part of the Hadrian''s Wall UNESCO World Heritage Site, it provides crucial insights into Roman military infrastructure and daily life on Rome''s northern frontier[1].\" -> \"Segedunum was a Roman fort at modern-day Wallsend, North Tyneside in North East England [1]. The fort lay at the eastern end of Hadrian''s Wall near the banks of the River Tyne [1]. The fort of Segedunum measured 453 feet from north to south and 393 feet from east to west, covering an area of 4.1 acres [1]. The original garrison of Segedunum is unknown, but in the 2nd century Cohors II Nerviorum was stationed there [1]. In the 3rd and 4th