    ('efe2bc83-83cc-4393-b725-a18543dc9d5f'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:efe2bc83-83cc-4393-b725-a18543dc9d5f:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on Antequera Dolmens Site', '9c09040b071506324576b2c977f706f2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Dolmen'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 16 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45425", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0112:chunk-0001: description ''A UNESCO World Heritage Site (inscribed 2016) in Andalusia, Spain, comprising three megalithic monuments and two natural landmarks. The Dolmen of Menga (c. 3750-3650 BCE) is the largest in Europe at 27.5 metres long, 6 metres wide, and 3.5 metres high, built with 32 megaliths including stones weighing up to 180 tonnes. The Dolmen of Viera is a c. 4,500-year-old corridor tomb, while the Tholos of El Romeral (c. 1800 BC) features corbelled vault chambers. Together with La Pena de los Enamorados and El Torcal, they represent outstanding examples of European megalithism.'' -> ''The Antequera Dolmens Site is a cultural heritage ensemble comprising three cultural monuments (the Dolmen of Menga, Dolmen of Viera and Tholos of El Romeral) and 2 natural mountain features (the Peña de los Enamorados and El Torcal) in and near the city of Antequera in Andalusia, Spain [1]. The cultural institution responsible for its protection is the CADA [1]. It was declared a World Heritage Site in 2016 [1]. Both the Dolmen of Menga and the Tholos of El Romeral have anomalous orientations [1]. The archaeoastronomer Michael Hoskin, who studied the site, noted that, whereas the axes of almost all dolmens around the Mediterranean are oriented to a celestial feature, such as sunrise at dawn on the equinoxes (as occurs in the Dolmen of Viera), the Dolmen of Menga points to the striking nearby peak called the Peña de los Enamorados [1]. This rises abruptly from the plain and contains the contemporaneous rock shelter of Matacabras, in which cave paintings are found [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('efe2bc83-83cc-4393-b725-a18543dc9d5f'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '55', '58', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:efe2bc83-83cc-4393-b725-a18543dc9d5f:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: rarity_score ''58'' restored on Antequera Dolmens Site', '9c09040b071506324576b2c977f706f2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 55 from mystery 6, cultural_influence 10"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45425", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0112:chunk-0001: description ''A UNESCO World Heritage Site (inscribed 2016) in Andalusia, Spain, comprising three megalithic monuments and two natural landmarks. The Dolmen of Menga (c. 3750-3650 BCE) is the largest in Europe at 27.5 metres long, 6 metres wide, and 3.5 metres high, built with 32 megaliths including stones weighing up to 180 tonnes. The Dolmen of Viera is a c. 4,500-year-old corridor tomb, while the Tholos of El Romeral (c. 1800 BC) features corbelled vault chambers. Together with La Pena de los Enamorados and El Torcal, they represent outstanding examples of European megalithism.'' -> ''The Antequera Dolmens Site is a cultural heritage ensemble comprising three cultural monuments (the Dolmen of Menga, Dolmen of Viera and Tholos of El Romeral) and 2 natural mountain features (the Peña de los Enamorados and El Torcal) in and near the city of Antequera in Andalusia, Spain [1]. The cultural institution responsible for its protection is the CADA [1]. It was declared a World Heritage Site in 2016 [1]. Both the Dolmen of Menga and the Tholos of El Romeral have anomalous orientations [1]. The archaeoastronomer Michael Hoskin, who studied the site, noted that, whereas the axes of almost all dolmens around the Mediterranean are oriented to a celestial feature, such as sunrise at dawn on the equinoxes (as occurs in the Dolmen of Viera), the Dolmen of Menga points to the striking nearby peak called the Peña de los Enamorados [1]. This rises abruptly from the plain and contains the contemporaneous rock shelter of Matacabras, in which cave paintings are found [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('efe2bc83-83cc-4393-b725-a18543dc9d5f'::uuid, 'total_power', '35', '36', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:efe2bc83-83cc-4393-b725-a18543dc9d5f:total_power', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: total_power ''36'' restored on Antequera Dolmens Site', '9c09040b071506324576b2c977f706f2', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "total_power = 35 from antiquity + fortification + cultural_influence + mystery + legacy = 7 + 8 + 10 + 6 + 4"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45425", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0112:chunk-0001: description ''A UNESCO World Heritage Site (inscribed 2016) in Andalusia, Spain, comprising three megalithic monuments and two natural landmarks. The Dolmen of Menga (c. 3750-3650 BCE) is the largest in Europe at 27.5 metres long, 6 metres wide, and 3.5 metres high, built with 32 megaliths including stones weighing up to 180 tonnes. The Dolmen of Viera is a c. 4,500-year-old corridor tomb, while the Tholos of El Romeral (c. 1800 BC) features corbelled vault chambers. Together with La Pena de los Enamorados and El Torcal, they represent outstanding examples of European megalithism.'' -> ''The Antequera Dolmens Site is a cultural heritage ensemble comprising three cultural monuments (the Dolmen of Menga, Dolmen of Viera and Tholos of El Romeral) and 2 natural mountain features (the Peña de los Enamorados and El Torcal) in and near the city of Antequera in Andalusia, Spain [1]. The cultural institution responsible for its protection is the CADA [1]. It was declared a World Heritage Site in 2016 [1]. Both the Dolmen of Menga and the Tholos of El Romeral have anomalous orientations [1]. The archaeoastronomer Michael Hoskin, who studied the site, noted that, whereas the axes of almost all dolmens around the Mediterranean are oriented to a celestial feature, such as sunrise at dawn on the equinoxes (as occurs in the Dolmen of Viera), the Dolmen of Menga points to the striking nearby peak called the Peña de los Enamorados [1]. This rises abruptly from the plain and contains the contemporaneous rock shelter of Matacabras, in which cave paintings are found [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f03f19b4-c5aa-41b9-a874-fa37fff2ead7'::uuid, 'category_group', 'Settlements', 'Religious', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:f03f19b4-c5aa-41b9-a874-fa37fff2ead7:category_group', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: category_group ''Religious'' restored on Ancient Thera', 'e5db75537798082524f45f66b8b08fcb', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "category_group = Settlements from site_type ''City/town/settlement'' is in group ''Settlements''"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:27944", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0033:chunk-0005: site_type ''Temple complex'' -> ''City/town/settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45429", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0112:chunk-0001: description ''A Dorian settlement atop Mesa Vouno mountain (365 m) on Santorini, founded in the 9th-8th century BC by Spartan colonists led by King Theras, for whom the island was named. The site preserves some of the oldest known Greek alphabet inscriptions, adapted from the Phoenician script, found at hilltop sanctuaries with dedications to Zeus, Apollo, and other gods. Excavated from 1896 by Friedrich Hiller von Gaertringen, the ruins span Hellenistic, Roman, and Byzantine periods.'' -> \"Ancient Thera is an archaeological site from classical antiquity on the island of Santorini, which sits on the top of a limestone hill called Mesa Vouno [1]. Starting in 1895, Friedrich Hiller von Gaertringen systematically investigated the city until 1904 [1]. The ancient city consisted of a street almost 800 m long and especially wide for its time (between two and four meters) extending in a southeastern direction and containing several imposing buildings [1]. Residential areas are grouped around an agora, which is across from a theater built into the lower slope [1]. In archaic times the city was an unimportant settlement at the southern periphery of the Cyclades [1]. It was founded by Doric colonists from Sparta [1]. Inscriptions from the city''s beginnings found at the sanctuary on the spur of the mountain ridge are noteworthy [1]. They date from the transition from the 9th to the 8th century BC and are thus among the oldest known examples of the use of the Greek alphabet [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
