    ('f2046843-60ed-4081-8bbe-95cde34ec6a7'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:f2046843-60ed-4081-8bbe-95cde34ec6a7:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Chellah', '097b1c5aaec19466a23340de44795fe7', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -1000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:29035", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0229:chunk-0001: period_start ''-1000'' -> ''-700''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36086", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: period_start ''-700'' -> ''-1000''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45455", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0113:chunk-0001: description ''The Chellah (or Shalla) is a medieval fortified necropolis and ancient archaeological site in Rabat, Morocco, on the south bank of the Bou Regreg estuary[1]. Phoenicians established a trading emporium here in the first millennium BC[1]. The site later became Sala Colonia, a Roman colony in Mauretania Tingitana, abandoned in Late Antiquity[1]. In the late 13th century, the Marinid dynasty began using it as a necropolis, enclosing a portion with walls and building a religious complex by the mid-14th century[1]. The necropolis declined in the 15th century, suffering earthquake damage and looting[1]. 20th-century excavations uncovered the Roman town ruins[1]. Today it is a tourist site and UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2012[1].'' -> ''The Chellah or Shalla is a medieval fortified necropolis and ancient archeological site in Rabat, Morocco, located on the south side of the Bou Regreg estuary [1]. In the late 13th century the site began to be used as a dynastic necropolis for the Marinid dynasty [1]. By the mid-14th century Marinid sultans had enclosed a part of the site with a new set of walls and built a religious complex inside it to accompany their mausoleums [1]. In the 15th century the necropolis began to decline and it suffered damage over the centuries due to earthquakes and looting [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
