    ('f69c8107-a8fe-45ae-a8d8-d5ab8e618e33'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '5', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:f69c8107-a8fe-45ae-a8d8-d5ab8e618e33:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''5'' restored on Hypostyle Hall', 'fabc89ea773e1f2ecc21bfbed60da73e', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Megalithic structures'', ''1500 - 500 BC'') is shared by 21 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45463", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0113:chunk-0001: description \"The Great Hypostyle Hall within the Karnak temple complex at Luxor, Egypt, was begun by pharaoh Seti I (c. 1291-1279 BCE) and completed by his son Ramesses II (c. 1279-1213 BCE). Spanning approximately 5,000 square metres, the hall contains 134 sandstone columns in 16 rows, with the central 12 rising to 21 metres to support a clerestory roof. Interior walls depict ritual scenes in Seti I''s refined raised relief and Ramesses II''s bold sunk relief, while exterior walls record battles with the Hittites and the earliest known international peace treaty (c. 1258 BCE). The structure remained in active religious use for approximately 1,700 years.\" -> ''The Great Hypostyle Hall is located within the Karnak Temple Complex, in the Precinct of Amon-Re [1]. The structure was built around the 19th Egyptian Dynasty (c.\\u20091290–1224 BC) [1]. The Great Hypostyle Hall covers an area of 5,000 m2 [1]. In 1899, eleven of the massive columns of the Great Hypostyle Hall collapsed in a chain reaction, because their foundations were undermined by ground water [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
