    ('f6f190b6-7c0b-4cf5-8ceb-cf9ec567997d'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:f6f190b6-7c0b-4cf5-8ceb-cf9ec567997d:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Abusir', '2ead6dacca8383d8fe56273f885f8209', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -2487..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43207", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0068:chunk-0001: description \"Abusir is an ancient Egyptian archaeological pyramid complex located approximately 20 km south of Cairo on the western bank of the Nile at the edge of the desert [1]. It lies between Giza and Saqqara, adjoining Saqqara to the north [2][3]. The site contains ruins of four kings'' pyramids from the Old Kingdom period, built by three 5th-dynasty kings (Sahure, Neferirkare, and Neuserre) around 2465-2325 BCE [2][4]. Neferirkare''s pyramid is the tallest in the necropolis where most kings of Egypt''s 5th Dynasty were buried [5]. The site is part of the Pyramid Fields of Memphis and its Necropolis UNESCO World Heritage Site [6]. The pyramids were poorly constructed compared to similar Egyptian monuments and are now in a state of disrepair [7].\" -> ''The Abusir pyramid complex is located on the Western Desert plateau at the edge of the cultivated plain, with the Giza Pyramids to its north, and Saqqara to its south, and served as one of the main elite cemeteries for the ancient Egyptian capital city of Memphis [1]. The locality of Abusir took its turn as the focus of the prestigious western burial rites operating out of the then-capital of Memphis during the Old Kingdom Fifth Dynasty [1]. Abusir was the origin of the largest find of Old Kingdom papyri to date — the Abusir Papyri [1]. In the late nineteenth century, a number of Western museums acquired collections of fragmentary papyri from the administrative records of one Abusir funerary cult, that of king Neferirkare Kakai [1]. Also found at Abusir were substantial remains of a Ramesside temple, perhaps built by Ramses II [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
