complex that is believed to have been constructed around the beginning of the 3rd century [1]. The best preserved room is the frigidarium, with intact architectural elements such as Gallo-Roman vaults, ribs and consoles [1]. It is believed that the bath complex was built by the influential guild of boatmen of 3rd-century Roman Paris or Lutetia, as the consoles on which the barrel ribs rest are carved in the shape of ships'' prows [1]. Like all Roman Baths, these baths were freely open to the public, and were meant to be, at least partially, a means of romanizing the ancient Gauls [1]. As the baths lay across the Seine river on the Left Bank and were unprotected by defensive fortifications, they were easy prey to roving barbarian groups, who apparently destroyed the bath complex sometime at the end of the 3rd century [1].\""}]'::jsonb),
    ('f859776a-9e93-4c62-9585-1f4aed2cbaa0'::uuid, 'mystery', '6', '7', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:f859776a-9e93-4c62-9585-1f4aed2cbaa0:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''7'' restored on Roman Thermae, Varna', '56776c7255171b23a68cda8212c4eef0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 6 from (''Bath'', ''1 - 500 AD'') is shared by 15 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45471", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0113:chunk-0001: description ''The Roman Thermae in Varna are the largest and best-preserved Roman bathing complex in the Balkans, representing the architectural sophistication of Odessus, the ancient Roman name for this Black Sea port city [1]. Constructed in the late 2nd century AD, these thermae rank as the fourth-largest surviving Roman baths in all of Europe [1]. The complex exemplifies typical Roman bath architecture with its hot (caldarium), warm (tepidarium), and cold (frigidarium) bathing chambers, hypocaust heating systems, and elaborate marble decorations [1]. As one of the most significant Roman monuments in Bulgaria, the thermae provide valuable insights into urban life in the Roman province of Moesia Inferior [1].'' -> ''The Roman Thermae are a complex of Ancient Roman baths (thermae) in the Black Sea port city of Varna in northeastern Bulgaria [1]. The Roman Thermae are situated in the southeastern part of the modern city, which under the Roman Empire was known as Odessus [1]. The baths were constructed in the late 2nd century AD and rank as the fourth-largest preserved Roman thermae in Europe and the largest in the Balkans [1]. The Roman baths of Varna were built towards the end of the 2nd century and remained in use for about a hundred years, till the late 3rd century [1]. Coins of Roman emperor Septimius Severus (r. 193–211) have been discovered among the bath ruins [1]. The Roman Thermae of Varna feature the whole range of facilities including an apodyterium, a frigidarium, a tepidarium and a caldarium as well as a palaestra [1]. Heating was provided by means of a hypocaust, an underfloor heating system of pipes [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
    ('f859776a-9e93-4c62-9585-1f4aed2cbaa0'::uuid, 'rarity_score', '32', '35', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:f859776a-9e93-4c62-9585-1f4aed2cbaa0:rarity_score', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: rarity_score ''35'' restored on Roman Thermae, Varna', '56776c7255171b23a68cda8212c4eef0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "rarity_score = 32 from mystery 6, cultural_influence 7"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45471", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0113:chunk-0001: description ''The Roman Thermae in Varna are the largest and best-preserved Roman bathing complex in the Balkans, representing the architectural sophistication of Odessus, the ancient Roman name for this Black Sea port city [1]. Constructed in the late 2nd century AD, these thermae rank as the fourth-largest surviving Roman baths in all of Europe [1]. The complex exemplifies typical Roman bath architecture with its hot (caldarium), warm (tepidarium), and cold (frigidarium) bathing chambers, hypocaust heating systems, and elaborate marble decorations [1]. As one of the most significant Roman monuments in Bulgaria, the thermae provide valuable insights into urban life in the Roman province of Moesia Inferior [1].'' -> ''The Roman Thermae are a complex of Ancient Roman baths (thermae) in the Black Sea port city of Varna in northeastern Bulgaria [1]. The Roman Thermae are situated in the southeastern part of the modern city, which under the Roman Empire was known as Odessus [1]. The baths were constructed in the late 2nd century AD and rank as the fourth-largest preserved Roman thermae in Europe and the largest in the Balkans [1]. The Roman baths of Varna were built towards the end of the 2nd century and remained in use for about a hundred years, till the late 3rd century [1]. Coins of Roman emperor Septimius Severus (r. 193–211) have been discovered among the bath ruins [1]. The Roman Thermae of Varna feature the whole range of facilities including an apodyterium, a frigidarium, a tepidarium and a caldarium as well as a palaestra [1]. Heating was provided by means of a hypocaust, an underfloor heating system of pipes [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
