    ('fc215bfb-e68e-4aa0-844a-c15d8047f62f'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:fc215bfb-e68e-4aa0-844a-c15d8047f62f:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Stones of Stenness', 'b1079ffe6a0ef1b098c67a78d47d42c0', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period -4000..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43263", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0070:chunk-0001: description \"The Standing Stones of Stenness are a Neolithic monument on mainland Orkney, Scotland, dating to approximately 3100-2900 BC and possibly the earliest henge in the British Isles. Originally 12 stones arranged in an ellipse about 30 metres in diameter, four survive today, reaching heights comparable to the nearby Watch Stone''s 6.5 metres. Excavations in 1973-74 revealed a central hearth with charcoal, burnt bone, and Grooved Ware pottery, surrounded by a rock-cut ditch 2 metres deep and 44 metres across. The site is part of the Heart of Neolithic Orkney UNESCO World Heritage Site.\" -> ''The Standing Stones of Stenness are a Neolithic monument five miles northeast of Stromness on the mainland of Orkney [1]. The surviving stones are sited on a promontory at the south bank of the stream that joins the southern ends of the sea loch Loch of Stenness and the freshwater Loch of Harray [1]. The stream is now bridged, but at one time was crossed by a stepping stone causeway, and the Ring of Brodgar lies about 1.2 km away to the north-west [1]. Maeshowe chambered cairn is about 1.2 km to the east of the Standing Stones of Stenness and several other Neolithic monuments also lie in the vicinity, suggesting that this area had particular importance [1]. Although the site today lacks the encircling ditch and bank, excavation has shown this site was a henge monument, possibly the oldest in the British Isles [1]. The stones are thin slabs, approximately 30 cm thick with sharply angled tops [1]. One stone, known as the \"Odin Stone\" which stood in the field to the north of the henge, was pierced with a circular hole [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
