    ('fe663edf-4b9e-480b-a976-c8ecf135249d'::uuid, 'legacy', '1', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:fe663edf-4b9e-480b-a976-c8ecf135249d:legacy', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: legacy ''4'' restored on Aihole', '9a5ee19b9724e0e800d67e557da6ccf6', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "legacy = 1 from period 1..None, 0 like(s), 0 bookmark(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:45499", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0114:chunk-0001: description ''Aihole in Karnataka, India, was the capital of the early Chalukya dynasty and is regarded as the cradle of Indian temple architecture. The site contains over 125 Hindu, Jain, and Buddhist temples built between the 6th and 12th centuries. Chalukya artisans experimented with 16 types of free-standing temples and 4 rock-cut shrine styles here, producing prototypes that influenced temple design across peninsular India. Aihole is on the UNESCO World Heritage tentative list alongside nearby Badami.'' -> ''Aihole, also referred to as Aivalli, Ahivolal or Aryapura, is a historic site of ancient and medieval-era Buddhist, Hindu and Jain monuments in Karnataka [1]. Located around an eponymous small village surrounded by farmlands and sandstone hills, Aihole is a major archaeological site, featuring over 120 stone and cave temples spread along the Malaprabha river valley, in Bagalakote district [1]. Aihole, along with nearby Badami, emerged by the 6th century as the cradle of experimentation with temple architecture, stone artwork, and construction techniques [1]. Over 100 Aihole temples are Hindu, a few are Jain and one is Buddhist [1]. The documented history of Aihole is traceable to the rise of the Early Chalukya dynasty in 6th century [1]. Aihole was fortified by Late Chalukya kings in the 11th and 12th centuries, in an approximate circle [1]. The Durga temple is the best known and studied of the Aihole temples [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
