    ('ff53d37c-ab2b-4874-8ed8-a3336ccd12fc'::uuid, 'mystery', '5', '4', 'card-stats-2026-09-23-rollback:ff53d37c-ab2b-4874-8ed8-a3336ccd12fc:mystery', 'rollback of card-stats-2026-09-23: mystery ''4'' restored on Maleme', 'eff8266e171c5d509708714b67958aac', '[{"source": "api/cardgame/generator.py:site_card_stats", "url": "api/cardgame/generator.py", "quote": "mystery = 5 from (''Necropolis/tombs complex'', ''3000 - 1500 BC'') is shared by 44 curated site(s)"}, {"source": "production export d65d0c3df8f22029 (2026-09-25 12:21:51.637605+00)", "url": "remediation_change_log + unified_sites + card_stats (read-only)", "quote": "counterfactual: 2888 journalled input value(s) put back reproduce the stored card_stats in all 60048 cells"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:28845", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase3:batch-0202:chunk-0001: site_type ''Necropolis/tombs complex'' -> ''Settlement''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:36114", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "2026-09-25_mechanical-journal-reversal-3: site_type ''Settlement'' -> ''Necropolis/tombs complex''"}, {"source": "remediation_change_log:43283", "url": "remediation_change_log", "quote": "phase4:p4-0070:chunk-0001: description ''A Late Minoan tholos tomb from the Late Bronze Age (c. 1450-1200 BC) is located on Kafkala hill near the village of Maleme in western Crete. The corbelled vault structure measures 8.15 m in diameter and 2.95 m in height, accessed via a 13 m entrance passage (dromos) with a doorway 3 m high and 1.60 m wide, capped by a massive poros-stone lintel. Accidentally discovered in the early 20th century and looted, the tomb was further damaged by bombing during World War II. Systematic excavation by archaeologist Kostas Davaras in 1966 recovered bronze tools, pottery, and sealstones of bronze and agate.'' -> ''A Late Minoan tholos tomb has been discovered in the vicinity of Maleme [1]. The tholos tomb, located on \"Kafkala\" hill, was accidentally revealed and then looted at the beginning of the 20th century [1]. During World War II a bomb caused the partial destruction of its roof and the backfill of the chamber [1]. It is a significant funerary monument excavated in 1966 by the curator Mr. C. Davaras and partly restored in 1970 [1]. A corridor (\"dromos\"), 25.10m long and 1.60m wide, leads to the chamber [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
