he 20th century [1]. During World War II a bomb caused the partial destruction of its roof and the backfill of the chamber [1]. It is a significant funerary monument excavated in 1966 by the curator Mr. C. Davaras and partly restored in 1970 [1]. A corridor (\"dromos\"), 25.10m long and 1.60m wide, leads to the chamber [1].''"}]'::jsonb),
